      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-09-23_WidnesExaminer_art0009" n="art0009">
        <head>JOTTINGS BY AN OLD JOURNALIST.</head>
        <p>JOTTINGS BY AN OLD JOURNALIST.</p>
        <p>A Costly Trick. What a lot of people yield to the temptation to attempt to defraud a railway company! Really, to take the lowest ground, the game is not worth the candle. A while ago a chance acquaintance gleefully explained to me a scheme which he had devised for saving a few coppers in this way, "without the slightesf risk of detection," and on the very next Saturday he was caught in the act, and, besides suffering the humiliation of exposure, had £3 to pay in fine and costs.</p>
        <p>Another Dodge. Once I saw a very well-known resident in an East Lancashire town enter a first-class com- partment with a person of the Opposite sex at Bradford. -I was travelling by the same train, and noticed that after riding some 20 miles they took advantage of a stop to alight and saunter about the platform. They completed their journey in a third-class carriage, and I after- wards ascertained from ,a collector that they simply held third-class returns from that point. A few weeks later, this gentleman over- took me in the street, and, clapping me on the shoulder, gave me a boisterous greeting. He proceeded to indulge in a little chaff, but when I asked him if he had been to Bradford lately, he gravely declared that it was a place he very seldom visited. I then told him that not long before I had seen a prominent citizen enter a first-class carriage at Bradford with a lady whom I did not know, and that the pair changed to the inferior class at a station far down the line for which they had merely return tickets for their destination. Remarking that it was a cloudy evening, the fellow hurriedly left me, and he always avoided me after that.</p>
        <p>" Excluded and Isolated." I have read with interest the recent state- ments respecting "the position of Cairo-street Chapel and its minister." It is an Old contro- versy. In one town in which I lived some years, the committee. of the Sunday School Union decided by a fair majority to admit the Unitarians, whereupon the Wesleyan repre- sentatives withdrew, subsequently forming- a circuit union of their own. On the constitution of a Free Church Council in that district, the Unitarians made a grievance of the fact that they were not invited to join, whereupon the promoters of the movement pointed out that they were affiliated with the National " Evangelical " Free Church Council, and had therefore no power to include Unitarians. The Unitarian Position.</p>
        <p>Our Unitarian friends always expect and pre- pare for this sort of thing. I ?ecall a confer- ence of the Yorkshire Union of Unitarian and Free Christian Churches at Huddersfield some years ago. A minister from Southport spoke on " The good and evil of denominations." His main argument was that the so-called schisms were needed, and had strengthened the spiritual life of the nation. Toward the conclusion he said he could see very few signs that the Free Churches would welcome the Unitarian and Free Christian Churches into their fold. The Unitarian Churches, he added, were the Protestants amongst the Protestants, and in all probability would still have to carve out their own path.</p>
        <p>More About Wasps. Last week's item headed "Drunkenness among wasps" constrains me to set down a few simple observations of my own which have led me to conclude that the wasp is a lazy, cowardly bully. Wasps would be quite service- able if it were possible to keep them to their own proper vocation, but as soon as there are signs of an easy, comfortable living without exertion, they become hopelessly demoralised. Does the housewife set on fruit to boil, they scent their "favourite tipple from afar, and become a nuisance in a neighbourhood where perhaps none of their kind have previously been seen during the season—unless, indeed, it were in the confectionery and sweatshop windows. This I have repeatedly demonstrated: however valiant, to all appearance, and vicious in the use of its sting, 'the wasp is, feally a "pusillanimous critter." If you can give one ever such a slight tap with any light article you may, happen to haveliandy, it - will, im- mediately grovel at your feet. I wonder if wasps have a weakness for scent among other luxuries—say, with a preference for lavender. This latter was used by a lady member of a party I fell in with on Saturday afternoon. The wasps were rather numerous, yet they troubled none but the lavender-scented one—not even when we attacked them with our walking sticks. The. lavender had a strange fascination for them, and they fell an easy prey to the lady's defenders. The Lady Motorist.</p>
        <p>As to that bit of " Gossip " respecting the lady taxi-cab driver, a doctor with a consider- able practice told me the other day that, th war having deprived him of his male chauffeur, he had had one of his housemaids trained to drive, and she had turned out "a gem." She cleaned and prepared the car herself and handled it to perfection, and in the early morn- ing and at thE, close of the day would expend her superfluous energy in the garden with the aid of the lawnmower or in householl duties demanding vigorous exercise. In a word, she was "keen as mustard" on work. At this rate, mere man will have to look to his laurels. Ah, but is he not adding to them by strenuous exertions in a more dangerous sphere? Blinded Heroes.</p>
        <p>Again what of the blinded sailors and soldiers in whose interests last night's concert was held in the Parr Hall? , Helen Keller's argument that mach of the prevalent blindness is un- necessary and avoidable hardly applies here. Among other things, these dear fellows will have to learn to read all over again. Would that all the blind had the advantages of a common print, for, strange as it may seem, it is not so at present. Some time ago I read in an article in "World's Work" that there is one insti- tution in the United States using embossed characters (line-letter), in spite of the fact that most of the blind prefer a point system; another, American braille (raised dots); another, New York Point—a kind of braille, in which a new periodical, the " Matilda Ziegler Magazine l'or the Blind," "the boon ifor which we have waited for many years," Pis printed. To print this periodical at all is expensive; it is much more expensive now that it has to be done in so many different types. "A plague 'upon all these prints!" exclaimed the writer. "Let us have one system, whether it is an ideal one or not. For my part, I wish nothing had been in- vented except European braille." " Cruel Rumour,"</p>
        <p>The bit of good news under this heading re- minded me of an incident occurring on the eve of the summer holidays. A hardworking man and his wife were at the point of departure for a few days' rest and change down by the sea, when a fussy neighbour rushed up on the plat- form and knked if they had not heard that their soldier-son was reported, missing. They had good reason to doubt the evil tidings; still, while deciding not to allow the rumour to inter- fere with their programme—a resolute step which was fully justified, as the sequel proved —they were naturally somewhat perturbed until fully assured that it was baseless. Most of us fail to understand, and certainly do not, envy, the state .of mind of those who are capable of deriving amusement from this kind of "joke."</p>
        <p>Spelling. Last week's little story of the smart child who complained that her spelling book was "all wrong," reminded me once more of the notice " Ile soled hear," to be seen not so long ago in the window of a small Warrington -shop—sug- gesting that the old spelling bee, which was a popular form of entertainment SO to 40 years ago, might not unprofitably be 'rived. -</p>
        <p>The Only Way. Teresa: "The man I marry must be bold and fearless." Viola: "Yes, dear; he must."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-09-23_WidnesExaminer_art0010" n="art0010">
        <head>lib</head>
        <p>lib</p>
        <p>CONTENDING ATOM.</p>
        <p>Alderman Bennett On The Mobilisation Of Intellect.</p>
        <p>ADDRESS TO BRITISH ASSOCIATION.</p>
        <p>Alderman Arthur Bennett read a paper on "The Federation of Cognate Societies" at the recent Conference of the Corresponding locieties of the British Association at New- castle-on-Tyne.</p>
        <p>Federation, he explained, he had defined as "union for common purposes, liberty in mat- ters of separate concern," which, in the enthusiasm of youth, he had trielcl to prove was an 'idea that, properly interpreted, would not only solve the Irish question and pave the way to a really united Empire, but would lead by stages to Tennyson's sublime ideal, "the Parliament of man, the federation of the world." We had seen the principle applied with great success in Australia and ,South Africa, and found men of every party feeling towards the simple truth that federation is the only way to solve the riddle of these islands consistently with the satisfaction of the claims of various parts of them to what is popularly called Home Rule. The Nonconformist bodies of the country have been drawn closer together through the medium of a Free Church Council and are rapidly advancing towards still closer union on the same elastic lines. Capital and Labour are largely organising on these lines, and the idea had spread, almost unconsciously, to almost ever, field of human activity. A Warrington Federation.</p>
        <p>In 1905 he advocated liefore the Warrington Literary and Philosophical Society and pleaded for a federation of the local societies interested in intellectual pursuits, with head- quarters at the Old Academy. He admitted the difficulties of merging these societies, but they could federate by having a general Coun- cil to regulate procedure, avoid overlapping and clashing of dates, secure an interchange of lectures and arrange occasional joint meet- ings. In May, 1914, his scheme was disinterred and at a meeting of the principal societies in the town the proposal was approved and a committee appointed to prepare a definite Scheme. That scheme was submitted in June, 1914, to the delegates of thirteen societies, representing practically every haven of local intellectual activity, and it was decided that the Federation be formed upon lines which were ordered to be submitted to the societies, with a request to them to send delegates to a meeting of the Federation. And "after that the Deluge." This frightful war broke upon us like a storm of Hell before the project had matured, and, like many other, hopeful and progressive movements, it is still in abeyance. '</p>
        <p>Why Not Nationally?</p>
        <p>If thie. idea could be applied locally, why not nationally? His object was the gradual mobilisation of all the intellectual forces of the country, and surely the need was never more acute than nosy. We are, or have been, one of the worst organised countries in the world; those who clamoured for reform had been as voices crying in the wilderness; the lesson had to be written in lightning before our slumbrous realm would learn. Instead of developing into an ordered commonwealth we had degenerated into a fortuitous concourse of contending atoms. Our .politics had become a mere scramble for the loaves and fishes.; our 'Churches wrangled over things irrelevant and overlapped and competed at every turn; our industries were animated mainly by the greed of gain and those of them that needed extra brains, newer methods and better scientific skill were quietly appropriated by more enter- prising lands. Instead of combining to make the most of their common country, the classes and the masses stood aloof and glared at each other and ever and anon growled like, two packs of hungry wolves. Although we; were spending more on education than ever before nobody- was really educated. Our Empire, with all its greatness, latent loyalty, untold possibilities in men and material, was such a ramshackle arrangement that one cannot won- der at the Germans for thinking that when the grim hour struck it would' incontinently fall to pieces. As for the world, the beet it could do after an infancy of unimaginable years was to use its ever-growing powers for purposes of sheer destruction, "plot mutual slaughter" and "reel back into the beast"</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-09-23_WidnesExaminer_art0011" n="art0011">
        <head>Vision of Better Things.</head>
        <p>A Vision of Better Things.</p>
        <p>But amid the chaos there had been the vision of "men with growing wings." This welter of blood and sorrow has revealed such heights of human nobleness as we had never dreamed of; such possibilities of properly co- ordinated effort, turned to less ignoble uses, as the bravest hitherto had scarcely ventured to conceive.</p>
        <p>The different societies in Warrington at pre- sent meet in different places—ranging from "pubs." to clubs and from Masonic Halls to Church Parlours. A federation might secure or even build in every town appropriate pre- mises, fitted with the requisite appliances and supplied with smaller rooms, which was quite beyond the power of separate societies. District federations would be a natural corollary to town federations, and these in turn would be linked up with the central Qrganisation, national, imperial or cosmopolitan, as the case might be. If England organised and really rose to the height of her opportunities there was not any nation in the world apable of greater things. On the motion of the Alderman the following resolution was unanimously carried : "That the committee of the conference of delegates be requested, to recommend the various constituent societies to consider the desirability of forming local and national federations of societies with kindred .aims."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-09-23_WidnesExaminer_art0012" n="art0012">
        <head>FOR CONSCIENCE' SAKE.</head>
        <p>FOR CONSCIENCE' SAKE.</p>
        <p>"Is there anything further about the two. Penketh conscientious objectors and the pay- ment of poor relief?" asked the Rev. A. M. Mitchell at the Board of Guardians on Wed- nesday. The Clerk (Mr. A. Bottomley) : I have car- ried out the Board's instructions and had inquiries made. The Rey. A. M. Mitchell: Has it been found necessary to grant/ relief to any of them? The Clerk: No. Both women have definitely stated that they will not have poor relief or relief from the Government unless their hus- bands are working and earning it. The Rev. A. M. Mitchell : That is very nice and noble of them. It is quite evident that the Tribunals have not treated conscientious objectors as they should have been, and am quite sure the Guardians would do all they I could for their wives and families.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-09-23_WidnesExaminer_art0013" n="art0013">
        <head>'NOBLE AND STRAIGHTFORWARD.'</head>
        <p>'NOBLE AND STRAIGHTFORWARD.'</p>
        <p>Butcher Congratulated By Board Of Guardians.</p>
        <p>some time ago Mr. H. Singleton, butcher;was granted by the Board of Guardians an increase of 3d. per lb. over contract price owing to the. enhanced cost of meat. On Wednesday', atl a meeting of the Guardians, Mr. A. Bottomley (the clerk) reported that Mr. Singleton intended lowering the price by a penny per lb. as meat had become slightlk cheaper. "Good !" exclaimed several of the members. Mr. W. C. Brooks: It is very nice for mem- bers to say "Good J"—and it is good—but, a. ther; is not a contractor in the whole universe who has done a similar thing, I think we ought to send a letter of our appreciation of his noble and straightforward conduct,-</p>
        <p>Mr. W.J. Shaw: I quite agree. The Rev. A(M. Mitchell: Do you think that is a wise thing to do? Mr. Shaw: It is wise to accept the penny.</p>
        <p>The Chairman: Mr. Mitchell only objects on a point of principle. But it is rather unique that a contractor should do this. The reverend gentleman agreed that the contractor might be told that the matter had been reported to the Board. Mr. T. Dale: I think we might appreciate what he has done, but not applaud it. It was decided that a letter of appieciation and thanks should be sent.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-09-23_WidnesExaminer_art0014" n="art0014">
        <head>EXAMINER, SATURDAY, SEPTEMBER 23, 1916.</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER, SATURDAY, SEPTEMBER 23, 1916.</p>
        <p>CLEVER STUDENTS</p>
        <p>Heartily Congratulated By The</p>
        <p>Education Committee.</p>
        <p>The Education Committee, on Monday, de- voted a considerable time to congratulatirig Mr. Leslie John Witts and Miss Kathleen Brown on winning the Warrington Scholarship, which entitles them to a three years' course at Man- chester University. Two other competitors, Miss Ethel White Johnson and Miss Bessie Lee "(candidates for the teaching profession) were awarded £25 each for four years, conditionally upon their taking a Degree course. The enconiums of •the committee regarding these two ladies were phrased, with equal heartiness and sincerity. Mr. John Upson made an explanation re- garding the proposed grant to Misses Johnson and Lee. He said the committee did not always get a supply of students who were competent to pass the examination, and to qualify -for the scholarship. Last year they did not award the scholarship at all, because there were no suit- able candidates. This year quite a number sat, and three girls passed the examination,.receiv- ing high approval from the authorities. While the committee had only one scholarship at their disposal, they did not think the other two girls should lose the opportunity of a university training. In view of the fact that they did not award the scholarship last year, they thought they would be acting well within their authority by making the proposed grants. Had the first girl, who did remarkably well, not been competing, either of the _other two would have gained the scholarship, and the School Management Committee thought they were well justified in suggesting the course they had done. Alderman Bennett particularly welcomed the suggestion of the sub-committee. No work which the Education Committee could do was of more value than that of affording' an oppor- tunity to really qualified boys and girls to take advantage of those higher educational facilities which in the ordinary course might not be available. He complimented the four students and, knowing Witts, he declared his belief that he would go far in the educational world, and it was a pleasure to be able to give him a help- ing hand. (Hear, hear.) Councillor Joseph asked whether the two girls who had come so near success were disqualified from sitting a second time for the scholarship? Mr. Upton replied that they were. He said that with some reluctance, because it -was opposed to his personal view of the matter, but he must be loyal td the committee. The majority thought that as the girls had already had one try and had also accepted a student teachership which carried a monetary payment of £2O, they would be in too favourable a position to compete against girls whop were a year younger. _ _ _</p>
        <p>- Mr: A. J. Jolley: I think Ethel Johnson can sit again, but the other girl cannot. Mr. Upson said he was under the impression that, as both. were candidates for the teaching profession, they would be debarred. Mr. Jolley: One is a student teacher and the other is not. If they were both student teachers they would both be disqualified. The Chairman (Councillor H. Roberts) first offered the congratulations of the com- mittee to Mr. William E. Mason, the first winner of a Warrington scholarship, on his con- tinued progress, and added that if ever a 'scholarship had been justified by results it had been in that case. The places of Leslie J. Witts and Kath- leen Brown, the two new scholarship holders, were simply excellent in the examination, as also were those of Ethel W. Johnson and Bessie' Lee. If the committee had only worded their scheme a little differently so that they' could have picked up in any year the scholarships which they did not award when they had not candidates of sufficient excellence, all these four might possibly have held scholarships. He also congratulated Mr. Thomas Malkin, a student at the Technical Institute, who had secured top. place in the examination in chemistry of the Lancashire and Cheshire ,Union of Institutes. (Hear, hear.)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-09-23_WidnesExaminer_art0015" n="art0015">
        <head>WANTED, A BENEFACTOR. How Secondary Education Could Make Progress.</head>
        <p>WANTED, A BENEFACTOR. How Secondary Education Could Make Progress.</p>
        <p>The Secondary School was awarded favour- able mention by the Education Committee on Monday evening, and, in order that, its benefits to the community might be-extended, Mr. John Upson, realising the impossibility at the pre- sent time of utilising ratepayers' money, sug- gested that some local lady or gentleman might yield up £50,000 for the purpose. It was during the discussion on the annual report of the headmaster ,Of the school (Mr. Broome) that the idea was mooted, *Mr. C. H. Unsworth having asked what it contained, and Mr. Upson replied that it was of a very favour- able character. It was the first report Mr. Broome had presented since his appointment, and the Governors were extremely giatified at the uniform stream of successes, both by past and present students—some of them of a very high order. The committee felt they had done the right thing in appointing Mr. Broome to that very important position. His enthusiasm and his professional skill had so acted upon the students who had come under his charge that they had participated in his enthusiasm, .and had worked strAnuously and with very brilliant results in many directions. The report of 'the head master of the School of Art (which was also submitted to the committee) was not of so satisfactory a nature owing to the falling off in the number of students. That school was under " the very serious consideration of the School ,Management Committee. They were a little perplexed and puzzled as to how things would turn out, but in that case, as in so many others, they 'could" only "wait and see." The School Management Committee, he added, had instructed their Director (Mr. J. Moore Murray) to consult with the architect of the Secondary School as to the possibility of,carry- ing out—in conformity with the present' archi- tectural features—certain alterations in order to provide a head master's room, of which there was a great need, as, at present, what was described as the head master's room was used as a common office and store room. It was desirable that Mr. Broome should have a room in which to interview parents, Inspectors and Governors. The only doubt in the minds of the committee was as to whether the suggested new room at the top of the staircase could- be erected without spoiling the appearance of the building, and on that account they decided to approach the architect. The whole of our secondary education was cramped and hindered by lack of accommodation. It was useless talking, but he wanted the public to know that at the very earliest moment they must, if the Education Committee were to fulfil their duty adequately, find additional accommodation for secondary students. Again the terrible want pf pence tied them up. Wbuld that some' local benefactor would spend £50,000 in the erection of another secondary school or in dupli- cating the present school. He believed the original plans provided for the duplication of the school when the opportunity came—and it had arrived much more quickly than the erectors of the school anticipated. They could , do- nothing while the war continued. But in I the meantime the young people who were I going to be the winners of the war after the I war—the conimercial,and industrial war—were intellectually and professionally., perishing for lack of opportunity to train their mental and moral faculties and powers.</p>
        <p>Alderman Bennett expressed his pleasure that the proposed alterations were to conform with the present architectural features. One had had many bitter experiences of attempted en- largements when the previous archi*tural features had been utterly, ignored for the sake of what might seem to be practical utility.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-09-23_WidnesExaminer_art0016" n="art0016">
        <head>BUTTER OR MARGARINE?</head>
        <p>BUTTER OR MARGARINE?</p>
        <p>Mr. W. J. Shaw's "Uneasiness" About Workhouse Inmates.</p>
        <p>Mr. W. J. Shaw asked at the Board of Guar- dians' meeting on Wednesday what difference there would be if butter were substituted for margarine, as he was told that - margarine should not be used_in that institution.</p>
        <p>The Chairman (Mr. A. 3. jOile;)TTl.ie Board has settled that point.</p>
        <p>Mr. Shaw: Yes, up to now; but I wish to draw the attention of the Board to the fact that there if an uneasiness outside that butter should be used.</p>
        <p>II So saying, he resumed his seat and the "next libusiness" was begun.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-09-23_WidnesExaminer_art0017" n="art0017">
        <head>NOT IDEAL.</head>
        <p>NOT IDEAL.</p>
        <p>Faults In Our Educational System.</p>
        <p>EFFORTS FOR IMPROVE- MENT.</p>
        <p>An important discussion upon the future of eduCation in the borough took place at the monthly meeting of the Education Committee on Monday evening, oOuncillor Henry Roberts presiding. Apart from an allegation, and, perhaps, admission, that our present methods of education are inefficient or inadequate, the regrettableinews that commercial subjects are not to be taught at the Secondary School yet awhile was made known. The discussion , was initiated by Mr. John Upson, who, in moving the minutes of the 'School Management Committee, said that that committee considered the development of the 'commercial side of the Secondary School prior to the war, and a very practical scheme Was evolved by the Director, and approved by the sub-committee. If the war had not inter- vened, the sclieme would no doubt have been in operatio4 now. In the circumstances, ,how- ever, they could not proceed. But without carrying out the full scheme, it was thought that something could be done in the traijning of some of their young people in the Secondary School, who desired to enter upon a commer- cial career. They advertised for a teacher of the necessary subjects, but, unfortunately, they had not been able to secure a suitable man. They had only two applications and they thought it would be better to defer the appointment until there were a greater num- ber of men qualified to take up the work and do it effectively. The question of the commer- cial side of the work of the Secondary School was ever present in the minds of the members of the School Management Committee. They were very anxious to meet the( needs of the community by turning out students who were adequately trained in commercial subjects, but who, in addition, had a good general educa- tion.- That was an ideal which he hoped they would realise in the near future.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-09-23_WidnesExaminer_art0018" n="art0018">
        <head>Low Level of Intelligence—</head>
        <p>Low Level of Intelligence—</p>
        <p>Expressing the opinion that it was disap- pointing, especially at the present juncture, that they should have to defer a branch of education which was so enormously important. and of which they had somewhat lost sight in the past, Alderman Bennett admitted with regret that they could not at present overcome the practical difficulties; .but he hoped the matter would be kept continually before the School Management Committee,- and that they would not be satisfied until they were able to give that commercial education whtch was so vitally necessary in view of the expected developments of our country. He was, how- ever, glad to express his satisfaction that the Rev. Canon Morley Steienson, Mrs. C. Jackson Holmes, the Rev. T. A. Hind, Mr. A. J. Jolley and Mr. John Upson had been appointed a sub-committee for the purpose of considering and eventually reporting to the School Man- agement Committee upon communications from the Association of Education Committees and the''Newport Education Committee with reference to present conditions and important aspects of national education. He said the feeling was very much in the air that, in spite of all the money we had spent on education, we had not yet arrived at the ideal system. From daily experience they found that people who had been through the elementary schools, and in some cases through the secondary schools were absolutely ignor- ant of some of the. things of life which really mattered. They had a smattering of certain definite subjects in- cluded in the curriculum, but once they got outside those they seemed to be wholly lost. The general level of intelligence of children who had passed through the schools of War- rington and elsewhere was remarkably low. He did not want to blame the schools. The fault was perhaps to a large extent rh the pupils themselves, but he could give them staggering illustrations of what he meant. Perhaps the curriculum itself was somewhat to blame. He knew cases of lads and girls, who had had what was regarded as an inferior education, but who, in the general things of life which really mattered, seemed to be more intelligent than those who had gone through a full course of instruction. He did not think the fault was in the teachers, who were very loyal and enthusiastic, -but he hoped that by deliberations on the lines propOsed the com- mittee would arrive at some method by which they could bring out the best in every lad and girl in the country. Endorsing the remarks of Alderman Ben- nett as to the national importance of educa- tion, Councillor Joseph said he hoped :he proposal mentioned would lead to similarly able men in other parts of ,the country taking up the subject and comparing notes so as to arrive -at the best possible solution of such a vital question. —But Sound at Heart.</p>
        <p>The Chairman, was rather optimistic, for while he joined with Alderman Bennett in appreciating that there were serious defects, he could not help pointing out that in this time of national and imperial crisis, when the vast mass of the people of the country, whe- ther on the field of battle or in the munition works, were upholding the highest and 'best traditions of our race, there could not be any- thing very seriously add radically wrong with a system of elementary education, poor as it was, which had given us a generation of men and women of the character and quality of whom .we were 'so proud to-day. (Hear, hear.) If, with the imperfect methods which the war had disclosed to be perhaps more in existence than anticipated, men and women had clone so much, what might be• expected when, with a better and higher type of education, they were called upon to bear the responsibilities of Empire? He, was full of confidence and did not think the Sun of the Empire was at all beginning to set, but that in years to come it would shine with greater effulgence than ever. (Applause.) • In his reply Mr. Upson said it was admitted that the ideal had not been reached, but one could parallel the - cases of gross ignorance alluded to by Alderman Bennett with examples on the, other side. , Not long ago he read a letter written by a lad at the _Front just prior to his death . in. action. The lad came out of one of the poorest houses in the bor- ough and left scliool when only, 13 years old In the whole of the, eight pagea he wrote there was no mistake in grammar, spelling, or punctuation. The letter would not have dis- graced the pages of ".The Spectator." Mr Upson went on to argue that those who were carrying on the practical work of education were crippled_and hampered in every conceiv-i able way by the .eternal lack of pence. All the teachers in the service of the committee, he said, had one Object in view—to turn out better citizens' than those who had already left the schools. At the same time, the war had very largely- proved the value of the education which boys and' girls received in elementary schools. (Applause.)</p>
        <p>The Difficulty.-- Madge : "The skirt should come to the shoe- tops." Marjorie: "I quite agree with you, my (dear. But I can't get a pair high 'enough !"</p>
        <p>Doesn't Apply. Sonny: "_Rave you ever noticed, dad, how often mother uses the expression:' and so on- ad so so on?'" •</p>
        <p>Daddy: "Yes, son.; but I am sorry to •say that it does not apply to my trousers' buttons."</p>
        <p>Not Conscientious ObjectorA_ Two boys went to Blanchlaud district for a holiday recently. Needing grub .for fishing, or else for mere mischief, they commenced to rob a wasp's nest', Soon they were kept very busy with the enemy, who charged them right and left. The lads withdrew for a short time for consultation. " Aa'll tell ye," Jack said to his mate, "Task aboot fightin' for yor hame, thor's not a conshehenshus Objector ania,ng them !"</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-09-23_WidnesExaminer_art0019" n="art0019">
        <head>Named After --:—.</head>
        <p>Named After --:—.</p>
        <p>Everything had gone off splendidly 'at the christening, and the small infant had been given the name of " Reginald Aother.'.' thetarty had retired to the vestry, and the, clergyman was making out the certificate. When writing down the second name he said: - _ .</p>
        <p>"Strange," he thought, "to find this name given to the son of a navvy." Aloud he said to the father, "Your favourite poet, I sup Pose?" "Poet nsaid the man. "Poet, sir? Lor, no, sir; 1 keeps pidgins."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-09-23_WidnesExaminer_art0020" n="art0020">
        <head>IN THE COURTS.</head>
        <p>IN THE COURTS.</p>
        <p>NO LICENCE.</p>
        <p>Fined For Illegally Trading As</p>
        <p>Pawnbrokers.</p>
        <p>While the maximum penalty for acting as a pawnbroker without a licence is £5O, George Wilcock and his wife, Mary A. Wilcock, con- victed of this offenge at Tuesday's Borough Police Cdurt, were fined only £7 10s. each. The prosecution was brought in the name of George H. Reynolds, excise officer, and Mr. Edwin R. Booth, solicitor, London, appeared for the Customs authorities.</p>
        <p>Mr. Wilcock said he ha'd nothing at all to do with the business, and his wife made a special defence.</p>
        <p>Mr. Booth said the prosecution/was brought under the Act of 1872; and Section 6 under which the defendants were alleged to come, de- fined the business of a pawnbroker as one who keeps a shop for the taking in of goods or chattels as security for money given in advance thereon not exceeding £lO. Section 37 provided for the payment of a pawnbroker's licence of £7 10s., and the penalty for failing to do so was £5O. He said he would produce as evidence a little girl aged 16 years and a boy aged 11 years, who redeemed a pair of boots on the 11th July for is. 9d. which had been pledged the preceding day for, is. 6d. The police officer who went to defendant's premises would tell the Court that he found the shop full of bundles of goods of all shapes and sizes—two or ;three hundred bundles—and a variety of articles, fiat irons, workmen's cans and baskets, Sm. The female defendant told the officer that she bought goods from customers and sold them baok to them.</p>
        <p>A little girl gave evidence as to the transac- tion mentioned. She had been going there for about four years for the same purpose. The little boy said he had taken in bundles for Mrs. Wilcock to lend money on. Twopence interest was paid for every shilling.</p>
        <p>Methodical Classification.</p>
        <p>• Detective-Sergeant Reddington said he visited the premises on the 14th July, in company with Detective-Sergeant Summers. He had a con- versation with the female defendant in the shop and told her she vvas 'carrying on the business of pawnbroker. She said she was not aware of it, but that she kept goods for customers and sold them back to them. Witness told her that N%76.--s solely the business of a pawnbroker. With reference to cards having the name and amount attached to each article, ,she said she put them there so that she would know what she paid for thetn. She was very sorry that she charged in- terest on the goods, but she had to get a living. In the back part of the shop on shelves he found bundles of every description, and articles of clothing were hanging up. Each had a piece of paper pinned on with the name of the person who brought the goods there and the price given for them. There were flat irons, work- men's cans, everything imaginable on the fiber, with pieces of paper attached. It was purely the business of a pawnbroker. He had known her to be carrying on this business for the past eight ' years. , The shop was carried on as a secondhand business.</p>
        <p>Detective-Sergeant Summers, who corrobor- ated, said that there were pieces of paper with the names and prices in the pockets of coats hanging up. Everything was arranged in the most methodical order and all the goods were classified.</p>
        <p>Mrs., Wilcock said that these things at the back of the premises were laid by for the people who sold them. She 'sold these children things which she had bought from them. The Chairman (Councillor Henry Roberts) asked defendant if she had bought the things outright what need was there for putting the names,of the people on them: Defendant said she got a paper from the police every morning with -a list of things that had been stolen, and in case' she bought stolen articles she could identify the persons she got them from.</p>
        <p>A Previous Charge.</p>
        <p>Superintendent Williamson said that in Afay, 1914, accused were • before the Court on a similai charge. On that occasion they were ordered to pay costs and a very strong caution was administered. They then gave an under- taking to desist from the practice. • The Chairman said the defendants had been committing a fraud on the revenue for some considerable time. Each of them must be fined £7 10s., being the amount of the licence they should have paid, or, in default, two months' imprisonment. If they intended to carry on this business they would have to take out the pawnbroker's licence,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-09-23_WidnesExaminer_art0021" n="art0021">
        <head>DEFENCE OF THE REALM.</head>
        <p>DEFENCE OF THE REALM.</p>
        <p>Military Service_ Act And Registration</p>
        <p>Order Cases.</p>
        <p>Several cases under the Military Service Act and Registration Order were heard at the Borough Police Court on Monday. Patrick Cullen, no settled abode, was charged with +failing to report himself to the authorities under the Military Service Act, and, further, with failing to register. Defendant pleaded guilty. Mr. Nicholls, chief constable, said defendant is about 36 years of age. Men of his class .put the military people to no end of trouble, ,and he submitted that he should not be treated the same as a man who flies to the Colours, lie should be punished as well as handed over to the military.—The magistrates ordered defend- ant to be handed over and fined him 405., to be deducted from his Army pay. , Wm. Anderson, no settled abode, stated that he had been registered while an inmate of Swansea Prison, but did not get a card. He said he was 39 years of age, had been medically examined in Liverpool, and was willing to join the Army. On the other hand the Chief Con- stable said the man had served a term in Usk Prison and had been five days in a Welsh /66gi- ment, from Which he was discharged as unfit. Fined 40s. or a month.</p>
        <p>Patrick Cooney, 11, Stanley-street, was charged by Detective-Sergeant Reddington, for failing to register.—The Chief Constable said the plan expressed his willingness to join the Army, probably because he knew he was unfit. (Laughter.) Ile had come -over from Ireland about three months ago and had been in War- rington since the 23rd June.—He was fined 205.; or, in default, 14 days' imprisonment, John Cooke, no fixed residence, who stated he had been registered in Shepherd-street, Pres- ton, was fined 405.; or a month's imprisonment in default: The Chief Constable said the man had evidently signed the usual list kept in lodging-houses, as required by he Registration of Aliens' Act.</p>
        <p>A fine of 10s., or, in default, 11 days, was imposed on Peter Banbrick, a nomadic Scots- man, who had failed to register. The Chief Constable raised a laugh by saying defendant was the dirtiest Scotsman he had ever seen, and, judging by his nose, he must have been working last in a distillery. Defendant said he worked in the Dunlop Rubber Company. The Chief Constable: Well, I think they use spirit there. (Laughter.) In addition to a charge of being 'drunk and disorderly, an elderly man named William O'Connor,/no settled abode, was prosecuted for not having a registration card. Defendant said that he was serving in the 'Army when the Registration Acrcame into force, and wb's,only discharged on the 7th April of the present year. The Chief Constable said it was true that de- fendant had been in the Army. It appeared that he had served 306 days and was discharged on the 7th April, 1916, as being no longer fit for military service. His age was entered as 44 years, but he (the Chief Constable) supposed it should ,be. 64.</p>
        <p>Defendant said he was 59, but had given his age as 44 when he joined. The Chief Constable: That may be so. Defendant was fined 95., or, in default, seven days' imprisonment on the charge of drunken- ness and disorderly conduct.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-09-23_WidnesExaminer_art0022" n="art0022">
        <head>POLICE POOR BOX.</head>
        <p>POLICE POOR BOX.</p>
        <p>Contributions From Motoring</p>
        <p>Transgressors.</p>
        <p>At Tuesday's Borough Police Court, William 'Boardman,, 158, Market-street, Hindley, was summoned by' Police-Constable Barr for using head-lights On a motor car on the Winwick- road at 10.50 p.m. on Septeml?er 3rd, contrary to the Defence of the Realm Regulations. The head-lights were obscured.—Defendant said that he had had his driver's licence for fifteen</p>
        <p>years and never had a mark on it.—Under the I circumstances the Chairman (Councillor Henry Roberts) said the Bench would not Mark a conviction if defendant would put 20s. into the Popr Box.—Defendant willingly agreed to do so. 41 similar concession was made in the case of a youth named Frank Faulkes, 201, Padgate- lane, who was prosecuted by Police-Constable Bennett for using two head-lights on a motor van.—Defendant, who had told the Court that the oil had run out in his side-lamps, looked rather glum when the contribution of 20s. was suggested by the Chairman, but apparently altered his view of the situation when Coun- cillor Roberts asked him if he knew that there was a very heavy penalty and imprisonment for the offence.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-09-23_WidnesExaminer_art0023" n="art0023">
        <head>ERRING FARMERS.</head>
        <p>ERRING FARMERS.</p>
        <p>The Need ;For Lists Of Employes Of Military Age.</p>
        <p>At the Warrington County Petty Sessions, held on Wednesday, a number of farmers were summoned for not having posted on their pre- mises, according to the requirements of the Defence of the Realm Act, a list of their male employes of military age. Such notices have to be "constantly posted in a conspicuous place." The decisions of the Bench were :—James Fairclough,- Brookhead . Farm, Burtonwood, fined 2s. 6d.; William Warburton, Dallam Farm, Burtonwood, fined 2s. 6d.; James Hatton, Dallam House Farm, Burtonwood, fined 2s. 6d.; 'Harry Bridge, Farm-in-th'-Field, Burtonwood, fined. 2s. 6d.; Joseph Hurst, Sankey Lodge Farm, fined 2s. 651.; Mrs. Mary E. Kilshaw, Ivy House• Farm, Sankey, case dismissed; John Millington, Primrose Valley Farm, Cuerdley, fined 2s. 6d.; and John Henry Crossby, Cuerdley Hall Farm, Cuerdley, fined ss. The Chairman (Councillor H. Roberts), who was accompanied on the Bench by Mr. W. Furness and Mr. W. Fairclough, said that as those were the first cases of that character that had come before the notice of the Bench they were inclined to treat them somewhat leniently. They would• deal more severely in future cases.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-09-23_WidnesExaminer_art0024" n="art0024">
        <head>REGISTRATION OF ALIENS.</head>
        <p>REGISTRATION OF ALIENS.</p>
        <p>Boarding-House Keeper's Responsi-</p>
        <p>bilities-.</p>
        <p>A charge against a boarding-house keeper named Sarah Wall, 27, Lovely-lane, for failing to register the name of an Italian who lodged at 'her house from the 4th to Bth August, was heard by the magistrates at Thursday's Borough Police Court.</p>
        <p>Detective Anderson said defendant's ex- planation was that the man had been unwell during the period, and that she had not been able to get particulars from him. Sbperintendent Williamson said the police knew of this man coming from Chester to War- rington to carry out some small job at a local works under a sub-contractor, but they were unable to trace where he was living until a special' officer was detailed for the purpose. It was important that people keeping boai ding- houses should recognise their responsibilities. A mitigated fine of ss. was imposed: ,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-09-23_WidnesExaminer_art0025" n="art0025">
        <head>LIGHTS EXPOSED.</head>
        <p>LIGHTS EXPOSED.</p>
        <p>At Monday's Borough Police Court the follow- ing fines were imposed on persons who had failed tq obscure lights alter scheduled time in business or private houses: Cecil W. Loy. Bewsey-street, 10s.; Fred Howard, 1, Whalley- street, 55.; Fanny Pearson, re premises, 124, Bridge-street, ss. For failing to subdue lights in their resi- dences the following were fined at Tuesday's Borough Police Court :—Evan Hughes, Cros- field-street, 55.; Edwin Edwards, 1, Fitzherbert- street, 10s.; Harry Dunn, 25, Lockett-street, ss. - The Chairman (Councillor Roberts) said the offence must not be repeated, as it was a very serious one indeed. A NORWEGIAN'S MOVEMENTS.</p>
        <p>Frederick Mann Hansen, a Norwegian, of 127, Wilderspool-road, was charged At the Borough Police Court on Thursday by Supt. Williamson with having committed a• breach of the Aliens'. Restriction Order. It appears that he had gone to Blackpool from Warrington on the 19th August, and returned on the 23rd of the same month. He failed to notify his in- tended change of address to the police, and did not inform them of his return.</p>
        <p>Superintendent Williamson said the police did not suggest any ulterior motive on the part of the defendant.</p>
        <p>A fine of 10s. was imposed.</p>
        <p>HAD PRESENTED HIMSELF.</p>
        <p>Arthur Herbert Marsh, 39, Winwick-road, was charged at the Borough Police Court on Thurs- day by Acting-Sergeant Hayes with having failed to report himself to the authorities as required by the Military Service Act. The officer stated that he arrested defendant in consequence of a telephone message received from the military at Orford Barracks. Superintendent Williamson said that the de- fendant had actually presented himself for enlistment.</p>
        <p>Under 'the circumstances the Court only made an order handing defendant over to the military escort in waiting.</p>
        <p>CHARGE AGAINST ARMY CORPORAL.</p>
        <p>At the Borough Police Court, yesterday, Corporal Joseph Thomason, a the South Lan- cashire Regiment, was charged by P.C. Clarke (Cheshire Lines Committee) with having been drunk and disorderly at the Central Railway Station the previous night. He was further charged with assaulting Ticket Collector Har- rington and Police-Constable Joynson. Constable Clarke stated that accused, who, with a private, was escorting a prisoner, was creating a disturbance on the south platform. The clerk in the booking office had refused to issue a ticket as the 10.15 train from Liverpool was about to leave the station. Defendant struck Ticket Collector, Harrington across the face and also assaulted P.C. Joynson in a simi- lar manner. He was in a drunken condition and used filthy and abusive language. He was arrested and taken to the Police Station.</p>
        <p>• Corroborative evidence was given by P.C. Joynson, Ticket Collector Harrington, and the booking clerk, Mr. Jackson. Defendant indignantly denied that he was</p>
        <p>• drunk or that he had used filthy language. It was not true that he had assaulted the persons mentioned.</p>
        <p>Chief Constable Nicholls said that on the pre- vious night at 6.30, the prisoner, with another escort, arrived at the Police Station to take over an Army absentee. When they came they were sober and the absentee was handed over at 6.30. They should have taken the prisoner away by the train before seven. When they turned up again at the Police Station after ten their condition was obvious. It was clear that the prisoner was too drunk to be allowed to go into the train.</p>
        <p>Defendant renewed his denial of the charges preferred against him and said he allowed the prisoner to go home to get some clothes and to see his wife and children. They all had some dinner in the house and a drink of beer. He had only one other drink afterwards during the evening. So long as he got his prisoner back at a reasonable time it was all right. He was naturally excited at not being allowed to catch the train.</p>
        <p>The magistrates fined defendant five shillings.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-09-23_WidnesExaminer_art0026" n="art0026">
        <head>MUNITIONS COURT.</head>
        <p>MUNITIONS COURT.</p>
        <p>Several Workmen Lay Corn- plaints Before Tribunal.</p>
        <p>Several interesting cases were heard at the Munitions Court on Monday, Mr. T. J. RidgwaY presiding. Henry Hill, 284, Winwick-road, aged 19, and who had been employed as a wire twister bY a local firm for the past eighteen months, asked for his certificate on the ground that he could better his position elsewhere. It was stated that the certificate had been refused as they were shorthanded. Applicant could earn up to £2 a week on piecework.—The certificate was refused. •</p>
        <p>Garfield Furnival (24), 171, Thelwall-lane, Latchford, wireworker, sought for a certificate from a Grappenhall firm of Wire manufacturers on the ground of ill-health. He had been seven years and nine months with the firm and hit wages were 265. a week. He had given the usual notice to leave.—lt transpired that he intended to get a position in another firm, and as the Court thought the conditions would be somewhat the same, as far as applicant's health was concerned, the certificate was fused.</p>
        <p>James Bell, 4, King-street, complained that a local coal and iron firm had unreasonablyi withheld his certificate. He applied for same, as he was suffering from Varicose veins and had been attended by the doctor. He wished to obtain more suitable employment. A doc- tor's certificate was 'produced.—A representa- tive of the firm said they would have been quite prepared to release this man if they could have got another man in his place. If they knew of the doctor's certificate they would have given him easier work.—The certificate was granted.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-09-23_WidnesExaminer_art0027" n="art0027">
        <head>Rejected Man.</head>
        <p>A Rejected Man.</p>
        <p>Arthur Charlton, 52, Wash-lane, Latchford, a moulder employed by a local firm, for whom he has been working for eight years, asked the Court to grant him a certificate, his allegatien being that the work was too heavy for him. Mr: Sinclair, secretary to the Moulders Union, who represented applicant, said the man wask•2l years a agn, and when he found the work too heavy be asked for lighter work, but did not get it. They had a doctor's certifi- cate saying that 11% was not fit for the work. The firm's, representative said that applicant had been- on the heaviest work in the shop. for nine weeks and had never complained. • , Applicant: I complained three weeks ago-._</p>
        <p>The Representitiv-e: You did not complain. until you were rejected finally for the Army. Mr. Sinclair said the man made a fool of himself keeping on working as he did; but, of course,-he was earning good money and kept on.</p>
        <p>The representative submitted that no con ?- plaint had been made about the work until three weeks ago, when the man was rejectea for the Army. Forty-three per eent. of the firm's men had been lost out of the foundrip and they were allowed to retain the medical'''. rejected men. The representative •added that he had never seen the doctor's certificate.</p>
        <p>The application was granted. A' Question of Wages.</p>
        <p>Alfred Hewitt, Stockoley Farm, Stretton, moulder, employed by the same firm, com- plained that they had unreasonably withheld his certificate. The reason he asked for the certificate was that he had been given a clas9 of work to do from which the standard rate of pay for moulders could not be made.</p>
        <p>Mr. Sinclair, who appeared in this case also; said this man cycled six miles each night and morning to and from his work. Owing to thr, description of work given him to do he ha° only drawn 14s. one week and 225. another week. The standard rate for moulders via° £2 25., with 4s. war bonus. The .representative of the firm produced sheets showing that for the twelve weeks end- ing May 13th applicant had drawn average wages of £2 2s. 54. and war bonus, the rate of pay being 10d. per hour, and for the twelve weeks ending August sth the amount WO £2 14s. 4d. The trade official was there t° make up and price the work and the firm were atwayS willing to assist. the men in every vial they possibly could. All the applicant had to do was to go to the trade secretary and have the 'matter settled. - ,The certificate was refused.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-09-23_WidnesExaminer_art0028" n="art0028">
        <head>...d&amp;i.</head>
