      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-09-23_WidnesExaminer_art0029" n="art0029">
        <head>Ittters woe Oita</head>
        <p>Ittters woe Oita</p>
        <p>CONCERTS FOR NA OI 'NDED</p>
        <p>SOLDIERS.</p>
        <p>Sir,—May I suggest that some organised effort be put forward to provide good concerts for the wounded " Tommies" in our midst? Last winter parties visited the hospitals, but there were not enough, and I think if one of our leading artistes would undertake the responsi- bility of organising the singers, &amp;e., of the town much more could be accomplished instead of throwing the burden upon one or two. I feel .eertain a ready response will be shown. The " Tommies" enjoy and desire good music, and, I can assure you, if anyone will move in the matter, at the eud of the season they will feel well repaid. AMATEUR.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-09-23_WidnesExaminer_art0030" n="art0030">
        <head>EXCLUDED AND ISOLATED.</head>
        <p>EXCLUDED AND ISOLATED.</p>
        <p>The Position Of Cairo Street Chapel</p>
        <p>And Its Minister.</p>
        <p>Sir,—l feel that some comment must be made on the Rev. W. Reid's very sympathetic and illuminating letter of last week. But, first of all, let me remove what may be a certain dis- agreeable thought in the minds of some of your readers.</p>
        <p>It may he imagined that, in the sermon I Preached a fortnight ago, I went out of my way considerably to criticise unfavourably my brother Free Church ministers. What I said Was in explanation of our position to my own People, for our isolation implies condemnation on the part of others, and we must occasionally justify our position for our own satisfaction. Vurther, it is contended by everyone at the pre- sent time that Churches and even the ChriAtian faith are in some peril; that they have to meet a new situation demanding greater loyally and service, and if they do not prepare themselves and reform themselves they are doomed. And, I for one, am convinced that unhappy divisions Is one of the great causes of the ineffectiveness of the whole Church, and that now is the time to examine ourselves, and, if pbasible, draw closer together in united service. So much in justification of this discussion.</p>
        <p>Now, Mr. Reid informs us that on two occasions there have been discussions amongst Free Church ministers as to whether the minis- ter of Cairo-street Chapel should be admitted to their fellowship, and that the decisions on both occasions have been that he shalL.not be admitted. To say the least of it, this is not a very dignified position for the oldest Free Church in 'Warrington to ovupy, a Church Which, as I said' a fortnight ago, was establishei in the interests of freedom in worship, and Which has been served by some of the ablest ministers and scholars and most useful and honourable citizens of Warrington; and surely, one can be excused for being indignant at such treatment. It is but necessary to recall the, close connection of The Academy with Cairo- street Chapel, and the names of Priestley, John Howard, Letitia Barbauld, Philip Car- penter, Rev. R. Pilcher, who to his honour and glory was known amongst many as "The Good Samaritan," and orm.wiLl realise that there can be no reasonable ground for this isolation and ostracism. On the contrary, if the Free Church ministers had but a glimmering of "the his- torical sense," and took the trouble to find out how Cairo-street Chapel, has been and is in- volved in the best traditions of the town, they would count it an honour to have it in their fellowship, and to work in harmony with it. However, I do not want to say hard words. But, we are told that "it is from the tail of the sermon there strikes the note which accounts for the undesirable position complained of." It is because of our position about the Trinity. Now, I am not going to enter into a theological discussion at the moment. But, I should like to be quite candid and positive. There is only one doctrine of the Trinity, and it is the basis of all statements of belief of the so-called orthodox Churches. It finds classic and authoritative expression in the Athanasian Creed. For myself, I reject that doctrine as there stated, because—and I say this in all humility and only for myself—it is unintelli- gible, unscriptural, and unsound. There are many Free Church teachers who have evolved doctrines of the Trinity of their own, and with many of them I can agree. There is one in last</p>
        <p>week's "Christian Commonwealth." But, when one speaks of the Ancient Catholic doc- trine of the Trinity, it is the one set forth in the Athanasian Creed which Is -._twetint. Now, do these Free Church ministers all agree on this doctrine? Will they all accept Mr. Reid's statement of it? It is no use quibbling about the matter for a moment. I know that they do not; and there are hundreds of Free Church ministers who do not. There are a score of interpretations of the Trinity, and they are all as heretical as my Unitarian doctrine, and some of them a good deal more so. Therefore, the position is this. The minister of Cairo-street Chapel is excluded from Free Church fellowship because he does not agree with these ministers about a doctrine on which they themselves can claim no unanimity. But, there is a more serious ques- tion I should like to ask. If one is excluded from Free Church fellowship because, in the opinion of Free Church ministers, he is unsound on the Trinity, is the same rule enforced with regard to Church members and office bearers? If it is not; and I know it is not, then there is inconsistency which is grossly culpable. But, is it .not time that we, ministers of all Churches, realised that there is a great deal more on which we are united than on which we differ, and that the former is more.important? We are not competitors for the orthodox or heterodox suffrages and support of the people, but we are humbly trying in all ways we can to build up a Christian community. There are a score of ways in which we might help one another, if we could only come together in the right spirit. And in many ways we already do help each other. I have Anglican friends who have given -me ideas I have found useful. I admit my inidebtedness to much which Canon Morley Stevenson has written. Conversations with other ministers yield their fruit, and we are all convinced that our power would be in- creased if we united together for fellowship, emphasising our unity instead of our disagree- ments. I am willing, and I think there are others also who are willing, to attempt to create a real Christian fellowship amongst ministers and laymen; and I make the sugges- tion because I think it is a good one, that we should all, Anglican clergymen, ministers of Congregational, Baptist, and Wesleyan de- nominations, Roman Catholic priests, and as many laymen and women as care to join, meet at stated times for (1) meditation and prayer, (2) discussion of the problems and difficulties which confront us all.</p>
        <p>IRVINE LISTER.</p>
        <p>Cairo-street Chapel, September 20th, 1916.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-09-23_WidnesExaminer_art0031" n="art0031">
        <head>EDUCATION AFTER THE WAR.</head>
        <p>EDUCATION AFTER THE WAR.</p>
        <p>Sir,—Despite the absorbing interest of war, and despite the political truce, the problems of national education are appropriating a large share of public attention. They are penetrating also into the political arena; for the misfortune is that almost every step in educational progress becomes entangled with politics, an may be blocked or hampered in the House of Commons. The Committee of the National Education Association feel that they must prepare, even during war, for the coming struggle and they invite your readers to join them. The Associa- tion has taken a leading but unassuming part on behalf of popular education in most of the struggles since 1870; but the great result is yet to come. Our programme is very simple; it is the full and free development of the intellectual capacity of all, under the guidance and main- tenance of public authorities controlled by pub- lic opinion. In -pressing towards this mark we are tied to no party, no creed, no sectional in- terest; almost alone amongst organisations we are free to pursue all developments of national education in public interest alonm</p>
        <p>Whilst the -war lasts we are not proposing to ask for a campaign fund. Our immediate pur- pose is to enrol men and women who care about national education, so that we can send them our papers, receive their suggestions, :invite them to our meetings, and in other ways ,pre- pare to have their co-operation at hand when the time for prompt action arises. The nominal enrolment fee (55.) will cover the cost entailed by the effort to secure this object. Preparation and organisation is our aim so that after the war, when molly other problems will force themselves upon statesmen and the public, we as a united body may be ready to keep education problems to the front. JOHN MASSIE, Chairman. 'A. J. MUNDELLA, Secretary. National Education Association, Canton House, Westminster, S.W.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-09-23_WidnesExaminer_art0032" n="art0032">
        <head>EXAMINER, SATURDAY, SEPTEMBER 23, 1916.</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER, SATURDAY, SEPTEMBER 23, 1916.</p>
        <p>SOLDIERS AND Itlf CHURCH.</p>
        <p>Rev. H. Ogden Blames Outside</p>
        <p>Influence For Failure.</p>
        <p>Last • Sunday evening at Golborne-street Baptist Church, the Pastor, Rev. H. Ogden, preached from the topic, "Has the Church failed ?" taking for his text Ephesians v., 23, "Christ is the Head of the Church." The sub- ject had been suggested by the utterance of an officer invalided home from the Front. Ex- pressing his opinion about chaplains at the Front, he said, "New padres who joined the mess were treated with indifference. When a padre received ' honour in the mess he had earned it. Right down in the minds of the men who had been in this hell was the query, "What had the Church been doing these many centuries?"</p>
        <p>The padres, as representatives of official Christianity, were under that cloud. What had the Church been doing these many cen- turies? "It is," said Mr. Ogden, "obvious that to prevent this European War the Church as an international organisation has done little. She failed to restrain the passion of kings and governors, and when war broke out she went down like a reed. The reason is not far to seek. There have been other heads than Christ. Allegiance to her risen• Lord has been con- ditional, and the conditional has destroyed her power. Lloyd George said concerning the power of the Church 'There is no evil could with- stand her combined forces if she were united.' Is she were united! What a significant if! The Church in the presence of gigantic evils has been at the best semi-helpless. The reason is not far to seek. A State Church financed by the State has her master to consider. Finance is a power- ful influence even in Churches. How otherwise could the Lutheran Church have been quiescent whilst Bernhardi preached that right is right so long as it is convenient? A State Church too that was Protestant.</p>
        <p>Helpless In England As Well As In Germany. "But why be content with a German ex- ample? No one but must reverence the piety of Anglicans, and yet the powerful Church of England has been semi-helpless in the face of immense social evils. A Free Church is not without its bane. Free Church ministers' lips have been silenced, or their speech rendered innocuous because the leading deacon or society 'steward resents such plain speech. The trouble with the Church was and is that she has other masters than her Lord Jesus Christ., In the face of a united International Church what Emperor or his son would have dared to break the European peace? They dared because kings and courts were the masters of the Church. The Church should enthrone the, Christ and bow to no other master and her power would return. "The Church has had her triumphs. She has striven to bring up the young in the fear of the Lord. She has given of wealth of money and lives to carry the Gospel to heathen lands. In- dividuals and isolated communities have fought the battle of the oppressed.. The failure of the Church has been relative rather than complete. She may be bankrupt, but she has some assets, but when Christ is the head of all the Church then will be ushered in the glorious day."</p>
        <p>4C.,++,0+0,44 .:**:. ..:. ..:. ...;.. •</p>
        <p>Children Corner.;</p>
        <p>o Conducted by UNCLE JACK. 4 • •</p>
        <p>4, 41. ie.., ++o 4. 4.0 4. ••4. •</p>
        <p>FRENCH TOWNS OF INTEREST.</p>
        <p>My Dear Children,— I have had a. very entertaining set of letters from you this week respecting the towns in France you would like to visit. Some of the towns named are not in France. Mons and Ypres are in Belgium, and however good an essay is, if it does not answer the question put, it cannot succeed. You must all remem- ber that at school, for many marks are lost through answering questions in the wrong way. I remember when I was a boy dividing a sum that should have been subtracted. I did the division correctly, but as a subtraction sum it was no use at all. It lost me a few marks and a big prize, so I have always been careful since to answer exactly the question I am asked. .</p>
        <p>I have given the prize th 4 week to 813\ Niece Emily Marshall,</p>
        <p>183, Winwick-road, who attends Silver-street School, an old member of the Corner, and a regular contributor to itS columns. This week we will have for our competition an essay on</p>
        <p>Comforts That Are Needed For Our Soldiers At</p>
        <p>The Frcnt.</p>
        <p>Winter is now eoming along, and comforts will be badly needed almost at /once. As you will see in to day's "Examiner," an appeal is being made for money with whieh to buy material to be made into socks, mits and other useful articles for the soldiers. That subject Should, I think, bring a lot of answers, for you are all interested in doing something for,our soldiers. Your loving, UNCLE JACK.</p>
        <p>(By permission of the Proprietors of "Punch!')</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-09-23_WidnesExaminer_art0033" n="art0033">
        <head>.:. .:. .:. 4, .:. .:. .:. .:. .:. .:. .:. .:. .:. .:. .:. .:. .:. .:. .:. .:..:. .:. .:. .:. .:. .:. .:. .:. .:. 4,* .:. 4. 4. .:. .:. .:. .:. .:. .:. .:. 8 .:. 4. 4.4. .:. .:. .c. ..... .:. .:. .:. .:. • 4. 4. .:. .:. .:. .:4 .:. .:. .:. .:. .:. .:. .:. 0 .:. .:. .:. 0 4. .:. .:. .:. .:. .:. .:. • .:. .:‘ * .:. .:. .:. .:. .1.. .:. .:. .:. .:. .:.</head>
        <p>.:. .:. .:. 4, .:. .:. .:. .:. .:. .:. .:. .:. .:. .:. .:. .:. .:. .:. .:. .:..:. .:. .:. .:. .:. .:. .:. .:. .:. 4,* .:. 4. 4. .:. .:. .:. .:. .:. .:. .:. 8 .:. 4. 4.4. .:. .:. .c. ..... .:. .:. .:. .:. • 4. 4. .:. .:. .:. .:4 .:. .:. .:. .:. .:. .:. .:. 0 .:. .:. .:. 0 4. .:. .:. .:. .:. .:. .:. • .:. .:‘ * .:. .:. .:. .:. .1.. .:. .:. .:. .:. .:.</p>
        <p>' .:..:. .. , .:..:. .:.• .13 AT WORLD 3 .... . 4.4. 4..:. .</p>
        <p>• • •</p>
        <p>women are about to patent a number of devites which are improvements on these. Thousands of women, new to industrial employment, work sewing machines and make khaki caps, over- alls, &amp;c. There are no slagkers among women, who are eager and quick to learn, and punc- tual and regular in their attendance_ at the works. Satin for Tailor-Mades.</p>
        <p>Apparently taffetas han at last reached the zenith of its popularity, and this autumn, at any rate, it will give place to satin. The latter fabric combines excellently with cloth, and tailors are using the two for the new one- piece dresses and coats. Velvet and satin is another favourite combination for the autumn, and each and all look well with fur. Dark colours are to prevail, various shades of wine being specially favoured for the moment. Rather freakish and very ugly were the skirts of uneven length that had a slight following a little time ago, on account of their novelty; they have, however, vanished, and fashion now decrees that the shortness must be at the back. The slight lifting is rather smart, for it is the right Place. Skirts are a trifle narrower and decidedly longer; exaggeration in any form is happily considered bad taste at the present time. The Piquant Small Muff.</p>
        <p>Fashion has always been given to extremes, and this winter the fashionable muff is very small, scarcely big enough to accommodate the hands of the wearer. The big muff had advan- tages; it was a most useful receptacle for small parcels, and as it covered a considerable por- tion of the body it was conducive to warmth. There is something piquant, however, in the small melon-shaped muff that is first favourite at present, and it goes 'very well with the fur- trimmed - tailor-made. Big muffs will not dis- appear this winter, more especially when made in strands of expensive peltry. The new collars, capes and stoles are almost grotesque in shape, and chiefly built of stranded furs that cover the "ears and chin. They can be worn in different ways, open or closed, and are lined with magnificent brocades, often veiled with white or coloured chiffon. The fox skins that have been donned all the summer will be modish throughout the coming season, the head and paws of the animal being used, to finish the ends and back. Benzoate of Soda. -</p>
        <p>Communications have reached the Board of Agriculture and Fisheries referring to a state- ment Which has appeared in the public Press to the effect that benzoate of soda may be used to replace sugar in the preparation of jam. The Board are advised that benzoate of soda is quite unsuitable for the purpose in question, and desire to warn the public against its use in jam-making. Serious results might follow an attempt to substitute this material for sugar.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-09-23_WidnesExaminer_art0034" n="art0034">
        <head>CHAT and GOSSIP.</head>
        <p>CHAT and GOSSIP.</p>
        <p>By MARKIRIE.</p>
        <p>Royal and Noble Artists. British royal and aristocratic ladies do not, in these democratic days, despise authorship, whilst not a few are first-rate sculptors and painters. That versatile Princess, the widowed Duchess of Argyll, is said to have contributed —under an assumed name—a number of learned articles to leading reviews and papers. She, too, is a noted sculptor. The Queen of Rou- mania, another British Princess, has published several books and has a natural gift for lan- guages and an innate taste for art. The Dowager Duchess of Buckingham and Chandos, who exhibited her pictures in 1912, is now arranging another display on behalf of war charities. Her Grace has made a reputation as a painter of portraits and landscapes, and is also an author and a great traveller.</p>
        <p>Lady Dorothy Fielding. When it was announced that King George had decided to award the Military Medal to women there was much speculation as to who -would be the first recipient of that coveted distinction. The first member of the gentler sex to be so decorated was a French girl—. Mdlle. Emmellienne Moreau, the heroine of Loos, as she is called.. The second recipient is a .Welsh woman, Lady Dorothy Fielding, the daughter of the Earl of Denbigh. Her work for the nation began in September, 1914, in Belgium, where, with her heroic friends, Mrs. Knock and Miss Chisholm, she tended the wounded for month's in the cellar of a house close to the firing-line. In addition to other work, Lady Dorothy distinguished herself as an ambulance driver at Ypres. She has re- ceived the highest Belgian decoration from King Albert, and the . Croft de Guerre from the French Government. Tributes to Women Workers.</p>
        <p>Lady Scott, the widow of the'great explorer, has been working for a long time in a muni- tion factory. She was most desirous to procure a weekly half-holiday for the women under her, and exacted a promise, from the manager to grant the favour, provided a certain maxi- mum was attained during the week. The girls' output .exceeded the highest estimate, and has been maintained ever since. Women have mastered the handling of eighteen- pounder shells, and now finish . and pack them. They also work wonders at lathes, presses, drills, and machines requiring great care and exactitude. Men claim nearly all the inventions used for the various processes, but</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-09-23_WidnesExaminer_art0035" n="art0035">
        <head>WAR, THE LEVELLER.</head>
        <p>WAR, THE LEVELLER.</p>
        <p>Emily Sparrow (who voluntarily does the washing-up at our soldiers' canteen each evening from 8 to 12): "Nah, then, Lady Mont- gumery-Wilberforce, 'urry up pith them plates!"</p>
        <p>earned.; and to see our motor ambulance corps bringing our wounded to the base hospital, and the German prisoners having a rest before they gci on a large motor to a place where they can get on a ship and sail to England. I would go and see the graves of some of our soldiers who have been killed in the war."</p>
        <p>Niece Lily McLachlan, 8, Ash-grove, Latch- ford, age 12 (Bolton Council School).—" 1 should like to go to Paris, .whicv is the capital of France, and stands on the river Seine. It is the largest, gayest and most beautiful town in France. Its public buildings are grander and its streets are brighter than any other town. Many of its streets are planted with rows of trees; which give the town • a nice appear- ance. I should like to see the public buildings in Paris, which are so splendid. One of its lovely buildings is the Louvre, which used to be a palace, but now it is a museum and art gallery, in which many valuable pictures can be seen. I should like to see the Notre Dame Cathedral, which is the finest of all the build- ings. Other famous buildings I would go to see would be the University and the Church of St. Louis, in which Napoleon was buried; Eiffel Tower, which is the highest building in the world. Paris has many beautiful monuments and fountains in public squares, also splendid cafes in which people sit out in ;the open air in summer and eat their lunch. My reason for going to Paris 'is because it is such a beautiful, interesting place, and its buildings are so historic."</p>
        <p>" Niece F. Osborne, 31, Leicester-street (Evelyn- street School).—" The place I should like to visit most in France is Paris, because at pre- sent it is the centre of great interest owing to the war. I have read in the papers that they have taken a lot of guns there which have been captured, and also many other relics of, ithe battlefield. When the Germans, were making their way to Paris the fortifications of the city were strengthened, and the principal bilildings were protected against aircraft. Now lam sure all'these things would be very interesting for us to see. Paris itself in peace times is very attractive on account of the fine buildings, and it is the centre of fashion, for we get most of our styles of dress from the people of Paris."</p>
        <p>Niece Elsie Johnson, 323, Lovely-lane.-- "Rheims,, with its statues and beautiful monu- ments, erected to the memory of great men. The most interesting place in Rheims is the cathedral, now in ruins. It has stood many' hardships, but none ks great as this, and now it lies helpless. It would take heaps of money to rebuild it, but money cannot make it into its old lifelike form, full of beautiful carvings and stained-glass windows, now ruined by these horrid baby killers, who are up to all kinds f mischief, finding out most of the important .places by spies, who are roaming everywhere. This town is most interesting, and if I could journey there I would be very grateful, and I hope it would not mind its new visitor from 'The Mother Country.'"</p>
        <p>Nephew Edgar Lchrle, 194, Battersby-lane (Beamont).—" The town I would like to visit is Ypres, because of the fighting that has been taking place there. I would like to speak to some of the peasants, too, and ask them what they think of war. also would like to see the havoc wrought by the German artillery to the !lome g .4e pooz inhabitants, who have to</p>
        <p>sleep in open fiel,ds, while we do not know the horrors of war. I thank the Allies, as a British boy should do, for keeping the Germans away from our homes."</p>
        <p>Nephew Denis Farrar, 46, Willis-street (Parochial School).—" The town in France 1 would like to visit is Verdun, after the war, to see the places ruined during the present war. I would see houses, churches, places of enjoy- ment and works destroyed. I would bring many valuable things home with me, which, perhaps, I would find in churches and houses. I would like to go to this town to read some of the names on the graves that might be there, and if I happen to know any of themi I could, perhaps, get a photograph taken of the grave of the person." Niece Lily Williamson, 53, Priestley-street.— "Above all, I would like to visit Ypres, a town where severe fighting has taken place. I would first like to look at the graves, where many men would be, from home and family. I would like to look at the trenches, for I have never seen real ones yet, and have a peep in the dug-outs, where the brave soldiers keep their food. I would .like to look at the ruins, which, I sup- pose, Would be very great. I wonder how many men, women and children have fled from their ruined homes. I would like to look at the guns and to see the soldiets work them. I should expect to see plenty of aeroplanes and other fly- ing things." Niece Dorothy Lowery, 39, Leicester-street (Evelyn-street School).—" There are many towns in France which I should like to visit, but I should especially like to visit Paris, be- cause it is the capital of France. It_is a very gay town in peace time, and all the rich people go there. During this war there would be many different kinds of people, such as Red Cross nurses, Anzacs, English soldiers, and many others there. I should like to see all the beautiful buildings, parks and gardens and to hear the witty French people talk." Niece Edna Fellows, 44, Bewsey-road.—" The town I would like to visit in France I think' is Paris. I have heard times without number of the beauties of Paris, which I can quite believe. I would like to visit the famous places about which we read, such as the Notre Dame. I should also like to see the famous pictures painted by many great painters." -</p>
        <p>Nephew Frank Overend, 74, Lythgoes-lane (Hamilton-street School).—" I would like to visit Peronne, where our gallant soldiers have made their name, so as to have a bird's eye view of the firing line and to see the enemy in their excitement. I would compare the ways of the French lieople by the English, and go to look at some of the glorious buildings which have been destroyed by the Germans. I would journey in the country to see the wounded soldiers, who are being helped to get cured by our to heroines, thp Red Cross nurses, and try to make them happy in some way. After I would see how the French school children are edueated."</p>
        <p>Nephew F. Edwards, 8, Monks-street.—" would now like to visit Calais to see what kind of a harbour it has, and I would compare Liver- pool and Calais and see which was the better. If Liverpool ..von the contest, which I probably think she would, I would take another 'English harbour and I would compare and contrast them. I would also like to 'Bee the boatloads of</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-09-23_WidnesExaminer_art0036" n="art0036">
        <head>PRIZE ESSAY.</head>
        <p>THE PRIZE ESSAY.</p>
        <p>By Niece Emily Marshall, 183, Winwick- road, age 10 (Silver-street School) : "One of the chief towns of J. 411 Belle France is Rouen, the principal French cotton town! It has a splendid \cathedral. In its market place is a statue of Jean D'arc, or Joan 9f Arc, who was burned there by the English in 1431; they said that she was a. witch. Rouen is on the river Seine. A bit further down this river is the city of Paris. I should like to go to Rouen if only to see its cathedral. It is also noted for its silk manufactories."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-09-23_WidnesExaminer_art0037" n="art0037">
        <head>HIGHLY COMMENDED.</head>
        <p>HIGHLY COMMENDED.</p>
        <p>Nephew Harry Heywood,-age 9,8, Matthew. stieet (Parochial School).—" A town in France I would like to visit is Rouen. I am sure it must be a grand sight to see our brave soldiers in their full kit leaving for the trenches; to see those that have been fighting coming to Iteu:,,n for a 411ort rest, which they have well-</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-09-23_WidnesExaminer_art0038" n="art0038">
        <head>FARM AND GARDEN.</head>
        <p>FARM AND GARDEN.</p>
        <p>CROPS UNDER FRUIT TREES.</p>
        <p>Fruit growers on a large scale seldom trouble with under crops, chiefly because they inter- fere with horse cultivation between the trees, and the crops are not considered worth the extra expense of hand cultivation. Smaller growers, however, have to make the most of their limited ground, and with them under- cropping is common. In the Evesham district, for instance, where most of - the holdings are small, and hand cultivation is tha general rut?, many crops are grown under the fruit trees. Strawberries are a very suitable crop to grow among young fruit trees, and if only a single row is planted between every two rows of trees there is no injury to the roots. If strong runners can be obtained for planting at once there will be a little fine fruit next summer, and in the second year the plants will be In their prime. After the third season the trees will probably have grown enough to shade the strawberries, which must then come out. This is a crop to grow only between young trees, but it is possible to grow winter and spring .crops under much older trees, becau,se at these seasons there are no leaves to shade the ground, while the bare branches afford welcome shelter.</p>
        <p>Other suitable crops to grow under fruit trees are cabbages, lettuces, and wallflowers, all of which can be planted out in September. Spring cabbages and lettuces do very well under the shelter of the leafless trees, and the crops are off before the leaves come again. They can be in single rows, cabbages 15in. and lettuces Bin. apart in the rows. Wallflowers are largely grown under the plum trees in the Evesham district; the plants can be 12in. to 15in. apart in the rows.—" Farm and Home."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-09-23_WidnesExaminer_art0039" n="art0039">
        <head>PACIFYING THE BULL.</head>
        <p>PACIFYING THE BULL.</p>
        <p>In a cornbelt neighbourhood three bulls have been simply and cheaply pacified. One was a fence-breaker and the other two were belliger- ent. Now all three behave themselves and remain peacefully in their own pastures. They have been chained. Attached to the nose ring, each of these bulls wears a 3ft. length of chain about as heavy as the old-fashioned trace chain. So long as the bull is quiet and deliberate in his movement the chain seems to cause no pain, but a quick' throw of the head or a poke through the fence gives his nose a sharp jerk by the chain. The automatic and instantaneous punishment for such actions has taught them how to behave. Each of these bulls is allowed to run with the herd all the time. The last one to be treated had become so mean that it was not safe for a man to go near him. Finally he was driven into the barn and tied by the head for weeks. Then it was learned that the other two bulls were successfully, conquered. A breast chain from the team harness was then snapped into his ring, and he was turned loose. He had a stubborn fight for two weeks with the chain. At first he would bellow and paw the dirt when anyone came near, but when his foot would catch the chain he squirmed with pain and quieted down. Even then he would stand his ground and refuse to be driven away until his chain was struck smartly with a club. The old fellow bent his nose ring and injured his nose —and evidently did some thinking. Now he takes gentle care of that chain and allows people to take care of themselveg. This is a simple method. Its one of the cases where the easy and inexpensive process is better than some elaborate device. It takes time, patience, coigne, and eternal vigilance to manage unruly 'bulls. The chain is always fearlessly on the job, and the bull knows it. It is worth using on bulls inclined to take too many privileges with strangers or. their keepers.—" Breeders' Gazette."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-09-23_WidnesExaminer_art0040" n="art0040">
        <head>IN THE GARDEN.</head>
        <p>IN THE GARDEN.</p>
        <p>BORDER CARNATIONS AND PICOTEES.</p>
        <p>These flowers are among the most beautiful grown to-day, and have been cultivated in Eng- land for centuries. It appears they were intro- duced by the Normanf and grown in monastic gardens for their beauty and fragrance. Dur- ing the last few years rapid strides have been made in the cultivation of carnations by florists, and now there are some really grand varieties in commerce. What is being aimed at is stiff- necked stems, erect blooms, non-bursting calyces and good growers. The improvement made in this direction is remarkable, and also in the size and beauty of the blooms. Border carnations give a wide range of colours, which add to their charm. As regards culture; any good ordinary soil will do, deeply dug and plenty of good rotted manure mixed with it., Raise the beds about 6 to 9in. for drainage, and plant about 12 to 15in. apart and make firm. Carnations should be grown in a separate besl to get best results. They also like soot, lime ' and salt during the growing season. Stake up as the flower spikes appear and disbud if size of bloom is desired.</p>
      </div>
