      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-09-23_WidnesExaminer_art0045" n="art0045">
        <head>In Memoriam.</head>
        <p>In Memoriam.</p>
        <p>COYNE.—In loving memory of our dear son, No. Wm. Coyne, aged 24 years, No. 2350, 4th South Lancashire Regiment, who fell in action 25th Sep- tember, 1915. Fondly we loved him, he is dear to us still. But in grief we must bend to God's Holy Will, Our sorrow is great, our loss hard to bear, , But angels, dear son, will guard you with care. Deeply mourned by his MOTHER &amp; FATHER. COYNE.—Private Wm. Coyne (2350), 4th South. Lancashire Regiment, killed in action 25th Sep- tember, 1915. We mourn the loss of our brother, So kind and good and brave, He died a herolon the battlefield. And now lies in a hero's grate. • "May he rest in peace. Amen." Deeply mourned by his SISTERS, BROTHERS, and SISTER-IN-LAW (MILLIE).</p>
        <p>FUNERALS BY J. MOUNFIELD &amp; SONS. The Best in all Grades. Office: 15. Church- street. Tel. 99.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-09-23_WidnesExaminer_art0046" n="art0046">
        <head>EXAMINER,</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER,</p>
        <p>SATURDAY, SEPTEMBER 23, 1916</p>
        <p>1 THE PLIGHT OF GERMANY.</p>
        <p>1_</p>
        <p>r, ,</p>
        <p>f ,1#</p>
        <p>About twelve months ago, when the Rus- sians were applying a check to the advance of the Germans, after what had admittedly been a terribly trying time for all the Allies, it was suddenly giv,en out that Germany had shotfher bolt in the East. Subsequent events have proved that interpretation of the situation to have been a true one, though Germany thought Russia had been put out of the fight. We think we may no* say finally that Ger- many • has shot her bolt, in the West, as well as in South-East Europe. A few Weeks ago there was a suggestion thathe Germans were about •to shorten their lines; it is now main- tained that they are se firmly held that they eCuld,not retreat if they desired to do so. The prospect before them is surrender or annihila- tion, in our part of the arena at all events. It is estimated that between July 1 and Sep- tember 18 we captured no fewer than 500,000 prisoners on the three main front's, and the killed and °wounded must ,have made up a horrible record. Since that date we have</p>
        <p>killed Boches, at an increasing rate, and' we find, as we might expect to find, a chastened tone in the enemy Press. The" Strassburger Post," for instance, -mournfully remarks: "We must not expect the 'battle of the Somme io cease, until the Allies are short of munitions, and that contingency has not yet arisen. The kar is no longer a matter for joy as formerly." The Viennese " Neue Freie Presse " eas discovered that "This is no longer war; it is extermination. It would 'appear that the offensive now so fiercely in progress on the Somme has for its aim simply the necessity of killing. No tactical idea is apparent; it, is simply butchery. One might suppose that the Anglo-French'Governments have made lin their Minds to kill a fixed num- ber of Germans per week or per month." It is a blunt way of stating the case, but it is an accurate way. Nothing but the slaying or the capture of men on the spot where they stand, where they have sinned against all the laws of God and man, will suffices and it is a happy augury that they are beginning to recognise the fact that we have now taken the " butchery " business, of which they once nearly had a monopoly, out of their hands. Our airmen have beaten the Germans to a stand, our inventors have given us modern chariots, our workpeople have given us the ships, the guns, and the shells—and, as we said, the German has shot his bolt. There is evidence that Germany is now short of muni- tions—that se cannot keep up with the pace we have set, a res;ult which redounds to the credit of the worker at home as much as the coming` shortage of German men redounds to the credit of our valiant representatives in the trenches. In the light of the last three months' experience we can well believe that the war has, not been " a matter of joy," as it was " formerly," beyond the Rhine.</p>
        <p>AN ARCH OF CRUTCHES. Unique Feature At A Popular Wedding. An unusual incident at weddings, perhaps unrecorded up to the present, was that which attended the marriage of Mr. Henry Collin, of Winmarleigh-street, and Miss Hammond, matron of the Infirmary, which took place on Saturday at St. Paul's Church. As many as thirty wounded soldiers collected at the approach to the church as early as nine o'clock, and when the happy pair left the build- ing after' the ceremony they had to pass, laughingly and. triumphantly, beneath an arch of crutches. , The nursing staff of the Infirmary hail pre- viously gathered in the hall and corridor to wish their popular matron good lick and God- speed. The officiating clergy were the ,Rev. G. %Fab ' given, vicar r 4 Orford, assisted by the Rev. d. Downham,`curate of St: Paul's. The bride 'was attired in a tailor-made blue coat and skirt and a picture hat, and wore as ornaments a diamond and gold brooch, which - had been, presented to her by the Ladies' Com. mittee of the Infirmary, .and a gold watch bracelet presented to her as a compliment to Mr. Henry Collin, who has for many years acted as president of the Master Builders' Association. The bride was given away by Mr. J. H. Smethurst, and Mr. C. E. Bunch, of Bridge House, acted as best man. Mr. and Mrs. Collin are spending the honey- moon in Blackpool. It is interekting to record that in addition to the contracting parties, those signing the register were Mr. and Mrs. ,Smethurst, Mr. Bunch, and the Misses Marion and Ada. Collin (daughters of the bridegroom), the last two having also tastefully decorated the chancel \with beautiful flowers. -- The public presentations which the bride Ins received have alre9dy been recorded, and we have now to add„with great pleasUre that Mr. Collin has been the recipient of a canteen of, cutlery (supplied by Messrs. Eustance and Co.), from the Conservative Club, of which he is hon. secretary. Mrs. Collin having resigned her *position at the Infirmary, she has been succeeded by Miss Strachan, who has held a similar poht at the .'',2.-Vallasey Hospital, Liscard.</p>
      </div>
