        <p>BIRTHS, MARRIAGES, DEATHS.</p>
        <p>Silver Wedding.</p>
        <p>BANNON—MATHER'.—On September 21st. 1891, at St. Mary's Church. Buttermarket-street, by Rev. 3. E. Turner. 0.5.8.. John Bannon to Margaret A. Mather.</p>
        <p>Deaths.</p>
        <p>ASHTON.—On September 17th, at 265, Knutsford- road, Louisa Ashton, aged 24 years. BENNETT.—On September 19th, at 233, Liverpool- road, Margaret Bennett, aged 72 years. . BROWN.—On September 20th, at 81, Orford-lane, Arthur Brown, aged 20 years</p>
        <p>ROLL OF HONOUR. uPOTTER.—In loving memory of my dear nephew, Private Arthur Potter. who died of disease in Mesopotamia on July 21st, aged 21 years. God knows ho W much we miss him, He knows the tears we shed. And whispers, "Hush, he only sleeps, Your loved one is not dead." The happy hours we once enjoyed. How sweet their memory still: No morning dawns, no night returns, But what we think of him. AUNT EMMIE, COUSINS JACK ec JIM. Priestley-street.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-09-23_WidnesExaminer_art0048" n="art0048">
        <head>GOSSIP.</head>
        <p>GOSSIP.</p>
        <p>I hear that the Mayor-elect is to be fixed upon at the General Purposes Committee on Monday. There is only one possible Mayor- elect at the moment, and that is the present occupant of the chair. I should say Alderman Peter Peacock will preside over our municipal destinies next year, and thus set up a record of a, four-years-continuously-in-office Mayor.</p>
        <p>Under the headline, "The Might of the Mite," the "War Savings Journal" for September; published in London, begins, "This apposite title for an article on the small investor and the war is taken from the report of a War Savings meeting in the 'Warrington Examiner?"</p>
        <p>Alone I did it. I was always good at arresting headlines—as the maid servant said when she took her mistress's new hat for the evening.</p>
        <p>Lieutenant-Colonel George Crosfield, p. 5.0., is again at The /rowers, Bewsey, and knocking about the town generally in a taxi.</p>
        <p>He attended the funeral of Dr. Adams at Lymm otr Tuesday afternoon, and, after liter- ally jumping out of his car, joined his broth.r officers. Colonel Crofffield was in mufti, and his artificial foot was encased ;in a brown boot.</p>
        <p>Mr. Frank Kay, a colleague of mine on "The Examiner" staff, rho enlisted soon after war was declared and .has been in Egypt and Gallipoli almost continuously since, is one of those who have had no leaves of absence.</p>
        <p>• He writes to me this week, saying he hopes in quarters. He now hopes he " may have a spell at home next Christmas—if not at Easter; if not, then August; if not, at Christmas. Any- how, he lives in hope and satisfaction, and is, in fact, a good English soldier who is happy to take thing as be finds them.</p>
        <p>"I am not alone," he says, "for I know lots of fellows who, although they grumble and grouse about soldiering in Egypt, never lose hope. They all look forward with keen antici- pation to going to 'Blighty,' but, be the delay ever so long, they are always cheerfully optimistic and never downhearted." •</p>
        <p>An object lesson-surely to some people who "grouse" at home at the least inconvenience to which they may be put.</p>
        <p>The Corporation are,getting on very well with the repaving of Sankey-strwet. and soon light- footed wayfarers an heavy-rolling vehicles will be thoroughly appreciative of the roadway.</p>
        <p>• In the meantime the romantic young Men and maidens parading at night delight in the fairy- land staging which the 'red, hanging lamps suggest.</p>
        <p>It is not often that the Corporation creates a diversion—the Bewsey 'bus has to get 1.0 Market Gate via Rylands-street.</p>
        <p>For the nonce in many months there has been no sitting of either th 9 Warrington, Warrington Rural District, or Runcorn Rural District Tiibunals this week.</p>
        <p>I wonder where the members have been enjoying di" vacation. Considering the " cer- tain liveliness" which has arisen, probably some unsuccessful 'appellants who have been before the Tribunals would like them to spend it in a little black dug-out in the Et, but I May say that several, have -been to a " Little Grey Horne in' the Wet"—that .at the Royal Court Theatre.</p>
        <p>Nearly a month ago the members of the Gas Committee received with sympathy tile sugges- tion that, owing to the darir.7l._.,,s of the streets at night, lampposts shpuld lie painted white in the middle so as tfi indicate their proximity to pedestrians sensitive to paia when colliding with a harder substance than ?iesh.</p>
        <p>The white armlets began to appear on the lamp-posts this week. vs. ,</p>
        <p>I have been privileged this week to see a letter written by Private Donovan, of the Irish Guards, three days before he was wounded. He was right in the thick of the fighting, as became an Irish Guardsman; but he shows the Army is being well-fed. •</p>
        <p>"Do not send me any more bacon," he wiites home, "as we are ,getting more of it than we can eat. Cake is the most welcome out here, because it is so scarce." I fancy the trenches are not the only places where cake is scarce just now.</p>
        <p>"I got the P.O. you sea. It came in handy, because we were in a well-populated village, and I got five francs for it, and five francs is a nice little sum to have a scout round with in a busy little French town." With bacon plenti- ful and French towns near the fighting line that are worthy of a " svut round," things would appear to be not so bad in Northern France.-</p>
        <p>VIGILANT.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-09-23_WidnesExaminer_art0049" n="art0049">
        <head>" MR. B. T. PARKES.</head>
        <p>" MR. B. T. PARKES.</p>
        <p>Appreciation Of Temperance Worker</p>
        <p>Who Goes To The Colours.</p>
        <p>Mr. B. T. Parkes Ole well-known Temperance and social worker, has joined up, and the fact has been made the occasion for wishing him .the best of luck and of placing on record an appreciation of the valuable services lie has rendered in many directions.</p>
        <p>Warrirgton and District Citinns Temperance</p>
        <p>League.</p>
        <p>The following resolution, moved by the Rev. T. T. James and seconded by Mr. J. Griffiths, has been passed by the above League:— . “That-this meeting of the Citizens' Temper- ance League desires to place on record its high appreciation of the work done by Mr. B. T. Parkes as secretary, its sense of the value of his efforts on behalf of the Temperance cause, and to express to him it good wishes while on service with the Colours, and to hope that he may be speedily at liberty to resume his place in this organisation." \ Legh-street Primitive Methodist Church.</p>
        <p>At the quarterly meeting recently held, the following resolution was passe&amp; proposed by Mr. J. Griffiths and seconded by Mr. A. E. Hoyle:—</p>
        <p>"At this quarterly 'meeting we were reminded of the exacting toll the war is still making on the nation's manhood. The following officials have already. responded to the call :—Brothers Bailey, Metcalfe and Floyd, and now our esteemed and respected Brother Parkes (cir- cuit secretary) has received the call. As this, in all probability, will be the last opportunity of meeting Brother ,Parkes officially, we think it fitting to place .on record our ,high apprecia- tion'-of the invaluable services he has rendered to the/Circuit for so many years. As super, intendent of the Sunday School, local preacher, and circuit Secretary he has shown qualities of able administration and good service. We are gad to Incl.,- that his high character is known Outside the Church. A few years ago the,Citi- zens' Temperance League appointed hi'm their secretary. In this 'position he is best known to the public. His work for Temperance has won him the respect bf alarge circle of friends. We deplore the fact that the exigencies of this tragic war are tearing from our side men like Brother- Parkes,: We pray that the Almight3' Father may watch over him in the interval of separation and may it please Him in His bwn good tithe to restore him to our midst."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-09-23_WidnesExaminer_art0050" n="art0050">
        <head>Warrington Railway Mission.</head>
        <p>Warrington Railway Mission.</p>
        <p>A very successful drawing-room American tea took place at The Willows, Moore, the other day,. when, much attractive plain and fancy work was displayed and speedily disposed of in aid of the funds of the Mission. There was also a large stall of produce and confectionery in the gardens. The 'brief opening ceremony consisted of the hearty singing of the 100th Psalm (Metrical Version), prayer, led by Miss Bogstrom, the Missioner, and a very' apt speech from Miss M. G. Rylands to announce the com- mencement of the sale. Miss B. Dutton, hon. treasurer, described the dev6ted and very use- ful work of the Missiod and thanked Miss M. G. Rylands for this additional proof of her warm friendship to the Mission. The proceeds of the gale, which included many kind Money 'donations from friends unable to be present, amounted to the satisfactory sum of £l4 16s.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-09-23_WidnesExaminer_art0051" n="art0051">
        <head>EXAMINER, SATURDAY, SEPTEMIER 23, 1916.</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER, SATURDAY, SEPTEMIER 23, 1916.</p>
        <p>A HOLIDAY IN IRELAND.</p>
        <p>Some Notes On War-Time Lights And Shadows.</p>
        <p>LIGHT RAILWAYS NEEDED.</p>
        <p>By C. E. PARKER.</p>
        <p>It might be thought that in choosing 'the shores of Sheepshaven in the north of Ireland as the scene of ouf family holiday we might get out of the shadow of the war. We soon realised that this; could not be even in this remote part of stony Donegal. It is true Ire- land has no conscription, and no doubt there are many who ought to be at the Front who ,remain at home. But there are others, Protest- ants and Catholics, Nationalists and anti-Rome Rulers, who have flocked to the Colours. It seemed impossible to find a single hamlet that had not sent its representatives to the great war, and in Many a humble Donegal cottage, with its earthen floor_ and dent thatched roof, there were anxious .mothers eager to get the opinion of an Englishman as to when the war would end, while not a few were mourning for their sons, and would not be comforted because they were not. The shadow of the war was apparent in many,of the industries. The little herring, fleets were depleted; some of the fishers had gone' as soldiers, others as mine sweepers, while the GOveinment-provided' boats were called away on more important work. Generally speaking, the war has brought pros- ;)erity to the agriculturists in Ireland, and the localities within reach of Tuarkets or railway connections are doing well. In most of Donegal the little fanners are so far from a market that they have not shared proportionately in the general prosperity. These are to a large extent, self-dependent, growing what they require for their own use, and only occasionally ha,ing ‘ur- plus stock to market. Still even these are ex- periencing the stimulus occasioned by the great ~y3rld struggle.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-09-23_WidnesExaminer_art0052" n="art0052">
        <head>flax G,-owing.</head>
        <p>flax G,-owing.</p>
        <p>Many farmers in Donegal have, for the first time, grown flax, and this has proved a profit- able venture. The amount of flax grown in Ireland this year is probably seven .or eight times the normal quantity. &amp;fore the wal-eire- land grew about one-fifth of the flax Manufac- tured in the country, now nearly half is grown. The value since the war began has about trebled. and I believe has reahied .£l2O a ton. This is after, if has been 'cleaned .and scutched. We can still .get flax from Russia, but the Bel- gian .supply, about a- third of the whole, is en- tirely 'cut off. heard of one farmer who affirmed that ,he had made £7O clear from an acre of flax, but this is very exceptional.- The flax plant has a thin wiry stem branched to- wards the ton to early the pretty sky-blue flower which is the blossom of the bolled fruit which contains the linseed. The stalk grows about three feet in, height, and is somewhat stronger than wheat. The fibres run in strands between the skin and the woody pith forming the hollow tem. 'Flax, when ripe, is not cut, but is pulled up by the roots, bound in sheaves and nut in some adjacent water to 'steep. The object of this steeping is to dissolve by fermentation the glutinous matter adhering to the fibres.' At the end of a fortnight it is taken out and dried in the fields. It is then bruised further to dis- entangle the fibre, then sent to the flax mills to he scutched and spun. The damp climate of Treland is specially adapted both to the growth and the spinning of flax, but the plant takes -nuch out of the ground, and cannot be grown on the same land in successive years. The rota- tion is potatoes, corn, flax. The Harvest of the Shore.</p>
        <p>The making of kelp from seaveed is an indus- try followed all along the northern coast of Ireland. The seaweed is cut and gathered at low tide and brought up to dry by carts or by the help bf donkeys with panniers suspended over the back. It is then spread on the grass or</p>
        <p>-rol to dry, and when dry burnt to -an ash. This ash is bagged and conveyed to the nearest means of transit to Glasgow or London, where_ iodine and .other products are extracted. It is also ak valuable manure, and is now in. demand for the hungry flax crops. The price the farmers of Donegal were getting before the war for kelp ash was .23 10s. per ton on rail; it is now at least .26 ss. All the small farmers along the sea coast turn their .attention to kelp when not busy with their land crops. Seaweed is also largely used direct as manure for crops.</p>
        <p>Peat Ireland's Fuel.</p>
        <p>, The getting in during the summer of the winter's supply of fuel—that is, turf or peat— engages a good deal of the energy and time of the Irish cottagerS. There are many parts of the country in which no coal is ever used.. Peat is. the only fuel, and often has to be hauled several miles. I have seen donkeys with their panniers full of peat on the coast of Donegal from the base of the hills, at an elevation of 1,500 feet, coming down. the bridle paths to the cottages near where I stood. I do not suppose that the weight of peat. carried by each donkey could be more than 701 b., this would be equal at best to 351 b. of coal, and it would probably take two or three hours to do a single journey. To this time and lribonr must be added the cut- ting .and drying of the peat earlier in th eason. The depth of the peat beds varies considerably; in Donegal it is generally not more than two or three feet, and when this has been cut there is often, nothing but rock below. In. such cases the pasturage is spoilt, and this is a serious pro- blem. Not only is the peat exhausted and ceases to grow, but the grazing area is diminished. Xhother result of cutting out the'peat, where the land is low lying, is that it is often, cut below the drainage level, in consequence the ground becomes waterlogged and rendered' unfit for crops. Thousands of acres are, being rendered' useless in Ireland fro nl this cause, and such low. eating ought to be made illegal. Generally, the right of cutting :tail on the .11.111 s goes with the holding; or a small charge is made/but in such cases turf can only be cut for the use of the teuant, and must not be Marketed. •</p>
        <p>Other Uses For Peat.</p>
        <p>Many efforts have been made to utilise peat for *her purposes than 'household -fires. Much motley has been spent and much lost in Ireland in experiments. While there have been many failures, some success has been achieved. Air- dried peat yields a capital power gas, and fac- tories are being run entirely With it; much to the owners' advantage while coal is so dear. No, doubt. much more could be done in this direction, but the factory must not be top far from the supply, as the bulky character Of the peat makes transit expensive. A further de- velopment of light railways would bring Much more of this fuel into use. Peat is now largely used for litter for horses and ourimports for this purpose from the. horses, and Ger- many before the war amounted to about 100,000 tons annually, a good deal of this went to Ire- land. This has now stopped to Ireland's bene- fit. A considerable quantity of peat dust is used in the making of nuilassine meal for cattle food. also for packing eggs and fruit. Sphagnum moss from peat. bogs is now extensively used in hospitals for dressing wounds, and volunteer parties are formed and go. out 'Pi Ith sacks to gather this moss. - The moss has then to be carefullY•picked over and all the hard substance taktcn out.. A use has lately been. found' for peat by rLi..ofessOr Bottomley. By inoculating peat with nitrogen-fixing bacteria he has produced a valuable fertiliser, and the Manchester Cot- poration has been granted the free use of his .proeess for a period. I have tried this 'bacterialged peat in my garden as a fertiliser for tomatoes; the result .has been an increased yield of 40 per cent. against a well-knoWn fer- tiliser. In this there is a great future for peat dust if the cost of manufacture can .be reduced. In Donegal the spinning-wheel and hand- loom are still in use for worsted and the fabrics made from it. I visited a cottage and found an old lady about eighty sitting by the peat fire busy spinning, and it was wonderful to see how' her aged .fingers manipulated the raw wool into an even thread by the help of her wheel: Three strands are then twisted together and become an even thread of three- ply, and from this the stockings are knit, and the famous Donegal tweeds, with their pungc.nt smell of peat reek, are Made:</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-09-23_WidnesExaminer_art0053" n="art0053">
        <head>Death of Miss Milner.</head>
        <p>Death of Miss Milner.</p>
        <p>We record with regret the death of Miss Milner at her residence, Moore House, on Sunday night. An extensive landowner in Cheshire, she was well-known for her philan- thropy. Born in 1831, at Moore House, she resided there all her life, and so completed a 200 years' occupatiop of the house by her family. The 'funeral took place at Daresbury on Thursday morning.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-09-23_WidnesExaminer_art0054" n="art0054">
        <head>COMFORTS NEEDED!</head>
        <p>COMFORTS NEEDED!</p>
        <p>Prepare For Winter Campaign In France And The East.</p>
        <p>VOLUNTARY WORKERS' URGENT APPEAL FOR £3OO.</p>
        <p>It is now certain that our gallant troops will have to face the Adiscomforts of the trenches for another winter, and it is clearly the duty of those who will be enjoying the luxury of home life to see that those discomforts .are minimised as much as possible. Fortunately, there are many willing workers to make the much-needed woollen helmets, scarves and socks and similar articles; all they want to set them going to keep up the supplies is the money to buy the material. For this money the following appeal has been made:—</p>
        <p>Comforts for our troops at the Front—France, Mesopotamia, Egypt and Salonica—and for our wouncled soldiers at home and abroad.</p>
        <p>THE WINTER CAMPAIGN:</p>
        <p>Dear Sir, or Madam, Since the outbreak of war, the women of Warrington have sent out many scores of thousands of pairs of mits, socks, shirts and other necessary and useful articles, largely for the men of our Territorial Battalions.</p>
        <p>In December, 1915, the Governinent decided— in order to prevent overlapping, and waste—to place the direction of the distribution of com- forts in the • hands of Sir Edward Ward, as Director-General, and at whose request. a local branch of the National Committee was formed here. For this purpose the members of the ,Territorial Equipment Committee, the War- rington Women's Liberal Association, and the Cairo-street and Moore Working Parties and Miss White's Central Committee have amal- gamated and have dispatched from time. to time on the requisitions of the Director-General large quantities of comforts for the Army generally, but with the proviso that the claims and needs of our local battalions should have first place. Over 500 ladies have been helping in this great work for the past nine months, and are most anxious to continue doing so. At this moment many thousands of mits, socks, hospital shirts, slippers,. operation socks and other articles are urgently needed, and part of the work is in hand.</p>
        <p>Our funds are almost exhausted ; we depend entirely upon voluntary help, and we cannot comply with the claims already made upon us without immediate and substantial financial support.</p>
        <p>In all the fields of wkir our fellow townsmen are fighting, and when we:remember •the awful disipmfort and danger which has hitherto been inellitable in trench warfare in winter—frost- bite, rheumatism, chest troubles, trench fever -and; the like—there is not one of us who is not prepared to, do his or her best to mitigate to the utmost possible extent, the horrors and suffer- jugs which our heroic soldiers are facing so ' cheerfully. Winter, is rapidly approaching! Time -is short! The claims of our troops are urgent! Three hundred pounds is needed forthwith and more later! Please help!</p>
        <p>Subscriptions may be sent to the Mayor (Alderman P. Peacock) president, to the Hon. Treasurer, Mr. Jno. Fairhurst, or to any of the undersigned. JAS. H. SMETHURST, Chairman, HENRY COLLIN, Hon. Sec., MISS WHITE, Holly House. Sankr-street.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-09-23_WidnesExaminer_art0055" n="art0055">
        <head>LATE DR. ADATOS.</head>
        <p>THE LATE DR. ADATOS.</p>
        <p>mpressive Funeral At Lytnm On Tuesday.</p>
        <p>A fortnight to the hour since he stood with troubled mein and uncovered head and watched- the body of his beloved wife lowered into a grave in the peaceful slope of Lymna Churchyard, all that was mortal of •Dr. Adams himself was laid to rest therein. on Tuesday afternoon.</p>
        <p>It was an impressive funeral. An autumnal day without the faintest ray of sunshine, the tendency to humidity in the atmosphere com- pleted the melancholy aspect, and it was with depressed. hearts that mourners and friends followed the bier into the church, where the Ilev. E. C. E. Carleton (vicar of Holy Trinity Church, Warrington) read the service before leading the procession to the graveside. The congregation was almost identical With that which assembled a fortnight before; his wife's friends were his friends. .There were many doctors, public and business gentlemen, and soldiers, and the general friends included scores of people from Warrington, particularly in. the neighbourhood of his residence, who attended to pay their tribute of esteem. Feelings of ,poignant grief were visible every- where.</p>
        <p>"Weeping at the feet and head, I can see your falling tears, I' can hear your sighs and prayers."</p>
        <p>Standing with Mr. Harvey Adams (son) as principal .mpurners were Mr. Spencer Adams (brother), Mr. J. Rowley Orr (cousin) and Mr. T. A. Mosse. Among .others present were •Doctors Langdale, Bowden, Binns, Muiray and Peacocke; Rev. B. Percival, .chaplain of the "Gilbert' Greenall " Lodge (1,250) of Free- masons; Mr. A. V. Baxter (representing the directors of Messrs. Joseph Crosfield and Sons, Ltd.), loieutenant-Colonel George R. Crosfield, D. 5.0., Lieutenant-Colonel A. b. Layton, Major Egerton Fairclough, CaptaV Stott, Sergeant Stephens, Mr. 'William Step ens (a servant of the late Dr. Adams for over'3o years), Mr. and Mrs. Joscph, WhitehOpse, Mr. and Mrs. W. Stephens and Mr. JosePh•Gerrard.</p>
        <p>The i4inCoin. Rural District -Council was represented by Councillors William MeTntyro, .T. Guest, J. Blythe, J. Hewitt., W. E. lidtigh (clerk), Stanley Wright (surveyor), J. Swinton (highway surveyor) and G. W. Lloyd .(inspector of nuisances).</p>
        <p>Mi. H. Flood, secretary, and Councillor G. Dale, treasurer, represented Crosfield's Ambu- lance Brigade,, and Mrs. Fisher, the Warrington Nursing Division. Beautiful wreaths were received from the following :—" Gladys-, Doris 'and Harvey," Col. and Mrs. R. Jackson, Mr. Spencer Adams, " Nettie and Minnie," Col. and Mrs..R.'Batley, Mrs. Balmain and Mrs. Ivan Hamilton, Mr. G. P. Simpson, Col. and Mrs. Harman and "William and Sarah," "Laura and Mary," Mr. T. A. Mosse, Mr. and Mrs. G., H. K. Kingdon, Officers of the 4th Battalion Scluth Lancashire Regiment, Dr. and Mrs. Peacocke,- .Dr. and Mrs. Langdale, Dr. and , Mrs. Fox, Lieutenant-ColOnel B. Fairclough and Mrs. Fairclongh, Councillor and Mrs. Roberts,. Lieutenant-Colonel .George R. Crosfield, D. 5.0., Mr.- and Mrs. A. V. Baxter, Mr. and Mrs. R. Bowman and " Alatal;" Mr. • and Mrs. P.. J. Lord, Mr., and Mrs. 0. Wheeler, Mr. and Mrs. 'Ellison, Miss M. Ellison, Mr. and , Mrs. Law- ton and Air. H. E. Ti..l4w6n, Mrs. and Miss Holloway and, Lieutenant Holloway, the British Red Cross Society Waresbury, Division, -Cheshire Branch), Mr. and Mrs. Fred Parson- age ,and family, Mrs. Chalmers. Mr., 'Mrs. and Miss Bradbury, Mr.. and Mrs. • Whitehouse. and family, " The patients of, Beu:sey," The Neighbours of Bewsey," Mrs. Thomas, Mr. J. Gerrard,. Messrs, J. Cro field and Sons, Ltd:: the directors, managers and staff of Messrs. J. Crosfiep and Sons, Ltd.,. the Crosfield Division of the St. John Ambulanc4 'Ass)ociation, Mr. and Mrs. Higgins and family, Nursing Divi- sion; Warrington Corps; Mr, and Mrs. Simp- son and family, members •Of the ' Panel Bri- pade, Medical Association, and Mr.' and Mrs. Burrows. .</p>
        <p>111 r. R. Johnson had Charge of the funeral arrangements.</p>
        <p>Gifts To Schools.</p>
        <p>Expressions ,of gratitude were made at meeting of the Education Committee on Mon- day regarding the following gifts :—Encyclo- Pmdia Britannica from the Rev. Canon Morley Stevenson to the Secondary School, bound col- unis of the "Studio' from, Mr F. Monks to the School of Art, and a framed portrait of John Travers Cornwell, the naval ,boy hero (with specially prepared text matter) from the Chairman (Councillor H. Roberts) to each senior school.—Alderman Bennett declared that' the. ,Chairman .had _done a very *lee and patriotic thing in endeavouring to bring home to the boys and girls in the schools the courage displayed by John Travers Cornwell. • _ . . _ .</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-09-23_WidnesExaminer_art0056" n="art0056">
        <head>CAPT. WHITTLE SAFE! DJ, y • al nititwure</head>
        <p>CAPT. WHITTLE SAFE! DJ, y • al nititwure</p>
        <p>Lights for Coming Week. Light up Obscure Vehicles. Lights. To-day (Saturday) 7.41 ... 8.41 Sunday Monday t Tuesday Wednesday 7.32 ... 8.32 Thursday Friday 7.27 ... 8.27 Saturday End of Summer Time. Sunday, Oct. 1 6.23 ... 7.23 Elmwood Avenue Church.</p>
        <p>The church anniversary services in connec- tion with Elmwood-avenue Congregational Church ariz, to be held to-morrow (Sunday). The appointed preachers are: Rev. C. P. Pitt, morn- ing, and Rev. T. W. Bond, St. Helens, evening.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-09-23_WidnesExaminer_art0057" n="art0057">
        <head>Town Clerk Receives News Of His Son.</head>
        <p>Town Clerk Receives News Of His Son.</p>
        <p>PRISONER IN PRUSSIA.</p>
        <p>-Yesterday the Town Clerk received a tele- gram from Messrs. Cox and Co., Army bankers, to the effect that "Geneva Gent" telegraphs :</p>
        <p>Captain 0. Lyon Whittle, Flying Corps, prisoner of war at Guterslohe, Germany.</p>
        <p>Guterslohe (16 Ce) is a small town in West- phalia, about 39 miles south of Minden (16 Cd).</p>
        <p>Capt. Whittle was transferred from the South Lancashire Regiment to the Royal Flying Corps, along with his companion, Capt. A. Skinner, on August 19. The two promptly got to work, for on August 31 they took part in a bombing raid in hostile territory. They failed to return, and the greatest anxiety' has been felt for them since. Yesterday's message will be received with universal pleasure, and the hope that similar good tidings may come to hand regard- .ing Capt. Skinner. Fp to lagt night nothing had been heard of ttat gentleman.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-09-23_WidnesExaminer_art0058" n="art0058">
        <head>OFFICIAL CONFIRMATION.</head>
        <p>OFFICIAL CONFIRMATION.</p>
        <p>Mr. J. Lyon Whittle received late last night a telegram from the War -Office notifying that Captain 0. Lyon Whittle, "previously reported missing, now officially reported prisoner .of war at Guterslohe."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-09-23_WidnesExaminer_art0059" n="art0059">
        <head>FROM CAVALRY TO iNFANTRY,</head>
        <p>FROM CAVALRY TO iNFANTRY,</p>
        <p>Stationmaster's Son Promoted</p>
        <p>To Second-Lieutenancy.</p>
        <p>His many friends will be .pleased to hear that Mr. .Charles McDonald Armstrong, stepson of Mr. Gordon, station master at Bank Quay, and Mrs. Gordon,,, 46, Arpley-street, has been gazetted a , Second-Lieutenant in the King's Own yorkshire. Light Infantry. Second-Lieutenant Armstrong, who is twenty- .</p>
        <p>SECOND-LIEUTENANT CHAS. M,.DONALD ARMSTRONG.</p>
        <p>six years of age, has been in the Army for eight years. He enlisted in the 16th -Laric:_.rs, and transferred to the 17th Battalion, along with his step-brother, Edward A. Gordon, and they went to India together. They were drafted from India to France in November, 1914, and have served there since. His step-brother is at pre- sent sick in hospital. Second-Lieutenant Armstrong has only had three days' leave dur- ing his whole Army career. He was educated at Wycliffe School, and attended the Sunday school and church. Before enlisting he was employed in the offices of Messrs. Greenall's dis- tillery, to which place he went from the offices of Messrs. Joseph Crosfield and Sons, Ltd.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-09-23_WidnesExaminer_art0060" n="art0060">
        <head>Warrington Market _Prices.</head>
        <p>Warrington Market _Prices.</p>
        <p>At Wednesdays Warrington Market the price of new wheat ruled from Bs. 3d. to Bs. 6d. per 701 b., and 4s. to 4s. 3d. per 451 b. was realised for new oats. Flour realised 475. 6d. to 525. 6d. per 2401 b., thirds 2s. 4d. to 2s. 7d. per 201 b; bran, is. 7d. to is. 9d. per 201 b.; wheat straw, 70s. per ton.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-09-23_WidnesExaminer_art0061" n="art0061">
        <head>Judge Sett Fox Complimented._</head>
        <p>Judge Sett Fox Complimented._</p>
        <p>His Honour County Court Juage J. Scott Fox, K.C., has been promoted to the important Leeds Circuit. At the conclusion of the County Court business on Thursday, Mr. Louis Kelly, barrister, on behalf of the members of the Bar and legal profession, offered to his Honour.their respectful congratulations. Judge Fox expressed his thanks for'the compliment. •</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-09-23_WidnesExaminer_art0062" n="art0062">
        <head>Infretious Disease.</head>
        <p>Infretious Disease.</p>
        <p>Last week the following new cases of infec- tious disease in Warrington were reported : Diphtheria, 2; erysipelas, 3; measles, 3; puer- peral fever, 1; tuberculosis, 6. It will be noted that there were no new cases of scarlet fever. There remained in the hospital the following local cases :—Scarlet fever, 'l.e; enteric fever, V; diphtheria, 7; phthisis (Sankey Hospital), 17; total, 37. Serious Street Accident.</p>
        <p>A serious accident occurred in Winwick-road on Monday, when Mrs. -Martha Hankinson, 22, West-street, was knocked , down and rim over by a waggonette. Two privates of . the R,A.M.C.. from; the Lord Derby 'War Hospital took the injured woman to the Infirmary, where it was ascertained that both her legs were fractured. Mrs. .Hankinson, who is 58 years of age, is further prostrated from shock and lies in a very critical state.</p>
        <p>New School Mahallers. The Rev. B. Schofield has been appointed foundation manager and correspondent of the Ladies' School of Industry. Miss Ferriman wrote to the Education Committee on Monday regretting that, as she had retired ' from her appointment at the Training College and was leaving Warrington, it became necessary for her to resign her position as a representative manager of the Smith-street School.—The Chairman (Councillor 11. Roberts) .said that Miss Ferriman's long experience in educational matters had made her an exceptionally able and valuable member, and he moved that the committee place on record their appreciation •of her services.—This was • seconded by Alder- man Smethurst and carried.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-09-23_WidnesExaminer_art0063" n="art0063">
        <head>Motor Cars Collide.</head>
        <p>Motor Cars Collide.</p>
        <p>When Private Thomas Pettit, Mechanical Transport, Aril3y Service Corps, was driving a motoi car belonging to the Ministry of Muni- tions ..ip Bold-street and in the direction of Sankey-street on Monday afternoon, he collided with another motor car driven and owned by Mr. Henry Charles Sutton, 14, Upingham-road West Derby, Liverpool. Mr. Sutton's cir was cominit along Palmyra-square to Suez-street, and the impact of the two vehicles was very violent. The civilian's Car .was knocked over on the footpath at Mr. Forshaw's office, and Mr. Sutton was slightly bruised about the chest. Pettit was uninjured. Both cars were damaged in the collision, and were 'subsequently removed to the Lion Hotel Garage.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-09-23_WidnesExaminer_art0064" n="art0064">
        <head>Parish Church Musical Recital.</head>
        <p>Parish Church Musical Recital.</p>
        <p>A recital of sacred music in aid of the Lord Kitchener Memorial Fund for disabled officers and men was given in Warrington Parish Church on Sunday afternoon. The attendance was a fair one. The choir sang the chorus "Be not afraid," from Mendelssohn's "Elijah." Beethoven's Hallelujah Chorus, and the anthem " 0 gladsome light." The follow- ing sang solos: Master Neville Davies, "Hear ye Israel," Mendelssohn ; Mr. J. B. Poole, "And God said" and "Now heaven in fullest glory," Haydn; Mr. J.. Sephton, "Be thou faithful," Mendelssohn ; and Mr. Crowe, "He was despised," Handel. "Overture," Handel; Cantilene," Salome; and "0 Sanctissima," Lux, were organ solos\ played by Mr. F. M. Darby, the organist.</p>
        <p>A Patriotic Movement. In consideration of the prevailing depletion of clerical staffs, the centrally situated Copying and Typewriting Bureau at Wood's College, 15, Bridge-street, has placed its resources and staff of expert typists at the service of patriotic firms in this district free of charge "for the duration of war." When .the "rush comes" and extra clerical help is needed, you• should 'call with your requirements at Wood's College. Provid- ing the stationery is supplied, the Bureau will undertake your envelope addressing, circulars, form letters, postcards, specifications, duplicat- ing, MSS., &amp;c. Strict privacy and speedy despatch. guaranteed. Anyone' engaged in any branch of philanthropic and national work is especially invited to place 'work to be typed there. -</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-09-23_WidnesExaminer_art0065" n="art0065">
        <head>Thank God 1</head>
        <p>Thank God 1</p>
        <p>Sir Gilbert Greenall, accompanied by Masters Gilbert and Edward Greenall, attended a matinee last Saturday given in the Chester Picturedrome in aid of the Allied Farmers' Relief Fund. In thanking the management of the Picturedrome, Sir Gilbert explained that the Royal-Agricultural Society collected £70,000 for helping the farmers who had suffered during the Franco-German war. . That sum enabled them to 'do the Fretfch farmers a Food deal of good; but the present needs with Servia, Bel- gium, Poland and Northern France devastated would be very much greater. Some said that an appeal made now was premature, but they must be ready with the funds at any moment if they were to do any good. As a matter of fact a great deal had already been done in the parts of France from which the enemy had been cleared; already ' a good amount of breeding stock had been sent and a lot of seeds „and agri- cultural implements, but after the war there would be an enormous amount of that work to be done, and they must have available fUnds„ "Thank God," concluded Sir Gilbert, "the war has not taken place in our land, and that our farms and homesteads and buildings have not all been devastated .as those poor people's were." The proceeds amounted to over £5O.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-09-23_WidnesExaminer_art0066" n="art0066">
        <head>Death of Mr. John Alban Tivnin.</head>
        <p>Death of Mr. John Alban Tivnin.</p>
        <p>The death occurred on Saturday', September 9th, of Mr. John Alban Tivnin, who was serving at .Cardiff as a! private in the Royal Defence Corps. Mr. Tivmn,--who 'resided at 54, Cock- hedge-lane, has had a life-long. connection, as also had his father, with the Vir.hitecross- Wire Works. He entered the employ of the company at the age of eleven years and served them as a wire cleaner until he joined the Army fifteen months ago. His death, which came suddenly was the result of hemorrhage of. the brain. The body was conveyed to 'Warrington, under the care of Sergeant Connor, who was also present at the funeral. The interment took place last Saturday at the Cemetery and a large number of friends and neighbours assembled in token !of respect. Bearers and buglers from the Bar- racks attended, as did also a firing party from Winwick. Father Willon performed the last rites and the members of the St. Mary's Young Men's Society met the Cortege at the Cemetery. The Mourners present were Mrs Tivnin (widow), Miss Sarah Tivnin and Miss Ellen Tivnin (daughters), Master John Tivnin and Master Jamee, Tivnin (sons), Mrs. Riley, Mrs. Murray and Mrs. Malam (sisters-in-law), Miss Lily Turner (niece) and Miss S. Lawless. Wreaths and floral tributes were sent by the officers of the Royal Defence Corps, the N.C.O.'s and men of the South Lancashire Regiment, Cardiff; "His Wife and family,' Mr. and Mrs. Riley and family, Mr. and Mrs. Warburton and family, "His sisters-in-law, Lily and Polly Tivnin."</p>
        <p>Messrs. J. kounfield. and Sons carried out/the funeral arrangements.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-09-23_WidnesExaminer_art0067" n="art0067">
        <head>SGT.-MAJOR A. E. FAULKS.</head>
        <p>SGT.-MAJOR A. E. FAULKS.</p>
        <p>Military Medallist Captured By</p>
        <p>The Huns.</p>
        <p>A local Military Medallist in the person of Co.-Sergt.-Major. Albert E. Faulks, of the South Staffs. Regiment, is a prisoner of war in Ger- many. 'His wife, who reside % at 16, Brookland? street, off Padgate-lane; received on Wednesday a card from him which stated: "I am a prisoner of war, and stationed at Dulmen (Westf)." This message was an assurance for Mrs. Faulks, who last Saturday received a leftyr saying that her husband was reported misiing. The message, dated 'September 7th, and signed Chief Q.-M.-Sergeant nriridley, stated in part, "We were both members of the South Staffs, and went through the -South African • 'campaign together. Whatever duty your husband had to perform he performed it</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-09-23_WidnesExaminer_art0068" n="art0068">
        <head>CO.-SERGT.-MAJOR A. E. FAULKS.</head>
        <p>CO.-SERGT.-MAJOR A. E. FAULKS.</p>
        <p>most cheerfully and to the best of his ability. He was a most excellent leader of men, for which ,ho had already been decorated. The officer,e, N.C.O.'s and men will no doubt miss Ihi ni very much." , CoMpany-Sergeant-Major 'Faulks, who is thirty-one years of age, is the scin of Mr. John Faulks, 201, Padgate-lane, who served in the South Staffs. 'Regiment for 29 .years,, during. which time he fought in the Zulu War and the first ,South African campaign, obtaining the meritorious medal. He acted for some years as lassistant paymaster at Orford Barracks, and !retired With the rank of Quartermaster-Ser- geant. Company-Sergeant-Major Faulk's grand- father served as a Quartermaster-Sergeant throughout the Crimean War, and was in charge, of the base hospital in which Florenot Nightingale worked. • 1 Company-Sergeant-Major Faulks went out with the original British Expeditionary Fgree, and fought in almost all the, big battles. He was wounded on the :14th September, 1914, and after spending some time in. hospital, was at home until Christmas. He brought with him 4 shrapnel bullet (extracted from his leg) and a, pair of powerful German field glat;ses, which two souvenirs he now treasures with just pride. He was educated at the Padgate C.E. School</p>
