      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-09-23_WidnesExaminer_art0069" n="art0069">
        <head>LADY MIME</head>
        <p>LADY MIME</p>
        <p>To Receive Warrington's Big</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-09-23_WidnesExaminer_art0070" n="art0070">
        <head>Cheque.</head>
        <p>Cheque.</p>
        <p>A VISIT NEXT FRIDAY.</p>
        <p>Warrington's big chgtiue—over two thousand guineas—representing the result of the great Navy Day effort on August 17th, will be pre- sented to Lady Jellieoe, wife of Admiral Sir John Jellicoe, Commander-in-Chief of the Grand Fleet, at a public gathering in the Parr Hall on Friday evening next. Lady Jellicoe is expected to arrive at about 6.30 o'clock and will be ivelcomed on arrival by his Worship the Mayor as representing the citizens. She will proceed to the Town Hall, where .her Ladyship will be entertained at dinner by the Mayor and Mayoress. Invitations have been sent out to 'a number of guests. The ceremony of handing over the cheque will take place, as already stated, in the Parr Hall, during the course of an entertainment to be arranged by members of the National Union of Teachers.</p>
        <p>Autumnal Convention Meetings. The Autumnal Convention in connection with the Independent Methodist Connexion will be held . Saturday, September 30th, at GrapPell: hall Chapel. The Convention commences ab 3 p.m., and at 6.15 Councillor Ilenshall will preSide over a public meeting.</p>
        <p>Summer Time Ending. "Summer time" will come to an end a week s to-day. The Act under which the nation approximated its hours of work and recreation more closely to those of daylight by putting on the clock an hour at two in the morning on May 21st direets that the clock shall be put back an hour at two in the morning (Green' , wich mean time; three ('clock, summer time) on October Ist. • - Entertaining Convalescent Soldiers. On Monday, by the kindness of Mr. Warbur- ton, thirty of the patients at Thelwall I{eYs Military Convalescent Home were entertained at the Royal Court Theatre matinee; and Afr• Jackson, the manager of the Ilippodrome, secured the services of a party of this week's artistes, who gave an excellent concert on Tiles" day in the Conservatory of Thelwall Heirs' Mrs. Frost desires us to thank all concerned.</p>
        <p>Harvest Services at Bold Street Church. - At Bold-street Wesleyan Church the harvest festival services are to be held to-morrow (Su11; day). In the morning the Rev. F. Hudson, foil in the evening the Rev. Percy meson wt.; preach.- The Pastor's anniversary is to be hew on Thursday, a service commencing at 4 p.•' and at 7.30. p.m. a public meeting will also be held, when the speaker will be the Rev. T. Turney (Liverpool).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-09-23_WidnesExaminer_art0071" n="art0071">
        <head>Wycliffe P.S.A.</head>
        <p>Wycliffe P.S.A.</p>
        <p>The Rev. •T. T. James presided at SundaY:! meeting of the Wycliffe P.S.A., and Mr. Drevri";, gave an address upon "Personal IdentitY: Mr. J. H. Draper sang "Waft her, angels, t° the skies" and "A dream divine," these being greatly appreciated. Mr. W. H. Payton Pre" sided at the organ. Mr. McEachern also took part in the meeting. The Secretary men; tioned that Drummer T. Turner, in India, an', Mr. Yeates, in Macedonia, sent their Iles' wishes to the brethren.</p>
        <p>Warrington Sunday School Union. Special meetings under the auspices of the, Warrington Sunday School Union are to be hel.n, to-day and to-morrow, when a visit will be Pill(; by the Rev. W. Melville Harris, secretary 01 the Young People's Department of the congre- gational Union. To-day the meeting is in the Wycliffe, Memorial. Hall at 3.30, to be foll°wed by tea at five, after which a tea-table confer- ence will take place. At 7 o'clock a Public gathering will be held. Teachers workers among the young are cordially invited. To-morrow, in Bold-street Wesleyan Chapel, a united scholars' service will be held at 2.30, tile speaker being Alderman Townend, of Middleton.</p>
        <p>Sick Nursing Classes. The Education -Committee advertise a &amp;es, for sick nursing to be held in the TechnicaL Institute on Wednesday evenings, to prepare women candidates for the British Red Cross Society's Certificate and an exN....4natlon reoog; nised by the Army Council. There is a ilemao!; for this class of instruction, and we are gla," the authorities have taken adequate steps 1° be satisfy the demand. The syllabus can. obtained from the Director of Education' Schools for women for dressmaking, cooker and millinery are also dealt with in the Pres- pectus.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-09-23_WidnesExaminer_art0072" n="art0072">
        <head>Compensation for Loss of Hand.</head>
        <p>Compensation for Loss of Hand.</p>
        <p>It was reported by the Clerk (Mr. A. Bottonr ley) at a meeting of the Board of Guardians' on Wednesday, that a woman who had been engaged as a "scrubber" at the Workhoul had met with an accident while so engaged an had had to have a hand amputated. Her corr Pensation money amounted to ss. Id. per wee's' and, as she had four children under work'Pg age, the Guardians were paying her 7s. 6el-,,13' week for a period.—Mr. Flanagan endorsed tl proposal, which was carried, and he oxide, that he considered the were acting very ously tOwardS her. "</p>
        <p>Compulsory Early Closing of §lioPs• The Government has deCided, I am' told, -532;5 the London correspondent of the " Mancheslll Guardian," to require the early closing of ' shops, during the coming winter. Subject 1" the existing half-holiday arrangement, the Pre- scribed • hours of closing are to be MondaYs.: Tuesdays and Wednesdays 7 p.m., Thursde' n and Fridays 8, and Saturdays 9. The presello , intention is that the Order shall come 111 t,, operation on the first business day after return to Greenwich time—that is, Metillq,.; October 2nd. This deeision has been taken 1 consequence of the failure of efforts made 9 commercial organisations in different parts, ee the country to secure earlier evening closl? by voluntary arrangement. The primary 01-1„"e of the move is, of course, the new lighting reg,"; lotions, which are to be much more strict" enforced next winter than they were last.</p>
        <p>Pearson and Knowles' Postponed Meeting. the The annual report of the directors 0f Pearson and Knowles Coal and Iron Co. P'--t been issued, but the closing of the balance sbeet,, is deferred pending certain financial aclitt ments. The annual meeting called for Tuesday will be concerned only with formal resolutions. and 'will be adiollr'-nt Dividends and bonuses to be declared represe" a total of 15 per cent. for the whole year. 'ir It is with feeling's of deep ,synipathy for t Peep relatives and of pride in tho patriotism of the ra r —in which they are confident the shareholdev,. will concur—the directors record that 252 Mee, formerly conneeted with the works; have i_beo killed at the Front, many more have 'Ll'ho wounded in ,action, and the total number iv have joined the Imperial Forces is 3,512. 6. re Mr. W. Peter Rylands is the retiring di tor, and is eligible for re-election. ' •</p>
        <p>Rev. W. Hendy Cock's New 800 The Rev, W. Hendy Cock, late vice-pr P inekikli of Warrington Training College, has, thron"gi- , Mr. 4obert &amp;Ott, Paternoster Row, 8.C., Plind lished a learned little book entitled ((Love a-b Cruelty." The love is on the part of our din% animal friends, who know more' and feel 'll than we give them credit for, and the cruel.Y is on our part, • and, consists of the manner ?I' which we oft-times return abuse for that IoA et ; Mr.- Hendy Cock makes various animals tel their own stories of their wrongs, and ill, iisC)s doing reveal the claim, which he charele;,—a by inference, that they not' only have a standard of 'intelligence, but also that tPezi_ have hopes in connection with the world I'd' come. "It is a tale of cruelty, love Ile mysterious power based on facts," and in "Ai telling of it Mr. Heady Cock empleYs,_lnun-n- -knowledge relating to such matters as tue tiquity and origin of horses, dogs, eat:s :an- other animals that could only have been a; is able to such n keen student as himself. f a powerful plea for a kindlier treatment,_2e animals, and as such we commend it rtn respectful attention of our readers,</p>
        <p>lislied at 2s. net.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-09-23_WidnesExaminer_art0073" n="art0073">
        <head>EXAMINER, SATURDAY, SEPTEMBER 23, 1916.</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER, SATURDAY, SEPTEMBER 23, 1916.</p>
        <p>DIED FOR 1114EIR eOUNT*Y.</p>
        <p>I very much regret to tell you that Lance- Corporal G. Maddock was killed in action on August 27th, whilst digging a trench with a working party. „ When I heard he 'had been killed it was a Treat shock to me, as he was one of the most relia:Ae and best men in the company. You. must console yourself in the fact that your husband has given his life for his King and;country." Lance-Corporal- Maddock; • who was thirty years of age, and the father of three children, was formerly employed by the WhitecrosstCo., Ltd. He served for five years in the Terri- torials prior to the war, and re-enlisted- two years agu next Christmas, and had been at the Front' sixteen. Months. As ,a boy he attended t. BarnabatiVehool. He was a good boWler, won several prizes when vfar..:ig for the Whitecro4s team. Mrs. Maddock has a brother and brother-in-law in the Army; the former is at present suffering from a trench disease and the latter has ''.leen wounded in the foot.</p>
        <p>r 4 ,^-rN ;''t P. C. LEAP.</p>
        <p>Secr.,:c::z o.llcll While In A Trench.</p>
        <p>MN. Longshaw, 69, Catherine-street, has re- ceived news that her son, Sapper Paul Caldwell Lear„ of the Royal Engineers, has been killed in action. A Lieutenant wrote as follows: " He was killed by a shell while waiting in a trench to go up to his work. . . Sapper Lear always did his work -well." A letter from Sergeant Stevenson expressed the great regret of the section to which the Sapper was attached. The N.C.O. spoke highly of his cheerful disposition and good nature. Sapper Lear, who was twenty-four years ef age, .enlisted immediately upon the outbreak</p>
        <p>WA. an4llheen in France nine months. He was of a bright and sunny disposition, always. expressed himself as being " the pink," when writing home. He was educated at Heathside School, and be-</p>
