      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-09-23_WidnesExaminer_art0074" n="art0074">
        <head>Additional Local Casualties in the Various War Areas.</head>
        <p>Additional Local Casualties in the Various War Areas.</p>
        <p>LIEUTENANT WRIGHT. Killed. ,</p>
        <p>Arthur Wright, son of Mr. and Ars. D. W. Wright,B Winwick-street. 0 _ _ _</p>
        <p>A -letter from Captain E. D. Baskin was re- ceived on Tuesday morning. "It is with the deepest Sympathy," he said, "that I have to inform you that your son was killed this morn- ing. As I haVe just last my only brother, I can well understand what a heavy blow this will be, and I feel most deeoy for you in Your loss. We, too, shall !greatly, miss him. At two o'clock this morninc, he was on duty with some guns on an advanced point, when a large shell landed quite close to him and 'a big splinter went right •through his heart, killing him 'in- stantly." A letter has also been received from the deceased officer's servant, in which he expresses his great sorrow and pays Mr. Wright a splen- did tribute. .</p>
        <p>Second-Lieutenant Wright secured his conhmi- sion in March, 1915, and took up duties with the Royal Warwickshire Regiment. After pass- ing a test for Machine gunnery he became attached to the Machine Gun Corps two months ago, and then he crossed to France. He was scholar of brilliant attainments. After being educated first at Arpley-street School and later at the Boteler \ Grammar School, he went to Manchester University, taking his B.A. and M.A., degrees. He then entered the teaching profession. He first took up duties as clatQical master at Hutton Grammar School, near Pres- ton, subsequently becoming second master at Eggar's Grammar School, Alton, Hants. lie belonged to St. Paul's Bible ,Class and when at home attended St. Paul's Church.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-09-23_WidnesExaminer_art0075" n="art0075">
        <head>PRIVATE R. SMITH.</head>
        <p>PRIVATE R. SMITH.</p>
        <p>One Of Four Soldier-Brothers Who Was Only Eighteen.</p>
        <p>Private Robert Smith, son of Mrs. Smith, 1, Bennett-street, and of the Machine Gun Corps, 'was killed on September 2nd of this year., lie</p>
        <p>PRIVATE R. SMITH. Killed.</p>
        <p>enlisted in October of last year, and went to Vranco on July 6th of this year. . Private Smith, who was only in his eighteenth year, first belonged to the South Lancashire Regiment, but transferred to the Machine Gun Corps. He was educated at St.. Thomas' and St. James' School, and was formerly employed by the Vernon-street Tanning Co., Ltd. His brother, Rifleman Joseph Smith, has been wounded, as stated in another column, while two other brothers, Private Charles Smith: of the Grenadier Guards and Sergeant John Smith, of the Inniskilfing. Fusiliers, are still serving; •</p>
