      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-09-23_WidnesExaminer_art0076" n="art0076">
        <head>"Our Day" Approaches.</head>
        <p>"Our Day" Approaches.</p>
        <p>There is to he a local ;colebTation of --Our Day ", by the holding .of a Cafe Chantant in Parr Hall on October 19. "Our Day" will spread itself over the whole kingdom and the Dominions overseas; in fact, it Promises to be the most completely organised 'day" that the war has called forth. By the success of the world-wide effort it is hoped that the patronage—given for the lint time to an appeal of this kind—of the King,. who, as well as the Queen and Queen Alexandra, is a patron of the movement, will be thoroughly. justified. Our Day" last year brought £1,400,000 to the Red Cross. It is the "day" of everyone who Las the spirit and intuition tb feel for the men Who are cheerfully suffering, fighting, and falL log for 'those who remain behind;. of everyone, roan. or woman, who, from .whatever cause, is debarred from a direct share in the contest of the nations. For those reasons, and no less for a host of others, the organisers of "Our Day" holis that this year will surpass the record, not Inerely of last .year, but of all other days. It is for Britons, and the Britons beyond the seas, to ensure. that they are not disappointed. Already 33.600,000 flags linvp been ordered, and tnatlY are being sent to Malta, Gibraltar. Singa- Pore,;.St. Helena, and other outposts of Empire. Window cards, which are being. supplied, will, lit is hoped, be hung in the window of every "se WhPrp a soldier or sailor has gone to Serve the King, and thus help to keep alive the spirit of "Our Day."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-09-23_WidnesExaminer_art0077" n="art0077">
        <p>. '</p>
        <p>DIED OF WOUNDS.</p>
        <p>CORPORAL G. H. COX.</p>
        <p>Ex-Organist Fatally Wounded</p>
        <p>In The Chest.</p>
        <p>Mrs. Cox, of 33, Wellfield-street, has had the , Mournful tidings of the death of her son, Cor- poral Geo. Hughes Cox, Royal Welsh Fusiliers, conveyed to her through one of his comrades. Writing from France under date 13th Septem- ber, Lance-Corporal J. C. Roff, -Machine Gun Section, said: "Just a line to let you know that your son George, who was wounded on the 11th, is reported since to have died of wounds; and I wish to express to you the heartfelt sympathy of the machihe gunners in your sad loss. He was a pal of mine since be 'joined the South. Lancs. He has been with me nearly all the time he was in France in my team, and his loss is deeply felt.- Let me know what I must do with his letters, •&amp;c., as I am keeping them. He asked- me to write and let you know what had befallen him. He was a true and faithful soldier, and was .highly respected by all." A further letter was received by Mrs. Cox from a nursing sister in a base hospital, stating that her son had been received and died there. He had been struck in the chest.</p>
        <p>Under date 13th September, Second-Lieutenant H. A. Parry, machine gun officer, wrote to Mrs. Cox: "It is my most painful duty to inform you that your son, George Cox; has died of wounds. His fellow machine gunners and my- self were exceedingly sorry to hear , the news this morning, and we deplore the loss of such a smart and popular man. A short time be- fore he was wounded early on Sunday morning I spoke to Mm while he was doing his turn as sentry, and stayed with him for a few minutes. He seemed very—km on looking out for Ger- mans. Later on when I came back that way 3 saw him wounded. He was wounded in the stomach by a rifle grenade. He bore the pain Very bravely' indeed. We put him on a stretcher and he was taken to the first-aid post as soon as possible, and from there to the hos- pital. We did not think that his wound was so serious because he was so brave over it. I can only hope that you will receive some con-</p>
        <p>solation in the fact that he died through re- maining at his post under heavy rifle-grenade fire like a trim British soldier. I sympathise with you sincerely at having lost, such a fine son; he was a smart soldier and machine gunner, and very popular with everyone who knew him."</p>
        <p>Corporal Cox enlisted first of all in the South Lancashire Regiment on January 22nd of the present year, but was subsequently transferred with a number of others to the Royal Welsh Fusiliers. He was drafted to France in June, and was attached to the machine gun section. This capable young 'soldier was in his 21st year. He was a well-known footballer, and played for' Warrington Excelsior F.C., and wa.- a good all-round athlete. He was the secretary of, the Buckley-street -Methodist Free Church Mission, and organist of the church.</p>
