      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-09-23_WidnesExaminer_art0078" n="art0078">
        <head>MISSING.</head>
        <p>MISSING.</p>
        <p>CORPORAL H. B3SCOW.</p>
        <p>'Former Ryls.ld3's Wiredrawer Missing.</p>
        <p>Mr. and Mrs. Boscow, 22, Ashwood-avenue, would be glad of any news concerning their sort, Corporal Harold Boscow, .of the Rifle Brigade, who was reported missing aftei an engagement on Angust 25th. His parents last heard from him on the 24th August: Corporal Boscow, who is twenty-five years of age; enlisted in October, 1914; and 'went to the Front in the following November. He came home on leave in December, 1915, and after "e- -turning to the Front, was wounded •in Febru- ary. He again returned to the Front after a few months in England, and a few weeks ago entered the bombing section., He was educated at the National School, and was formerly employed as a wiredrawer by Messrs. Rylands Bros., Ltd. He wasln good bowler and billiard</p>
        <p>player, and, by his prowess in these directions, won several prizes. •</p>
        <p>SERGT. BRADBURY PROGRESSING.</p>
        <p>Wo are pleased to state that Sergeant Brad- bury, of the R.W.F., son of ,Mr. W. Bradbury, 168, Orford-lane, who was admitted into a 'base hospital, on September 3rd r—rterlng from severe abdominal wounds, is now in an English hospital progressing satisfactorily.</p>
