        <p>CORPORAL HAROLD BOSCOW, Missing.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-09-23_WidnesExaminer_art0079" n="art0079">
        <head>WOUNDED.</head>
        <p>WOUNDED.</p>
        <p>SERGEANT J: JACKSON.</p>
        <p>Has Had A Piece Of Scrap-Iron Taken From His Lee.</p>
        <p>Mr. and Mrs. J. Jackson, 205, Knutsford- road, have been informed that their son, Sergeant John Jackson, King's Royal Rifles, has been wounded. Writing home he says he has had a piece of scrap iron taken from his leg, and is now going on quite nicely. He is at present in hospital at Colshqw Hall, Cheshire.</p>
        <p>Sergeant Jackson, who is:. 24 years of age, enlisted in September, 1914, and has been in France eighteen months. He formerly worked for Messrs. T. LoCker and Sons, and was an old boy. of St. James' School, also being a chorister in St. James' Church Choir. His brother, Bombardier Percy Jackson, is at pre- sent stationed in Ireland.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-09-23_WidnesExaminer_art0080" n="art0080">
        <head>LANCE-CORPORAL E. BOOTH.</head>
        <p>LANCE-CORPORAL E. BOOTH.</p>
        <p>Fine Soldier Who Did Not Know What Fear 'as.</p>
        <p>Two letters have been the means of conveying to Mr. and Mrs. Joseph Booth, 22,. Rhodes- street, the .news of the wounding of their son, Lance-Corporal Edgar Booth, South Lancashire Regiment. The wounds are in the right shoulder, there being five altogether.</p>
        <p>The first letter from a comrade was as follows: "I am sorry to let you know that your son, Edgar, got hit this morning, September 9th. He was one of my best 'friends, both out here and at home. You must not upset your- self, because I believe he is all right, and I dare say he will be removed to England. He was one of the old brigade, who came out here in 1915.".</p>
        <p>The second letter, from Corporal L. Gough, ran: "It would not surprise me to hear of him being in England before this letter reaches you. We were great pals, as you know, and there is no doubt about it, he was one of the finest soldiers out here, and did not know what fear was. My one regret is that I was not on the spot when he was hit, !7o that I could give you more details. I can assure you he will be sorely missed 'both by me and his comradvs, as he is well known for the good work he did when a stretcher-hearer, as he always seemed to be ready for duty at all times."</p>
        <p>Lance-Corporal Booth was brought to England and now lies progressing , well 4fl hospital at Southport, where he was visited by his parents on Monday. When wounded he was blown into a shell hole.</p>
        <p>Lance-Corporal Booth, who is 20 years of age, enlisted on the outbreak of war, and went to France in February, 1R13.. He formerly worked for Messrs. Peter Walker and Son, Ltd., is an old boy of Hamilton-street. School, and attended</p>
        <p>Brick-street Mission. He was interested in football, and was a great admirer of music, he, himself playing a clarionette in the South Lancs. Band.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-09-23_WidnesExaminer_art0081" n="art0081">
        <head>Shrapnel Wounds Lead To Amputation Of Leg.</head>
        <p>Shrapnel Wounds Lead To Amputa- tion Of Leg.</p>
        <p>We understand that Lance-Corporal T. Alcock, of the South Lancashire Regiment, who has had one, of his legs amputated owing to severe shrapnel wounds, is progressing as favourably as can be expected. News of the operation having been performed in a hospital in Kent was received last week at his home, 112, Chester-road, Lower Wa/ton. Lance-Cor- poral Alcock, who is unmarried and 28 years of any was educated at Arpley-street School, and before pining the Forces was employed by Messrs. R. A. -Naylor, Ltd., for a period of ten years. He enlisted in June of last year, and was sent out to the Front after only. three months with the Colours. His brother, Cor- poral J. B. Alcock, has had the distinction of being mentioned in dispatches.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-09-23_WidnesExaminer_art0082" n="art0082">
        <head>RIFLEMAN MASON.</head>
        <p>RIFLEMAN MASON.</p>
        <p>Praises Work Done For Wounded In A Wesleyan Hospital. The "Bath Herald" publishes some verses</p>
        <p>written by Rifleman Steven Mason, a White- cross wiredrawer,- of the Rifle Brigade, and son of Mr. William Mason, 29, Napier-street, War- rington. It will be remembered that Rifleman Mason, who was wounded in severhl places, in- cluding his foot, had the pleasure of seeing the King and Queen when 'hair -Majesties visited' a. Weston-Super-Mare hospital in which ,he was then a patient. He has herd no fewer than nine operations with a view to saving his foot, but the dootors have not yet succeeded in •their task.. He is now a patient in the Kingswood V.A.'Hospital, Lansdown, Bath, and the verses -are in praise of the nurses and the treatment wounded soldiers receive in that institution.</p>
        <p>• Kingswood School, of which the hospital is a part, is a school for Wesleyan ministers' Sons, and at the present time its tudents include two sons of the Rev. •W. T. • Derry, Bewsey-road Wesleyan Church. The last verse has referene't to the fact that the Wesleyans have provided this accommodation;--- • Will the Wesleyans of, Great Britain , Accept Tommy's splendid thanks For the care' they give to Tommy And attention to his wants.? And When this war is over , • No matter where I roam, . I shall think of dear old Kingswood School, The place that gave me home.</p>
