        <p>I R.1V1.1.M.1N S. MASON.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-09-23_WidnesExaminer_art0083" n="art0083">
        <head>PRIVATE R. ROWLEY.</head>
        <p>PRIVATE R. ROWLEY.</p>
        <p>Poisoned By Gas, Wounded, And Suffering From Shell Shock.</p>
        <p>Private Harry Rowley; • South Lancs. • Regi- ment, and son of Mr. and Mrs. J. G. Rowley, 57, Nicholson-street, who was wounded a short time ago, is now in hospital suffering from shell shock. He is wounded in the left wrist. Writing to his parents he says be is able to walk about a little, and hopes to return to England soon. He suffered from the effects of gas poisoning last rear, but was soon able to take his place .% il 11 1-h' -n-nrades again. Private Roww,,o, vrht, nqd belonged to the South Lancashire Regi,flent for four years, volunteered at the beginning of hostilities for active service. He is 21 years of age, and after leaving Wycliffe School he was first employed by Messrs. Joseph Crosfield and Sons, Ltd.,Thut latterly was the employment of the L. and N.-W. Railway Co. He was also .a member pf the Church Lads' Brigade, belonging to St. Paul's: Company.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-09-23_WidnesExaminer_art0084" n="art0084">
        <head>PRIVATE F. P. DONOVAN.</head>
        <p>PRIVATE F. P. DONOVAN.</p>
        <p>Iped To Drive havarians Bach Over</p>
        <p>Three Miles.</p>
        <p>Private Francis .Patrick Donovan, son of Mr. and Mrs. John Done Van, 35, West-street,- who joined the Irish Guards . a few months before the War began, was wounded September 15th. He took part in the severe fighting ea-Oy in September, and in a letter written three days before- he was wounded he said a parcel froin home , had just reached him on coming out of the trenches-. '` or rather, where there should be trenches." He added, "It is a very rough quarter just hero, and our fellows are still going ahead and bringing in Prisoners every day. I am just beginning to think, we are winning. It is almost incredible how the British `Tommies' are playing the game in the thick of it; every man is really worthy of a V.C. That is the way we. are smashing German tyranny!' - The next letter was written in a Red Cross train in England, and announced that he had got two bullets through the right shoulder, but would soon be quite fit again. A third letter stated that he was in a hospital at Wrexham,</p>
        <p>and was geting on famously. He added: "It was on the morning of September 15th that the Guards attacked the last stand of the Bavarians at about 6.30 a.m., nnd after we had pushed them back about three miles and a half I got two bullets through the shoulder." Private Donovan, who is a piano and violin player, is 41 years of age. Before joining the Army he was apprenticed to Mr. Dolan, builder, in the joiners' department. Ile attended St. Benedict's School and People's College. .</p>
        <p>RIFLEMAN J. SMITH.</p>
