        <p>PRIVATE F. P. DONOVAN Wounded.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-09-23_WidnesExaminer_art0085" n="art0085">
        <head>MISSING.</head>
        <p>MISSING.</p>
        <p>PtiIVATE A. GLARE.</p>
        <p>Any, NeVin His Whereabouts Win Be Gladly Welcomed.</p>
        <p>His commanding officer having written to his wife, Mrs. Clai'e, 39, Wellington-street, .sa4ing that her husband, Privatt Alfred Clare, South Ittueashire Regiment, had been woundei, and is /lbw missing, Mrs.- Clare is naturalfr very and iny new of his where- t es may be able to give will _abncx..ui anxious s aboutc comrades him, s, he gladly welcomed. A stretcher-bearer who was going to his aid said that just as he neared him a shell burst and he did not see him again. Private Clare, who is 37 years of age, for- merly worked for Mr. Daniel enoper, builder, Alexandra-street, and is the father of .five'ehil- dren. He was educated at the National School, Church-street, and was interested in football.</p>
        <p>Schoolboys Raise £5O for War Funds. The scholars of the Parochiftl Boys' School, Church-street,, have subscribed the ;following sums of money since the beginning of the war : £l.O for the Belgian Refugees' Fund„ £5 for the Lord Derby Hospital, Winwick, £5 for the Whitecross Hospital, £5 for "Our Day" Fund, £25 for-the Prisoners of War FuAd. Parcels are Sent weekly to two old scholars who are prisoners of war in Westphalia'. The headmaster (Mr. G. W. A. Brown) and staff take this opportunity of thanking the parents for their generosity.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-09-23_WidnesExaminer_art0086" n="art0086">
        <head>PRIX-ATE JOHN APPLETON.</head>
        <p>PRIX-ATE JOHN APPLETON.</p>
        <p>Reported Wounded And Is Still Missing.</p>
        <p>Mr. and Mrs. Appleton, 72, Ellesinere,road. ,Lower Walton, have not yet had any news of their son, Private John Appleton, who was re- ported as Wounded on •Auglist 2nd. Private I Appleton, who joined the Cheshire, Regiment. was called up in March, and is 29 years of age. He had been on active service since July, and was formerly in the employment of Mr. W. Monks, grocer, Moore.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-09-23_WidnesExaminer_art0087" n="art0087">
        <head>RIFLEMAN T. IL SKELHORN.</head>
        <p>RIFLEMAN T. IL SKELHORN.</p>
        <p>Wounded In The Chest And Mouth. Mrs. Skelhorn, who is 'at present residing at Malmsbury-place, Wilderspool, has, had a com- mlinication from the Rev. H. Traywarl, Army Chaplain, dated 6th Sept,nnber, stating that her huM3and, Rifleman Thomas Ilenry Skelhorn, is lying in 'No. 2 Stationary .11ospithl, France, suffering from -wounds in the chest and mouth. The reverend gentleman adzied that the patient was getting on nicely, and was receiving 'every attention. He hoped to visit him 'every day as long as he was there to cheer, and comfort him as much as possible. . On Tuesday 'Mrs. .Skelhorn • ,received an encouraging letter from. her husband saying that he was still in hospital and very weak, and there were no signs' yet of, his going to'Eng- land. His main trouble appears to be that he lost his shaving kit in the trenches, but added as an afttr thought that he was lucky to get away with his life. The action in which he was wounded appears to have taken place before the sth September. Rifleman Skelhorn is 34 years of age, and</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-09-23_WidnesExaminer_art0088" n="art0088">
