        <p>RIFLEMAN T. H. SKELTIORN.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-09-23_WidnesExaminer_art0089" n="art0089">
        <head>Wounded</head>
        <p>Wounded</p>
        <p>joined. the King's Royal Rifles in September, 1914. He is a native of Latchford, and was educated- at St. James' School. ' Ho was a mem- ber of the church choir: •Previoueto the war he worked at Young's, basketmakers, and learned his trade with the well-known firm of• Messrs. Thomas Hutchinson and Co., hamper, skip and basket manufacturers. His brother, Sergeant Arthur Skelhorn, who was wounded at Hill 60, is the prominent footballer, and another brother, Frank Skelhorn, is now at the Front.</p>
        <p>LANCE-CORPORAL T. ALCOCK.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-09-23_WidnesExaminer_art0090" n="art0090">
        <head>One Of Four Soldier Sons Wounded</head>
        <p>One Of Four Soldier Sons Wounded</p>
        <p>In The Leg.</p>
        <p>Mrs. Smith, •of 1, Bennett-street, has been informed that one of her four soldier sons, Rifleman Joseph Smith, of the Rifle Brigade, has been wounded in the leg. Rifleman Smith, who is 20 years of age, enlisted on the outbreak of war, and had been in France twelve months. He formerly worked at Reynolds's Tannery, and was an old boy of St. Thomas' School, Stockton Heath. He has had a brother, Private Robert Smith, killed, as reported in , anothef-Qcolumn, and has two more brothers still serving, Private Charles Smith of the Grenadier Guards, and Sergeant John Smith of the Inniskilling Fusiliers.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-09-23_WidnesExaminer_art0091" n="art0091">
        <head>RIFLEMAN JOHN FEENEY.</head>
        <p>RIFLEMAN JOHN FEENEY.</p>
        <p>Received Bullet Wound In The Left</p>
        <p>Iland.</p>
        <p>Rifleman John Feeney, of the King's Royal Rifles, 44, Catherine-street, has been brought to Townley Hospital suffering from a bullet wound in the left hand, and he is going on well.</p>
        <p>Rifleman Feeney, who has a wife and seven children, is 35 years of age, enlisted in Decem- ber, 1914, and has seen 14 months of active ser- vice. He was educated at St. Benedict's R.C. School, and was formerly employed by Messrs. Pearson and Knowles Coal and Iron Co., Ltd.</p>
