        <p>RIFLEMAN JOHN FEENEY. Wounded.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-09-23_WidnesExaminer_art0092" n="art0092">
        <head>PRIVATE H. S. BAILEY.</head>
        <p>PRIVATE H. S. BAILEY.</p>
        <p>One Of Three Soldier-Brothers Wounded.</p>
        <p>- Private 11. S. Bailey, South Lancashire Regi- ment, whose home is at -6, Church-street, has been wounded in the right arm and shoulder.</p>
        <p>Private Bailey, who formerly worked for Messrs. Joseph Crosfield and 'Sons, Ltd., is a son of Mr,. Bailey, of Stockton Heath, and is 32 years of age. He has played football for Warrington, Runcorn, Stockton Heath and Cadishead teams. He has two brothers also serving, Sergeant Clarence Bailey and Private Albert Bailey.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-09-23_WidnesExaminer_art0093" n="art0093">
        <head>PIM t;. A. APPLETON.</head>
        <p>PIM t;. A. APPLETON.</p>
        <p>A Richmond's Employe Has A Hand Amputalvd At The Wrist.</p>
        <p>The relatives of Private George Adam Appleton,, South Lancashire Regiment, who resides at 4, Hart's-place, off Bridge-street, have been officially informed that the Private has been wounded. His relatives went to see him on Sunday and found him going on as well as could be expected, he having had his hand amputated at his wrist. Writing from a Northampton hospital, a sister said that he was very cheerful and that she hoped he would soon be well again Private Appleton, who was called up under the Derby Group System. in February of this year, and had been in France five weeks, for- merly worked for Messrs. Richmond's Gas Stove and Meter Co., Ltd. He was educated at Heathside School, and has a brother in the A.S.C. and a cousin in the R.F.A.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-09-23_WidnesExaminer_art0094" n="art0094">
        <head>"MAGGIE" MISSING.</head>
        <p>"MAGGIE" MISSING.</p>
        <p>Fruitless Search For A Beautiful</p>
        <p>Warrington Girl.</p>
        <p>EVERYONE INTERESTED.</p>
        <p>" Maggie". remains undiscovered. In the official casualty. list she would be recorded as 'missing."</p>
        <p>Last week-end, owing to the publication of her photograph in "The Examiner," "Maggie" was the talk of the town. It will be remem- bered that during a charge a dying comrade gave her photograph to Private Oldham, of 33, Ellesmere-street, and „asked him to give it to "Maggie, of Howley." Like Private Oldham, everyone joined in the search, but no one could locate her. Those fond of a pretty fate and the romantic 'constituted themselves into patrol parties in the hope of finding her, but all to no krpose. The latest report is "Still no news of her.",</p>
        <p>Whether she ever graced a house in Howley has not yet been established. Had there been Women's Suffrage one might have been helped by the Voters' List. But that she is not in the district now is beyond doubt. With her face as the, target of every .eye, she could not move undetected.</p>
        <p>Few people have refrained from looking tor .her. Hunting for a girl is invariably interest- ing, if she is good-looking like !` Maggie." It is much more exciting than following the hounds. Frequenters of the town have been most fervent in their devotions to her. Not a girl has passed by without being subjected to a facial scrutiny, which has proved fatal to the hope ,within them. Before the " Knuts " turned out 071 Saturday evening they were extra careful over their toilet, lest they should meet and their charms should not match her's. But the " Knuts" no more attracted, her to Market Gate than the policeman. She was °aside the orbit of their influence.</p>
        <p>To everyone on Saturday the one question was, "lave you seen 'Maggie and in places where the people meet for converse and conviviality a toast to :the absent one was honoured. .Some vf the barmaids were re-christened -" Maggie," mid, glancing at her portrait Once more, gave the "long pull." for the compliment. - • • : It is not Our fault that she goes undiscovered.</p>
        <p>One of our observers called at Private Old- ham's residence, 33, Ellesmere-street, yesterday- afternoon, to see if there had been any develop- ment. Re had hardly drummed his knuckles against the door when, one after another dozens of other doors in the street opened, sand the eyes of many ladies With Friday-afternoon- blacking-the-grate-appearances were directed towards No. 33 on. the chance of learning whether the interviewer had news of Iler. "So you haven't found her," remarked the lady of the house, after, in depressed, jilted-like tones, he had told of Ills failure. "Neithe'r have we," she added. "We are awfully disappointed. A lot of people have taken au,. interest in` the matter, and we have had several callers asking whether she has been heard of." In Ellesmere-street and district the story of the missing lady has caused much excitement. 'Every shop has been transformed into a con- sulting moth.. The most curious part about the whole affair is that not a few people fancy they have seen "Maggie." Perhaps the explanation is that it is a compliment to oneself to mention that one knows a pretty girl. We have a theory that Private Oldham mis- understood the directions of his dying comrade, who', after all, was in a Liverpool Regiment.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-09-23_WidnesExaminer_art0095" n="art0095">
        <head>HIT IN A CHARGE.</head>
        <p>HIT IN A CHARGE.</p>
        <p>Rifleman Parkinson Describes Experiences Graphically.</p>
        <p>"A HELLISH TIME."</p>
        <p>Said by an officer to have been in an action, as severe as any in the line, Rifleman Herbert Parkinson, son of Mr. and Mts. William Parkinson, 102, Bewsey-road,- who. now lies wounded in a Dublin hospital, has written a graphic letter relating his experiences. Rifleman Parkinson, who is 37 years of age,.</p>
        <p>left Warrington in 1904 to take tip an impo-r-- ' tant appointment with the Surrey Education Committee. He is an Arpley-street "old boy" and before. securing an engagement at the Warrington Education Office was an assistant at the Museum and Library. He was also asso- ciated with Bewsey-road Wesleyan Church, being a member of the choir, and was well- known in musigal circles generally, being at- tached to the Musical Society and • Male Voice Choir. His interest in the art never flagged, and at Kingston-on-Thames, where he resided, he took part in several operas, interpreting leading characters with conspicuous success. As a letter writer he: is excellent.</p>
        <p>"I am so pleased to be able to let you know am still alive and kicking, although I have Lad a piece of iron or steel in ray head. One iece was taken out When I arrived at the</p>
        <p>iressing station from the 'trenches (a piece of</p>
        <p>my helmet) and the other piece (shrapnel) is still inside my knapper.' I expect to be oper- ated on next week, probably Tuesday, 26th inst., and then I shall be all right. "Well, I have had an experience' and no mistake. For many nights we had to walk four miles there and also four miles back from the trenches to assist the Canadians to repair</p>
        <p>trenches and make dug-outs. It was there I really came under fire, and at first I confess I felt a little uncomfortable, but in time I got used to the whiz of the shells coming over, and the flying of shrapnel pll about. Several of our chaps got wounds, but I, was one of the lucky ones to come through scot free. It broke me in finely, and I was prepared afterwards for the hellish time.. that was to COW. , '</p>
        <p>- - "We then had ,two. days in the train to an- other part of France, and we billeted at for two days. to give us a rest; then on we went for another long march up .to the reserve trenches.</p>
        <p>"Then the ,business started in earnest 'We were in some old German trenches for the first ,night, waiting for morning, to march to the next trench. At midnight a shell or two came over just 50 yarOs from me, and buried ten Of our chaps, wounded eight and killed five. We dug out the ten, managing to get five out alive. We moved on in the morning and got to the</p>
        <p>valley, which the men nick-named 'The Valley of Death,' and it was and no mistake! The English had pushed the Germans back abeut three miles two days before we arrived, and therefore 200 of us were detached to take water to our soldiers over the old battlefield. What a night! Although it was dark we could see the trenches were heaped up with German dead, some just coming out of dug-outs (I ex- pect they were killed by our.bombs) and others all lying over the fields where they' had fought their last. I was very glad to see they were Germans; in fact, the English dead were , very few.</p>
        <p>"Well, we. went down a valley, up. another hill, right to the top of the crest: The Germans sent up their shells and we were subjected to • about 15 minutes' severe fire (shell and rifle). We all got in- shell holes and came out very fortunate, got the water tO the camp, and walked back as quickly as we could. • After• doing above for several nights and other work we gradually worked. our way up to the first , line trenches. It was hell, and no doubt. We got in the communication trench, but the Ger- mans discovered us. Then the shells came thick and half of were wiped out before we got to ,the wood where the trenches were. Up and down the trenches we were run until our 'chaps were in position, and then didn't we give them gip! "We took two trenches all Iright, and were just going over for the third time when a shell burst five yarcjs from me. I heard it coming and dropped on the ground to avoid the shrap- nel. It did not come off. I got a piece right through the back of •my helmet, knocking part tf it into my head, and then going into my napper,' and is still there. The blood spurted out of my head, down my face, and then I thought it time to get back to the. dressing station. When I came to myself I put a bandage on the wound, and then had to make a dash back over the trenches 'we had taken'. Shells and rifle fire were everywhere, but I had to risk it. I waited some time, and, when things eased down, jumped on top of the trench, 1, made a dash for the nearest 'shell hole, . and waited my opportunity. for, another dash. My heart was in my mouth, but I went on repeat- ing the job, ' until I got,/ to the ambulance. dug-out. I had my head dressed there and then, was told that all the stretcher-bearers were wiped out or had gone with other cases back beyond the fire zone. I said I would endeavour 'to go back by myself, so three of us walked in stages. It was three miles to go, and wasn't I glad to' get there! They were exceedingly kind to us, arid then I had, the piece of helmet taken out of my head, had it properly bandaged, and was put in an ambu- lance after ha,ving had something to eat.. By stages I •arrived :at Rouen Hospital and was: kept there for five days. They fed me -up (breakfast, porridge and eggs; dinner, chicken and rice; tea, bread ,and butter, jam, &amp;c.; ,Supper. cocoa' and bread and butter) to get my strength back, gave me cigs. and grapes, &amp;c., ' and we had a tip-top time I had may head X7rayed, and then made for `,Blighty;'. 'but' I• have arrived in Ireland. I am very hakppy here and well looked after. The Irish nurses are very- kind and have done all they can for 'us_ Of course ,the food'is plain, but good, and we shall not do badly 'on it."</p>
