      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-09-23_WidnesExaminer_art0098" n="art0098">
        <head>MAKES WEAK PEOPLE STRONG AND THIN PEOPLE FAT, OR MONEY BACK.</head>
        <p>MAKES WEAK PEOPLE STRONG AND THIN PEOPLE FAT, OR MONEY BACK.</p>
        <p>•</p>
        <p>Chemist's Remarkable Offer.</p>
        <p>That -nitro-phosphate is an unequalled and positive cure for sleeplessness and nerve troubles is now universally admitted by chemists and medical men. Further proof of its wonderful value as a nerve food is found in the recently published report of an eminent specialist, who states that the outstanding feature of the thousands of cases investigated was that, in nearly evety instance, in addition to complete restoration of • nervous vitality and mental vigour, the patients reported a remarkable increase in physical strengtn and endurance, and an increase of from one to three pounds a week in sound, healthy flesh. In view of the con - elusive proof of these unusual merits, a leading British chemist has arranged to put up pure Bitro-phosphate in packages of 5-grain tablets; giving with each package a binding guarantee to return the money to anyone who, after regularly taking a 5-grain tablet with each meal as directed, is not satisfied with the results. Sufferers from nervousness, insomnia, nerve weakness, thinness, and lacl?.` of strength are therefore strongly advised to at once begin the use of Bitro-phosphate, the nerve food and flesh- builder, that is sold by 11. M. Ashton, 63, Sankey-street, and other high-class chemists everywhere, under a binding guarantee of satis- faction or money back.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-09-23_WidnesExaminer_art0099" n="art0099">
        <head>WERE ENGLAND FRANCE</head>
        <p>WERE ENGLAND FRANCE</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON LILLE,</p>
        <p>Why We Should All Join The</p>
        <p>Volunteers.</p>
        <p>By S.D.M.</p>
        <p>Entering Bridge-street, Warrington, the other day,, my attention was arrested by a large poster which stands near the place where that memorable drawing of Nurse Cavell used to stand, forcibly calling upon all eligible War- rington men to avenge so ghastly a crime. This poster is of an entirely different nature, and simply bears the following message:— I t is the duty of every man at home to join the Volunteer*</p>
        <p>How true! How clearly it tells us our duty. Although I have been a Volunteer for some considerable time, I honestly confess that until I read that poster I had not regarded my mem- bership of the Volunteers as seriously as I ought. I had gone to drill'time after time simply because I liked it, and not because it was my duty. Automatically, my mind seemed to travel to our dear lads out there, those dear brave lads who are fighting so treacherous an enemy and so bravely suffering the horrors of war, for you and me, for right against might./ Strange, But True.</p>
        <p>Nine-tenths of the people at home who, through Tribunals, starred trades, over age, &amp;c., have been exempted, are content to walk about doing nothing. What would you do if England were France, if Warrington were Lille? The horror of it! Warrington, our dear old mother town, in the occupation of so vile an enemy! Children torn from 'their mother's side; girls of tender ages sent to no one knows where; men going -about the streets carrying their lives in their hands, not daring to offer resistance, knowing too well the ghastly penalty, for a shot from a civilian may mean the whole- sale murder of the entire population; no one allowed to have a mouthful of food until the Kaiser's- gluttonous soldiers have had their fill. Fancy it. It seems like a horrible nightmare, but it's true, too true. That is the condition of things in Lille to-day, and the same would apply to Warrington, if England were-Northern Fran.-:e. The Fate of the Non-combatant.</p>
        <p>I am not surmising for one moment that the same conditions will ever apply to us. God prevent it. But remember we-are still at •war. and the enemy we are up against will stop at nothing. Nothing is too low or too dirty for them that would assist them to accomplish their heart's desire, " the invasion of England.:' Thanks to our gallaht lads in blue it is almost an impossibility. Let it be truly recorded here, and impressed upon the minds of all liable men who chance to read this article, that in the event o fan invasion no one who is a non-combatant will be allowed to take up arms to defend that, and those he loves so dear. He will have to stand by and allow things to take their course. Naturally he may be called upon to do such work as digging trenches and burying dead, but under no conditions will he be allowed to take up arms. This is one of the outstanding conditions 4et down in the Hague convention, and England thinks a little too much of her honour to allow her subjects to break so sacred a vow.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-09-23_WidnesExaminer_art0100" n="art0100">
        <head>Be' a Volunteer!</head>
        <p>Be' a Volunteer!</p>
        <p>To remedy this and give every man who is, ineligible for his Majesty's regular forces a chance to do his bit, the Volunteer Training Corps have been thrown open to all. By taking a solemn vow to bear true allegiance a civilian instantly becomes a soldier, he is given all the advantages of a soldier of the line. By sacrificing an hour or two each week he will be able to prepare himself, and if the worst comes to the worst he will know how to act. Let the men of Lymm—and Warrington—respond as eagerly to this appeal as the youth of Lynim— and Warrington—did to that of the late Lord Kitchener.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-09-23_WidnesExaminer_art0101" n="art0101">
        <head>VOLUNTEER ORDERS.</head>
        <p>VOLUNTEER ORDERS.</p>
        <p>Warrington Company. Headquarters, Drill Hall.</p>
