        <p>The Young Angler. Pa: "Didn't you promise not to go fishing again?" Freddy: "Yes, sir." Pa: "And didn't I promise to whip you if you did?" Freddy: "Yes, sir, but I didn't' keep my promise and I wcm't hold you to yours."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-09-23_WidnesExaminer_art0102" n="art0102">
        <head>– .....- , ,_.. ..._. _ ~,,, – . _, ....., .„. , _., .„. ~ ,: _.: , : 10. ISTRICT . .c EIVS i• ....-...._, I ...,.. ....._ _. -.,</head>
        <p>- .....- , ,_.. ..._. _ ~,,, - . _, ....., .„. , _., .„. ~ ,: _.: , : 10. ISTRICT . .c EIVS i• ....-...._, I ...,.. ....._ _. -.,</p>
        <p>LAY MM</p>
        <p>LOCAL FACTS AND FANCIES.</p>
        <p>By CRITIC.</p>
        <p>The allied feeling i as keen in the workshop as on the battlefield. .</p>
        <p>A Belgian refugee was this week the recipient of a beautiful silver cigarette case as a token of esteem from his fellow-employes at a local munition works.</p>
        <p>He left them to join the Belgian Army.</p>
        <p>As-I anticipated last week, the soldiers' con- cert went off Al.</p>
        <p>I . People came from far and ,ne'ar to witness what proved to be a most extraordinary enter- tainment.</p>
        <p>Even the good old " Examiner " landed in the first line of trenches.</p>
        <p>• "Our hospital," plus the author's vivid im- agination, was one of the principal attractions.</p>
        <p>Sergeant coreoran made a fine hit as the Matron. •</p>
        <p>His acting was splendid, and the part he was called to play suited him down to the ground.</p>
        <p>Corporal Heath, as the Doetor,*exe.lle.d him- self, and his quiet way of turning the patients over to the matron's own kind jutlgment caused endless rounds of laughter.</p>
        <p>Private Fairfield, as, Jerry, was here, there, and everywhere, and certainly was the most popular comedian in the cast. .</p>
        <p>His sly way of making lore to the nurse- girl fairly tickled the enthusiastic audience.</p>
        <p>The splendid result was due to the splendid management; every soldier had his own par- ticular work to do, from stage manager to call boy.</p>
        <p>The matron herself deserves a deal of credit, for the entire show came Under her own able management.</p>
        <p>What did the Sergeant Matron mean when he advised his patient to be very careful about choosing their company?</p>
        <p>And his continual remarks about the seat round the corner?</p>
        <p>I hear that there is to be a repeat perform- ance this afternoon for the benefit of school children.</p>
        <p>Three Septembers! What a coincidence</p>
        <p>A local soldier reported wounded this week, enlisted in September, 1914, drafted out to France in September, 1915, and returns to Englancl-Wounded, in September, 1916.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-09-23_WidnesExaminer_art0103" n="art0103">
        <head>JOY RIDE</head>
        <p>A JOY RIDE</p>
        <p>With Unpleasant Results: Police Court Sequel.</p>
        <p>The Hon. J. E. Cross (chairman), Xice- Chancell6r Dudley Stewart Smith, K.C., Messm John Coxon, H. Holden and F. Lockyer, magis- trates sitting at Lynam on Wednesday, heard a 'prosecution under the Licensing Act which was somewhat unusual in its nature.</p>
        <p>Two men hailing from Altrincham, named Harvy Arnold, painter, and William Jackson, labourer, were summoned by Constable Bison far being drunk on licensed premises on the Bth September. Defendants denied the charge. Inspector Brewster prosecuted.</p>
        <p>_ Mr. Joseph C. Gough, licen.ee or The Jolly Thresher, Lymm, stated that at about 8.50 p.m. a taxi drove up to the door and the driver and Arnold came into the house. They were both drunk, and witness absolutely refused to serve them. They refused to go out, and said hey should have a drink, or tobacco or something. The taxi driver went out, but Arnold refused to do so, and was abusive. Witness went to get a police officer, and when he returned Arnold had gone out by the back way. Jackson was in the car and witness could not</p>
        <p>Arnold: All I said to you was, "That is how you treat wounded soldiers."</p>
        <p>- Witness stated that Arnola said he had been fighting at Mons or somewhere. He called wit- ness a German and said he should be under the sod.</p>
        <p>Mrs. Gough corroborated her husband as to Arnold's condition. The other defendant, Jack- son, also came in and asked, for a glass of whisky and soda, which she would not serve him because he was drunk. She told him to go out and he did so. The two defendants had gone away when the policeman arrived. Inspector Brewster said this affair ,seemed to have been the result of a joy ride. The taxi driver was locked up and charged with being drunk while in charge of a motor car. He was fined 205., and the magistrates expressed the opinion that these two men should also be brought before the Bench. Arnold said he was not drunk on that occasion. He had been invited for a drive by Jackson, who was going to see his sister. When he saw the condition of the driver he refused to go further in the car. He walked to Heatley Station and got the train home. Mrs. Arnold stated that her husband got home about 1,0.30. He was not drunk.</p>
        <p>Insperetor Brewster: Did he tell you that Jackson had a bottle of whisky in the motor car?—Yes, and that he gave the motor driver a glass of it. Jackson also denied having been drunk on the occasion., When he was refused a drink he walked out immediately. • The magistrates fined each of the defendant, 10s. and ss. costs.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-09-23_WidnesExaminer_art0104" n="art0104">
        <head>ALLIED FEELING.</head>
        <p>THE ALLIED FEELING.</p>
        <p>Presentation To Belgian Refugee</p>
        <p>Called To The Colours.</p>
        <p>A pleasing ceremony took plqee at the Lanca- shire Tool Works, Henry-street, on Tuesday afternoon, when Mr. Andrew Van Der Mens- brougghs, a Belgian refugee, was presented with a silver cigarette case by his fellow employes. Mr. Andrew Van Der Mans- brougghs tells a remarkable tale. His home, he says, is in a Belgian town which at present is in the occui)ation of the enemy. His parents are still there, and he has not heard from them for months. When the Germans first entered his native town he was advised to get away, and after considerable difficulty he was able to get over to England. He came to Oughtrington and secured a situation with Messrs. Welford and -Co. at Broomedge, afterwards securing I) good berth with, Messrs. Wm. Moss and Sdns, of the Lancashire Tool Works. He has just turned 18 years of age, and has been called to the Belgian colours, and left Lymm to undergo his medical test in London on Wednesday. morn- ing.</p>
        <p>-Everything was going on as usual oui Tuekday afternoon, when suddenly the large gas engine which works all the tnachinery ceased, work was stopped, and all the employes were quietly told to assemble in one of the large store rooms upstairs. When the excitement had died down Mr. Albert Brown, one of the employes, handed their Belgian workmate a beautiful silver cigarette case as a token of good feeling from his fellow employes. Ve remarked that they were all sorry to lose him, as he was a good workmate, but they were plea.sea to know that he was goinkc to fight for the Allies' noble cause of right against that military machine which represented nothing but might. (Ap- plause.) Replying, the recipient said, "I am too full to say much, but I thank you from the bottom of my heart. I have tried to do my bit here making munitions, but now my time has come, and I am pleased to be able to go and fight side by side with the British ,Army, and to help to bring this terrible war to an early finish."</p>
        <p>•The pleasing ceremony then concluded with the singing of "For he's a jolly good fellow," which re-echoed through the whole village.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-09-23_WidnesExaminer_art0105" n="art0105">
        <head>THREE SEPTEMBERS.</head>
        <p>THREE SEPTEMBERS.</p>
        <p>Extraordinary Coincidence Relating To</p>
        <p>Local Soldier..</p>
        <p>News has this week been received to the effect that Private Percy, Appleton, the Cheshire Regiment, has been wounded in the left arm. Writing to his sister in Statham, the gallant soldier says: "Here we are again, still aliVe and kicking. No doubt you will be surprised to learn that I have landed in 'Blighty' with a nice one through the muscle of the left arm. The shrapnel went in the muscle and came out through my elbow. Now. that I am able to get up and knock about I would not part with it for a quid, although it has been 'some sore.' I have been under two operations, but it is now Al. It is a treat being in hospital and 'not half a nice warm bed; and what a relief to get rid of 'those visitors.' The only drawback, is that it is a war hospital and we don't get those</p>
        <p>Wounded</p>
        <p>extras, but never mind, we manage to get a packet of cigarettes a week. Oh! but it is a nice one, and the nurse is just coming to dress it. It is Hell on eaPth on the Somme front."</p>
        <p>This gallant soldier enlisted in September, 1914, was drafted out to France in September, 1915, and returned to .England wounded in September, 1916. He is 22 years of age, and was educated at Lymm School, and worshipped at the Primitive Methodist Church. He resides with his father at Heatley, and prior to the war was employed as a fitter by Chas. Moore Erni Co., salt manufacturers, Heatley. He is at present in the Northumberland War Hospital, N 'wcastle-On-Tyne. •</p>
