        <p>PRIVATE PERCY APPLETON,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-09-23_WidnesExaminer_art0106" n="art0106">
        <head>PRIVATES JOSEPH CLARE AND WILLIAM JACKSON WOUNDED</head>
        <p>PRIVATES JOSEPH CLARE AND WILLIAM JACKSON WOUNDED</p>
        <p>As we go to , press we learn- that Privates Joseph Clare and William Jackson, two well- known members of 'the Cheshire Reginient, have been, wounded for the second, 'time. Both are well known in- local sporting circTes.</p>
        <p>Private Wm. Jackson is a nieniber of the</p>
        <p>PRIVATE JOSEPH CLARE.</p>
        <p>Wounded.</p>
        <p>Lymm A.F.C.„ and resides in Moston-street, Newfields. His brother, Private Harry Jackson, we reported, as ".missing" a ,few -weeks ago. Private. Joseph Clare is of considerable foot- ball fame, being an old member of the Lymm A.F.C., and later a member of the Altrincham A.F.C. He was looked upon by football critics as a coming man. He resides with his mother</p>
        <p>PRIVATE WILLIAM JACKSON</p>
        <p>Wounded.</p>
        <p>at Booth's Hill. His brother, Company-Sergt.- Major H. Clare, is at 'present somewhere in</p>
        <p>France.</p>
        <p>CHESHIRE VOLUNTEER REGIMENT</p>
        <p>LYMM PLATOON.</p>
        <p>Orders for week commencing Saturday, &amp;pt. 23rd, 1916:— Tuesday.-7.30 p.m., Recruits' drill. Other members may attend for musketry. 8 p.m., Platoon drill. Wednesday.—lnspection at Chester bY Field Marshal commanding Home Forces. Particu- lars later.</p>
        <p>Thursday.-7.30 p.m., Recruits' drill; 8 p.m., Platoon drill. Orderlies.—Sergt.. S: D. Moston. and Pte. T. Hatton.</p>
        <p>G. R./WILLIAMSON, Platoon Commander.</p>
