      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-09-23_WidnesExaminer_art0107" n="art0107">
        <head>RED CROSS CONCERT.</head>
        <p>RED CROSS CONCERT.</p>
        <p>Wounded Soldiers As Authors And Entertainers.</p>
        <p>Times without number we have been treated to concerts, garden fetes and similar pleasures in aid of our' Brookfield Hospital, but last Wednesday's effort was of an entirely different nature. It was a concert given in aid of our Brookfield Hospital by the wounded soldiers themselves„ Nor has there ever been an enter- tainment given to an appreciative audience which contained more artistic talent than that given to the exceedingly large audience that gathered on this occasion. Every item on the programme was of "tip-top" excellence, and well deserved the thunderous rounds ..f applause that almost lifted the roof of the large building. The introduction was made by Private Fair- field, the author and producer of the two sketches, who asked the audience not to be too Yard on the artistes, as they had had little time in which to practise, and if they happened to make any mistakes [which they didn't] would the company treat them as lightly as possible? Then came the following splendid pro- gramme :—Chorus, "My old "Kentucky home," " Tennessee," Gunner Montague; comic, In which figured the renowned cinema artiste. "Charlie Chaplin," Gunner Gibbons and Private Bartlett; recitation, " Hursley Camp," Private Jeffery; "Mandalay," Private Fair- field; " Which swithch, &amp;c." Private Davies; [" The Trumpeter," Private Davidson; "Micky Dun's Party," Private Piggott; "Come, sing to me," Private Cooper; recitation, Private Jeffery; "Long, long trail," Gunner Peck. " Our Hospital." Then came the first of Private Fairfield's remarkable sketches, "Our hospital," in two scenes. The characters w-?.re: Geo. Parker, Gunner Gibbons; Jerry Knowles, Private Fair- field; Matron, Sergeant Corcoran; Red Cross Nurse, Private Holt; Doctor, Corporal Heath; Mrs. Wells, Private Piggott; Baby, Private Kentish; Nursemaid, Private Davis; Patients, Privates Jeffery, Cooper and Taylor. The first scene was the kitchen of Mrs. Parker's resi- dence (the hospital washerwoman), in which some amusing cross-talk was obtained from Geo. Parker and Jerry Knowles, chiefly con- cerning the hospital round the corner, the follies of women, nurses working for nought, and the seats in the new Lever-road. The second scene represented a ward in the Brook- field Hospital. The matron (Sergeant Cor- coran) is busy putting the patients through their early morning test (and torture), when somehow or other Geo. • Parker and Jerry Knowles get mixed with the patients and, though loudly protesting, they are placed in the fatal chair and put through a series of tor- tures. Then comes the doctor (Corporal Heath), who again puts them through it, and adds insult to injury by putting them all, on special (very special) diets. He also confines them to hospital for the day, and the' matron gives them some sound advice about the hos- pital's good name, keeping their ties straight, choosing their company, and concludes by tell- ing them that we want "none of their Winwick tricks here." Meanwhile the baby sitting in a bassinette is vigorously protesting, and creates considerable amusement. That con- cluded the first sketch, cleverly written and brilliantly acted. When \the curtain rose at the commencement of the second half of the programme it could be noticed that the scenery had undergone a sudden transformation and now represented the centre of a large wood. The second half com- menced with a quartet and was followed by : Song, "Major General Worthington," by Private Davis; "Genevieve," Private Piggott; comic, Gunner Gibbons; "The Broken Doll," Gunneralontague; " To-morrow will be Fri- day," Private Fairfield-; "Mountains of Mourne," Private Davidson; "Polly Wolley Doodle" (with chorus), Private Cooper; "When I lost you," Private Peck. Lymm News "Somewhere in France."</p>
        <p>The second sketch was "Somewhere in France." The scene represented a portion of the first•line of trenches. A party of English Tommies" were waiting to be relieve&amp; seated round a cokp fire, when suddenly the Sergeant enters and informs them that they are shortly to be relieved by Captain Williamson and his Cheshire Volunteers. (Applause.) Then comes the rum ration, followed by the chorus "If the Sergeant drinks your rum, never mind," and "Baby poll." Then the orderly goes out for rations, but manages to get hit with a Maxim gun round the corner and is brought in by the stretcher-bearers with a nice "Blighty one" in the arm. Then there is trouble about another ration orderly to take the place of the one wounded, which ends up with the chorus "Pack all your troubles in your old kit-bag." Then the postman. arrives with a:parcel bearing the Lymm postmark for "Jerry," and a sheaf of letters. The parcel is immediately opened and the first article taken out is an English paper, "The Warrington Examiner." (Loud applause.) ".Good old Lymm! What's the Lymm News?" (Renewed applause.) The Sergeant gets a letter from Cherry Cottage, Cherry-lane, which turns out to be a love letter, and as he finishes reading it the soldiers 'sing "Are we downhearted?" This is followed by the "Old folks at home," and as the soldiers fall asleep "Beep the home fires burning" could be heard in the distance. The finishing touches were given by the rattle of a Maxim gun and the boom of a big gun behind the scenes. - Praise for the Workers.</p>
        <p>During a break in the programme, Mr. A. de C. Parmiter, a member of the General Committee, said no Lymm entertainers had ever received thanks more cordial and sincere than those they would give the soldiers that evening. The idea of the concert origin- ated from them, which spoke very well for the hospital. Nurse Morehouse, the pianist, was on night -duty at the hospital, but she had sacrificed her sleep in the daytime to enable tte, men to -practise. (Applause.) Next came Sergeant Corcoran, the heart of the whole thing and orgauiser-in-chief—(renewed ap- plause)—who would be feeling very pleased at the success of his efforts. Private Fairfield's two sketches were cleverly written and splen- didly acted. "It is a pity he has not given us a representation of what he thinks the General Committee is like," said the speaker', amid laughter. "Now I will ask you to give a hearty round/ of applause for the soldiers' splendid effort." The applause was duly given.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-09-23_WidnesExaminer_art0108" n="art0108">
        <head>Result.</head>
        <p>Result.</p>
        <p>The proceeds of the concert amounted to .237 10s. 91d.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-09-23_WidnesExaminer_art0109" n="art0109">
        <head>POSER FOR THE BENCH.</head>
        <p>A POSER FOR THE BENCH.</p>
        <p>A Difference Between Cigarettes And</p>
        <p>Tobacco.</p>
        <p>At Lymm Petty Sessions James Shepherd, a local tobacconist, having his premises in Bridgewater-street, was summoned by Sergeant Griffiths for having, on the 20th August, sold cigarettes to a boy named Hatton, who was under the age of 16 years. _</p>
        <p>The Sergeant stated that he saw four boys, of whom Hatton was one, and who were all under the age of 16 years, smoking cigarettes in Back- lane.</p>
        <p>Defendant admitted having sold the cigarettes, but stated that the boy, said they were for a young man outside. Defendant said that Mrs. Shepherd desired to know if a gentleman sent Et. boy under 16 years for his tobacco was she to 'serve him.</p>
        <p>The question occasioned much merriment amongst the magistrates, and the Act of Par- liament was at once requisitioned. It was then discovered that the clause in the Children's Act only binned " cigarettes " and "'cigarette papers." .</p>
        <p>1- - The Chairman (Hon J. E. Cross) asked Inspector Brewster if this was the first case of the kind in Lymm, and the latter replied that it was to his knowledge. The Chairman said 'defen4ant hao,asked a question and raised a point that had given the Bench much amusement. The Act only applied to cigarettes and cigarette papers, and, there- fore, they should not ,have been sold to the boy. As it was elle first case of the kind in -Lynam defendant would be fined only one shilling. '</p>
        <p>Dear Apples: Warrington Boys .Charged With Theft:A-At the Juvenile Court, which sat at the Council Offices on Wednesday morning, three Wllrrington boys came before Messrs. T. J. Ridgway and H. Holden, charged with steal- ing , 71b. of apples, the property of Mr. Peter Hankey, of Thelwall. Defendants were found guilty and were fined .55., 3s. 6d. and 2s. 6d. respectively. These petty thefts are getting quite 'a common occurrence, and it is hoped thee(' summonses will be a warning to others.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-09-23_WidnesExaminer_art0110" n="art0110">
        <head>LATE MR. A. W. CALLENDER'S WILL.</head>
        <p>THE LATE MR. A. W. CALLENDER'S WILL.</p>
        <p>The will of Mr. A. W. Callender, of Lymm, has been proved at £10,265.</p>
        <p>Stack Fire At Rettclish.—Last Thursday after- noon a serious fire broke out in the stack yard of Reddish Hall }arm, Reddish, the property of Messrs. R. and W. Heatheote. A large stack of hay weighing about 35 tons caught fire and was burned to the ground. The alarm was immediately given and the Lymm Fire Brigade, under Captain Wm. Rose, were soon on the scene, but the fire was then beyond con- trol. They succeeded in preventing the fire from spreading to two other stacks which were close at hand.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-09-23_WidnesExaminer_art0111" n="art0111">
        <head>STOCKTON HEATH.</head>
        <p>STOCKTON HEATH.</p>
        <p>CHESHIRE VOLUNTEER REGIMENT.</p>
        <p>Stockton Heath Detachment.</p>
        <p>Orders for week commencing 25th Sept.:— Monday.—Company and Ceremonial Drill for all. Fall in, Headquarters, 7.45 sharp. Tuesday.—Appleton Rifle Range, for all members who have not yet taken their cards. Wednesday.—lnspection by Field-Marshal Lord French at Chester. Fall in, Bank Quay Station, 4 p.m. Revew Order, haversacks and great coats rolled. Recruits to attend in civilian dress, wearing brassards where issued. Each member of the company is expected to be pre- sent on this,occasion.</p>
        <p>Thursday'..—Recruits' Squad, Headquarters, 7.45 sharp.</p>
        <p>FRANK WARBURTON, Officer Commanding.</p>
      </div>
