      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-09-23_WidnesExaminer_art0113" n="art0113">
        <head>PADGATE.</head>
        <p>PADGATE.</p>
        <p>Private Scott Ward.—We are pleased to • re- port that Privates Scott Ward, of Cinnamon- lane, Fearnhead, who has been removed from France to King George's Trospital, London, is progressing favourably. He was visiteil by his mother and sister on Sunday, and they were agreeably surplised to find him so cheerful and comfortable.</p>
        <p>Works Picnic.—Owing to the kindness of Mr. Edward Gorton, the employes of the Padding- ton Bone Works had a very enjoyable outing to Southport last S4urday. The party left Padgate in the morning in two saloon coaches, special facilities for stopping express trains for them having been arranged by the railway com- pany both on the outward and return journeys. The firm very kindly provided railway fare and dinner, and,the day turned out very fine, which added much to the enjoyment. Mr. W. Jump (secretary) and Mr. H. Stafford (foreman), accompanied the party. Women's Missionary M eeting.—The quarterly meeting under the auspices of the Women's Auxiliary was held in the Padgate Wesleyan Sunday School on 'Thursday afternoon, presided over by Miss Turner, the Vicarage. A very in- teresting address was given by Miss Lowen- stein, of Birmingham, organising secretary of the WOmen's Auxiliary, in which she outlined various methods of raising funds to help on the good work being done amongst the Zenanas. She also made a strong appeal for helpers to carry on the work, and explained the course of training which was necessary in order to be- come a missionary worker. Mrs. Cottrell, of Fearnhead, was the soloist, and she also gave P sweet rendering of the solo, "I gave My life for thee!' Miss Turner and the speaker were given a hearty vote of thanks. A collection taken in aid of the society realised £5 10s. 6d. There were a goofl number present, and after the meeting tea was provided at a small cost.</p>
        <p>Church Lads' Brigade.—A concert organised by the members of the Church Lads' Brigade was held in the C.E. Schools on Thursday even- ing. Recently this brigade became affiliated with the Cadet Corps, and the members- are now wearing khaki uniforms, and the iponcert was promoted to defray the expenses of the new equipment, as well as to raise funds for the further development of the organisation. There was a good atray of talent on the programme, and judging by the numerous encores the selections were suited to the taste of the audience. Mr. Poole was 'very successful in his songs, "Drake goes west" and "Up from Zummerzet," and Private Young sang in a spirited manner "Sunshine of your smile." Mr. Sephton sang "Maisie, my girl" and "The trumpeter," whilst Mrs. Sephton was heard in the old favourite, a Angus Macdonald." Miss Hewitt, who is always a favourite with Padgate audiences, sang in her usual finished style, "When the heart is young," also joining in the well-known duet, "Excelsior' along with Mr. Poole. A duet• entitled " Springtime " was given by Mr. and Mrs. Sephton. "Watchman, what of the night?" by Messrs. SephtOn and Poole, and a humorous item, "Don't we, Harold," by Messrs. Beswick and Berry. A. humorous trio, "The ideal home," was given by Messrs. Beswick, Berry and Harrison, and the quartets, "When the budding bloom" and "Anchored"• were also sung by Members of the concert party. Mr. Harrison created much amusement by his humorous selections, " P.C. 49" and "Phil the fluter's ball." There were a good number present, the chairman being Mr. George Proffitt, of Padgate.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-09-23_WidnesExaminer_art0114" n="art0114">
        <head>SHORTAGE OF MEN AND LEATHER.</head>
        <p>SHORTAGE OF MEN AND LEATHER.</p>
        <p>Relative to the unbadging of men in the trade (says the Warrington correspondent of the "Leather Trades Review ") there have been several interesting appeals made locally during the past week, and whilst in some cases mutual arrangements have been made 'at the Tribunals to allow a proportion of the men to remain in their employment, others are held in abeyance pending further inquiries. There is no doubt that this depletion of labour will cause much inconvenience at a time like this (especially when all those who have been released have joined up), as in face of the fresh demands of the War Office for leather of all classes, the trade could do with more men and not less, and as there is practically no more substitution to be got, there•will,be great difficulty in combating the requirements. On top of this there is a likelihood, of further combing out, and„ as pointed out in these columns a few weeks ago, there will be nothing for it but reduced inputs.</p>
        <p>A feeling was prevalent in certain quarters that a proportion of bends would be released for the civilian trade towards October, but there seems very little hope of this, considering the numbers that are still required by the Army authorities. - The local representative of the "Leather World" says : Tanners have now information before them of the requirements of the Government of the different kinds of leather and the apprdximate quantities to be provided up to the end of the year. It is certainly an enormous amount, and covers the Italian boot order, a further million pairs of boots for the Russian Government, bends for repairing Army boots, and the upper ad sale leather for the service boots for our own Army. Whilst tanners can sell through factors with the guarantee that the leather will be used for purposes approved by the Director of Army Contracts after October, this is, of course, no concession for boot manufacturers for domestic purposes, who will evidently have te• rely on imported leather absolutely, as, in. .order to' fill Government requirements, practi- cally all the leather produced in this country will be earmarked down to 61b. bends.</p>
        <p>OVERWORK and worry don't pay. Nobody can defy Nature's laws. We all need peace of mind, rest, outdoor exercise and eight hours' sleep to keep well. We must not overeat, nor drink much, un- less it be milk or water.</p>
        <p>Unwise habits load the blood with uric poisons. The kidneys act as safety-valves, filtering the• blood and passing off the poisons, but are bound to weaken under any long continued strain, and, once they falter, you feel dullvtired and nervous. Your back aches, you lose weight, you have headaches, dizzy spells and urinary disorders.</p>
        <p>The first thing to do is to get back to simple, sensible habits. You must also help the weakened kidneys. You can depend for this on Dean's Backache Kidney Pills.</p>
        <p>Many in Warrington have learned their worth and willingly tell about it.</p>
        <p>Send for Free Book on " Moderation, Cheer- fulness, and Other Long Life ' Laws."</p>
      </div>
