      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-09-23_WidnesExaminer_art0117" n="art0117">
        <head>All dealers, or 219 a box from Foster-McClellan Co., 8, Wells St., Oxford St., London, W.</head>
        <p>All dealers, or 219 a box from Foster-McClellan Co., 8, Wells St., Oxford St., London, W.</p>
        <p>Backache Kidixey rfl</p>
        <p>BOWLING.</p>
        <p>"GEORGE AND TOSS."</p>
        <p>Veterans Start Charity Bowling</p>
        <p>Tournament.</p>
        <p>Still another charitable effort was favoured with magnificent weather when an extremely interesting bowling match between Lancashire Professionals and Warrington Aniateurs came off on Saturday at Rylands- ntothers' Recrea- tion Ground.</p>
        <p>Although the afternoon became rather chilly, the opening stages of the contest were conducted under, extremely pleasant conditions and brilliant sunshine was a prominent factor in attracting hundreds to the fixture. The proceeds being in aid of local charitable insti- tutions, a great debt is due to that excellent sportsman, Mr. William Green, jun., who not only got the professionals together, but de- frayed their expenses, and, in addition, gave a handsome donation. The ground was in splen- did trim, and although plenty of rain fell the previous day, the green where the matches were played offered every inducement for clever play. Standing and seating accommoda- tion had been provided through the kindness of Messrs. Peter Walker and Son, Ltd., and it was estimated that between five and six hundred persons were present when the pro- gramme opened. The arrangements were ad- mirable, under the direction of Mr. J. Wood, hon. secretary. Mr. F. Dean was referee, and Messrs. T. Pa4tington kBlackliool) and T. Garnett acted as measurers.</p>
        <p>It was a great pity that the silver band of the R.A.0.8. was not present to play on those grand old exponents of the game, W. Taylor (" Owd Toss ") and George Ball, sen., who headed the programme and had uninterrupted use of the green for fifteen minutes, before a later generation were permitted to enter the lists. It .was pre-eminently an occasion for a blare of trumpets, and "See the conquering heroes come."</p>
        <p>The Psalmist's limit pf three score years and ten has been well exceeded by these doughty veterans, for they are both, to use a nautical phrase, "Seventy-fours." Taylor had the advantage in age—he is a month older than Ball; but the latter had the advantage in bowls, because he won the game. Both the old men were in the best of spirits, and replied to the hearty reception of the crowd with Some ancient quips, which, apparently, lost none of their pun- gency on the present occasion. Starting from the centre of the green, they gradually lengthened their distances, until they finally "showed off" with long corner shots. They were pretty stiff at first setting out, but as they warmed to the game and got exhilarated with the onlookers' applause., they followed the jack with a wonderful amount of energy. , There was a deal of arm waving, urging and demonstrat- ing as each of the aged players tried to give his opponent the inside crook, "Good shot, Toss !" "Well done, George !" was the order of the day, and excitement ran high when the game wa level at nineteen up. The veterans had worried each other's flanks all the time, and when a tremendous effort by Ball got "two in" and completed the victorious figure twenty- one, there was a long round of hearty applause. It was a notable conflict—a tough, hard-won fight. _ _</p>
        <p>The match resulted in an extremely credit- able win for the amateurs by 33 points. Appended is the list of players and scores:—</p>
        <p>Professionals. Amateurs. W. Taylor (Old Toss) 19 G. Ball, sen. ..... 21 G. Beatty ....... l5 Jack Gleave (L.C.)... 21 John Wilson T. Hackett (capt.) Ti. Banks Tom Hilton (Teapol).. 12 J. Isherwood Nat Sharples ...... .21 T. Humphries (L.C )17 Geo. Entwistle Sam Bradley Ben Hilton' Peter Bromilow (Shino) Jas. Southern S. Barker (Fetch)</p>
        <p>.. 21 J. Lodge .. 16 A. Gleave, jun. .. 14 Joe Gleave (L.C.) 2l .. 21 Jack Monks .. 16 E. Blackledge</p>
        <p>M. -Sharpie's</p>
        <p>Slater ...</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-09-23_WidnesExaminer_art0118" n="art0118">
        <head>R \ K QUAY CHARITY MAIN.</head>
        <p>R \ K QUAY CHARITY MAIN.</p>
        <p>First Round.—Continued.</p>
        <p>The first round in the charity main being run by the Bank Quay Club was continued on Saturday, with the following results:- G. Savage (4) 13-,11. Bennett (M.H.) (3) 13-12, A. Randles (8.H.) (2) 13-12, A. Middleton (3) 13-10, W. Gleave (M.H.)--(5) 13-12, J. Berry (5) 13-12. A. Wright (4) 13-12, W. Miller (4) 13-8, E. Hill (C.) (5) 13-10, J. Grounds (4) 13-12, D. Armitage (4) 13-9, J. Timmins (2) 13-2, J. Ash. burner (4) 13-10, C. Boylin (4) 13-10, Albert Payne. sen. (4) 13=-11, George Gleave (4) 13-11, J. Hatton (3) 13-12, James Taylor (4) 13-4, R. Gorst (4) 13-12, Bob- Brobbins (3) 13-11, T. Moore (4) 13-8, Alfred Gleave, sen. (S.) 13-6, W. Houghton (4) 13-11, J. Millington (5) 13-6, Jim Harrison (C.) (1) 13-7, J. Daintith (3) 13-12, R. Kirby (6) 13-9, Albert Gleave (owes 2) 13-6, D. Jones (F.) (5) 13-12, J. Woods (1) 13-12, T. Welsby (4) 13-9, F. Deakin (4)^.13-10, Albert Payne, jun. (2) 13-9, C. Hodgkins (4) 13-7, J. Middleton (1) 13-8, G. Willcox (2) 13-12. G. Warburton (2) 13-8, James Ellison (4) bye. The following had walldo.over:-T. Shay (5), S. Fisher (5), Bill Taylor (4), J. Darby (5), J. Bird (5), H. Hutchinson (4), H. Orsburne (4). W. Shay (5), J. Lockett (3), A. Timmins (5), J. Shaw (4), H. Cook (scr.), B. Green (2), Alfred,Gleave, jun. (owes 1), J. Eaton (4), John Gleave (owes 1), H. Jones (2), J. Kendrick (4), J. Joyner (5), J. Watscin (3). H. Williams (4), E. Moores (4), Joe Brown (5), J. Wilding (5), T. Woods (6), S. Hackett (6), W. Webb (4), P. Hill (4), J. Cartwright (C.) (4), J. Lester (C.) (4). G. Ward M. T. Crutch (4), John</p>
