      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-09-23_WidnesExaminer_art0119" n="art0119">
        <head>THREE-ASIDE AT ARPLEY.</head>
        <p>THREE-ASIDE AT ARPLEY.</p>
        <p>The three-aside competition at Arpley concludes to-day, and it is hoped that players will be proniPt• Mrs. Frost is to present the prizes.</p>
        <p>THE LATCHFORD CADET CORPS.</p>
        <p>Orders for week ending September 29th :</p>
        <p>_ - Parades.—Tuesday, no ;are. Fridail fall-in at 7.30 prompt at Headquarters. all" march to a selected spot for skirmishing, 1'; officer-in-charge, Lieutenant IL Kitchen. Orderlies.—Corporal E. Lea and Lance-Corpl. S. Cartwright. Promotion.—Cadet G. Hackwell to be Lance- 'Corporal, with effect from Friday.</p>
        <p>J. B. STARKEY, Commanding Officer.</p>
        <p>DRUNK, DISORDERLY AND BADGED. At the Borough Police Court on MondaY: Johin Graham, a man of military age, no settled abode, was charged by Acting-Sergeant Clark° with having been drunk and disorderly °II Saturday. The officer stated that Graham l'T5 wearing a war badge at the time. The wag's' trates fined defendant 19s. 6d. or 14 days. The Chief Constable: He will be reported and we will have him debadged.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-09-23_WidnesExaminer_art0120" n="art0120">
        <head>COURTEOUS MOTOR CYCLIST.</head>
        <p>A COURTEOUS MOTOR CYCLIST.</p>
        <p>Enalyn Evans, 691, Knutsford-road, was stall' moned at the Borough Police Court on Tsl°s; day for riding a motor cycle without a ligh' at 10.5 p.m. on the 6th September along th,e Manchester-road. Owing to the courteous in which he gave every information to ti'e police officer who stopped him, telling hit° that the water had' given out in his acetyletle lamp, he was only fined 10s.</p>
        <p>Self-torture. We pity the misguided devotees who Put stones in their shoes for a pilgrimage, or 101 walk over thorny paths to "make merit" many of us have days when we walk our IP,: road in much the same way, and with no sue' worthy end in view. We translate the careles; speech into intentional offence, the litt'„e neglect into deliberate unkindness, the comixo' duty, into hardship, and press them in ulxl our hearts until the whole spirit is sore al:: bruised with self-torture.—" Great Thoughts. Just What She Wanted.</p>
        <p>The much-travelled young man had just turned from foreign climes, and, of course, re must entertain his rich old aunt (with whoa Pi was in favour) with stories of the wonderf°' sights he had seen. "Yes," he said in the course of his remari.?' "there are some spectacles that can never De forgotten." ld "Dear me," exclaimed the absent-minded ° lady, "I do wish you would get me a pair ° them, John."</p>
        <p>!."...</p>
      </div>
