      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-09-23_WidnesExaminer_art0123" n="art0123">
        <head>BY ALEC J. BRAID.</head>
        <p>BY ALEC J. BRAID.</p>
        <p>sectors in this story are purely Imaginary living</p>
        <p>CHAPTER XVII.</p>
        <p>THE SING OF DIAMONDS AND THE QUEEN OF HEARTS. When Esther had sufficiently recovered from her 'exciting experience she and Quabba bade farewell to the kind-hearted mine boss and his wife. "You Will find Mr. Powell on his yacht at Santa Barbara." they were told, and in a trap placed at their disposal they started for the railroad thirty miles away. The driver's face was wreathed with a cheerful smile of appreciation at the honour he felt was his in convoying so fair a young woman and so interesting a personage as the foreign droll, Quabba, ensconced on the back. Esther listened to the driver with seeming atten- tion, but her thoughts were far away. The old feud of the stanleighs would not die down. Girl as she was, Esther felt inspired with blood hatred, Which is as strong as blood's more kindlier ties, and stronger. Blair was in, her thoughts, more in Contempt than fear, while of his gipsy companion and accomplice, Luke Lovell, she thought not at all. As the trap sped along towards the base of the mountain, Blair Stanleigh and Luke Lovell had gained the summit in their long and arduous tramp from the mine portal to • the other side of the range. , Blair's field-glasses were focussed upon Esther, it would seem, at the very moment her mind's eye Was focussed upon him. "There goes Esther," cried Blair, putting down the field-glasses and pointing to the trap that Moved, a mere speck to the naked eye, along the road far down below. "We can intercept her yet 117 dropping straight down this cursedly steep biasuntain," Blair added. Luke grunted a surly assent, and the two uesPerate adventurers started down the rough, straight mou.ntain trail at as brisk a pace as they dared attempt. That Arthur Stanleigh was or was hot what he seemed was of little moment to Luke. Ile was bewildered on the edge of a great secret of Which he had but the faintest glimpse and grasp. only remembered that Matt Harding, Hagar's 'hug dead husband, had trafficked with the gentle- folk of Stanleigh Hall and had made his gipsy fortune thereby. Luke also knew that Esther had the document that was the essence of this Stanleigh secret. It also concerned The Diamond from the Sky. Luke had no love for Blair Stanleigh. He determined ,ur.on two things. One was that no harm should befall Esther, for whom Luke had long held a wild affection. The other resolve concerned the dia- mond. Seemingly the accomplice of Blair Stan- leigh in his tiesperate schemes to regain and Possess it, yet in the back of his mind he felt his deepest joy would be when he throttled Blair Stanleigh and tore it from his weakening grasp. As Blair and Luke sped_ down the mountain, over a rocky and half-obliterated trail, to intercept Esther, liarmaduke Smythe, guiding himself by the sun, plodded along the road at the base of the mountain. Marmadnke was imbued with one idea. The idea was that "England, home, and beauty," as he expressed it to himself, lay to the east. So at sunrise he faced the east, carrying the suit case, his gun, and the deer head, which more and more he had come to believe was a bona- fide trophy of his prowess as a hunter in the American jungle. The Diamond from the Sky Meant little or nothing to Marmaduke Smythe. " How it came where found I cannot for the life hf me imagine," he mused. "I only know, Mar- Inaduke Smythe, you are a jolly fool to have ,ever left England to find the Yankee heir to the Stan- leigh Earldora." These reveries were ended abruptly by a succes- ion of pistol shots. The startled lawyer next heard a pounding clatter of hoofs and the rattle of wheels coming behind him. Then he felt him- self bowled over and scattered with his belongings dust. Picking himself up, he saw that the wagon was driven by the pretty girl who had shared the Coach with him just previous to the wreck that had made him what he was now—a wanderer in the wilderness. But hardships and adventure had sharpened the wits of the timid lawyer. He gave a willing hand to help the horses out of their tangle of harness against the hillside, and was quick to accept the • proffer of a lift from the Mysterious young woman who had been his fellow- •.bassenger in the wrecked coach and the strange- looking hunchback with her. Luke and Blair had failed again. They had intercepted the trap, and had shot down the brave young miner, who had refused to halt at their sudden shouts to do so when they had sprung out from the brush of the hillside on to the road. "They have shot me! Drive on and save your- selves," had been the last words of the driver, and even as he had fallen, or thrown himself from the seat, he had struck the horses a quick, heavy blow with the whip. The maddened horses left the Pursuing Luke and Blair far behind. At a fork in the road Esther took the wrong turn and left the road to the rail. Realising this, they waited till daybreak. Around their small camp fire the three so strangely thrown together recounted the eventful" adventures that had befallen them since the wrecking of the coach. 'lt is a coincidence, my dear young lady," Ifarmaduke Smythe remarked finally, "that you are seeking for this elusive young millionaire, John Powell, too. I have a letter for him- from that astute chap Blake, the Richmond detective. This slake is a most reticent person, but he assured ..'ne that if I came to California and saw this John tiowell, the latter would help me to locate Arthur Stanleigh. Whether he is a ticket of leave man 'r is to be lynched or remain as a fugitive is not 'll my province to argue. He is the heir to the Stanleigh Earldom. But frankly, young lady, if Yigland expects me to go any further in doing my duty in this matter, England is vastly mistaken. I am going back as soon as I can find a train and boat to take me. If you are seeking this John 4"owell also, will you ask him to convey this to Arthur Stanleigh? Oddly enough. although I was not at all surprised at anything that happened to ' hie in America, I found this yesterday." To the breathless amazement of Esther and 9uabba, the lawyer brought from his pocket, in the Most matter of fact manner, The Diamond from the Sky. Neither the girl nor her hunch- -IMcfc. r)rr, reer)seeer, ceecerxr.</p>
        <p>0</p>
        <p>and have no connection with or relation to any person.l</p>
        <p>back protector could grasp or solve the mystery of the strange reappearance of the great and ancient Stanleigh heirloom here in this wild land. As .for the lawyer, he only asked to be rid of it. "I wish no further /responsibilities, and I would not stay in the jungles of America for a leather hatbox full of diamonds from the sky," he added. "So please deliver it to 'Mr. Powell' for Arthur Stanleigh, with my compliments, exacting, of course, a receipt." As he said the words he made a brief receipt in his notebook and tendered the book and the fountain pen to Esther, adding, "Please sign, Miss Harding." But when Esther affixed her name boldly, "Esther Stanleigh," the English lawyer scratched his head in a puzzled manner. But although he was puzzled he was not surprised. Nothing that could now happen in America would surprise him. Esther clasped the diamond around her neck. by every right and title it belonged to her. The impulse that had actuated her father, the late Colonel Stanleigh, to rob her of her birthright in order that the great gem might never go to Blair Stanleigh, his enemy's child, was the impulse that made Esther's 'heart beat resolutely with the same resolve. If The Diamond from the Sky was in sooth the Stanleigh tarm against harm, Esther Stanleigh needed its protecting influence now. Later in the day they reached the railroad and travelled to Santa Barbara.</p>
        <p>Called by business to Los Angeles, Arthur in- vited Vivian Marston to accompany him. In that city she was weaving the spell of her /charms about the rich young man. Her task was not so easy as she imagined it would be. ~Arthur's heart was with Esther, and he longed to see the sweet girl and pour out the story of his love and hopes. It was because of this that Vivian did not find Arthrr such an easy victim as she did Blair, and the young woman, eager to possess the diamond, could not understand Arthurts attitude.</p>
        <p>Seated in the garden of her hotel and planning the future, Vivian was astonished when a dapper young Frenchman, attired in the height of fashion, claimed acquaintanceship. In response to her denials, he deftly placed a card in her hand and was gone.</p>
        <p>Vic-lan had recognised de Vain, and was almost afraid to look at the card he had thrust upon her. The card was a playing card, the king of dia- monds! "What can Durand want?" she whis- pered to herself as she Quietly walked back to the hotel.</p>
        <p>In the privacy of her room she split the card to find the message. If the message was genuine she knew where to find it. There it was, with all its probabilities. "To the Queen of Hearts.—You will work with us. We, too, are after The Diamond from the Sky.—The King of Diamonds." No, there was no mistake. The past had come back to strike her a cruel blow. With a run the walls of the castle she had been rearing crumbled to the ground. Clever schemer though she was, Vivian Marston was not a match for Frank Durand, and she realised that now he was after the jewel, if the gem were discovered it would be sold and the proceeds divided as mere plunder. The King of Diamonds had no sentiment in his make-up. He was all the time out for business, and the recovery of the Sta,nleigh "charm against harm" was to him merely a business proposition. Never would she possess the jewel she had schemed so much to gain. She found, too, that, old ties were not easily broken. When Vivian met Durand she tried to bluff him. "That won't do, Vivian," he replied. ." You have hidden yourself successfully for many months, but we are not through with you, my dear girl. We must find the diamond, and share and share alike. You tell me you don't know where it Is. Flatly, I don't believe it. I insist that you help us. You dare not defy me 1" And Vivian knew that Durand spoke the truth.</p>
        <p>Having lost Esther and the document, Blair re- turned to Santa Barbara and wired to Los Angeles to Arthur, who, at the instigation of Vivian, by telegram instructed the sailing master to allow Blair to take charge. "Make 'yourself at home, Mr. Stanleigh," said the ,sailing master, when Blair went aboard. "You will find things in Mr. Powell's cabin." " "I will need a good deal of fixing up," Blair explained, laughingly. "My man and I got lost in the mountains while hunting," and he indicated Luke, who had come aboard with him. "Let your man go to the forecastle, and I will see he is fitted out," said the sailing master. "As for you, sir, I take it from Mr. Powell's telegram that you are to help yourself to what you wish of his things until you are sent for." While Blair was making himself presentable in Arthur's cabin, Esther, accompanied by Quabba, was making way across the • harbour in the hope of finding Arthur on the yacht. Arriving by the companion-way, Esther cautioned Quabba to make no explanation if questioned, as she wished to, give Arthur the happy surprise of facing him unannounced. But no sailor on watch barred her way, nor were any questions shouted over the rail to Quabba. Esther, crossing the deck, saw no sailor or officer in charge. She heard someone stirring abaft, and, in what was evidently the owner's cabin, she noted a tall and stalwart young man in uniform, his back turned to' the open door. Esther tiptoed forward, and, pulling The Diamond from the Sky out in view upon her neck, her lips framed the word "Arthur," when she found herself gazing horrified into a mirror that reflected the handsome but wicked face of Blair.</p>
        <p>Esther turned with a cry for help, and sped across the doorway to the forecastle. The bulky form of a leering, sodden brute creature faced her. It was Luke Lovell. Esther turned and darted to one side, missing ,the clutching grasp of Blair Stanleigh by a hair's-breadth. The sudden revul- sion from hope and happiness to fear and despair had driven Esther to a frenzy of sudden fright. As though upon the wings of fear, he fled. Hardly knowing what she did save that she escaped her enemies, Esther plunged over the sheer side of the yacht into the sea! (To be continued.) &gt;recerrAceeercereeeCee"ni</p>
        <p>The Great Flying -A</p>
        <p>Serial Film.</p>
        <p>_ _ • r, - ,40V4. _ . • ir401,1 - - r":"</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-09-23_WidnesExaminer_art0124" n="art0124">
        <head>Qra4JaOQJJ4, J430c4Ci)(4444Q4 44Qc4C4C3ZQQQ(41QQ4JJ4c41,</head>
        <p>Qra4JaOQJJ4, J430c4Ci)(4444Q4 44Qc4C4C3ZQQQ(41QQ4JJ4c41,</p>
        <p>HUMANITY SAVED AT VERDUN.</p>
        <p>Mr. Lloyd George's Glow ing Praise</p>
        <p>Of Prance.</p>
        <p>The following official account is published of banquet given in honour of Mr. Lloyd George VI Friday in the 'casements of the citadel of lerdun, • to which he was conducted by M. Albert Thomas, Minister of Munitions, and ?eneral Dubois. After dinner General Dubois !I_3r.ilg proposed the toast of the distinguished visitor and the great allied nation, Mr. Lloyd George replied as follows :—I wish YO of all, General, to tell you of the pleasure -,1111.11 have given me by asking me to sit at table with Your officers in the very heart of your those i&amp; adel of Verdun. I a happy to see around :Lose who have come from battle and those "na will be in it to-morrow and those who mount guard with you on these. inviolable The name of Verdun will awaken in tlfiltul7e ages an imperishable memory. 'None of great feat of arms with which ,the history France is filled has better displayed the pleheat qualities of the French Army and i.rench nation, and this bravery and this devo- :,(ln to the Fatherland, to Which the world has 'ways paid homage, have been reinforced by a</p>
        <p>coolness and tenacity that need not fear com- parison with the British phlegm_ , The memory of the 'victorious resistance ,at Verdun will be immortal, because Verdun has saved not only France lint our great common cause and all humanity. On the heights which surround this old citadel the maleficent power ,of the enemy has been, 'broken like a furious sea on a granite rock. They have tamed the storm which,menaced the world. For my own part I feel profound emotion on treading this sacred soil. I. do not speak only for myself, but bring you the heartfelt admiration of my country and the great Empire which I repre- sent here. They bow like myself before the sacrifices and glory of Verdun. Once more humanity turns to France for the defence of the great causes with which its whole future is bound up. - .</p>
        <p>In the midst of deep silence Mr. Lloyd George then raised his glass and said :—" To 'France, to the heroes and the graves of Verdun!"</p>
      </div>
