      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-09-23_WidnesExaminer_art0129" n="art0129">
        <head>RI \CORN AT WILDERSPOOL TO-DAY.</head>
        <p>RI \CORN AT WILDERSPOOL TO-DAY.</p>
        <p>Runcorn are visiting Wilderspool to-day (Satur- day), and an interesting match should exisue. War- rington's team will be: dolley; Mills, Britton, Tranter, Baker; Dainteth, young; Green, Cunliffe, Matthews, Glegg, Dumbell and Coillam. Britton and Dumbell are coming up from the Intermediate League. and ,standard, play is ex- pected, for they have showed good farm. Harmer joined the Army on Friday of last week.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-09-23_WidnesExaminer_art0130" n="art0130">
        <head>LAST SATURDAY'S RESULTS.</head>
        <p>LAST SATURDAY'S RESULTS.</p>
        <p>G. T. P. 0. T. P. Salford Leigh . 2 210 St. Helens Recs 1 1 5 Wigan ......... 4 420 Rochdale H. Hull Dewsbury ...... 0 1 3 Wakefield T. ... 1 0 2 Halifax Leeds .......... 1 3 11 Huddersfield 8at1ey.........0 1 3 Bradford Hunslet ........6 6 30 Brighouse</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-09-23_WidnesExaminer_art0131" n="art0131">
        <head>INTERMEDIATE LEAGUE.</head>
        <p>INTERMEDIATE LEAGUE.</p>
        <p>A FAMOUS 22.</p>
        <p>Ileathside Make Rings Round Beamont.</p>
        <p>The opening mateh in the loeal Intermediate League was played on the Wilderspool ground on Saturday, Heathside-and. Beamont being the opposing teams.</p>
        <p>Beamont were not at full strength, but, even if they had been, it is questionable whether the result would have been much different. Heathside were a bigger and speedier set, and flung the ball about in no half-hearted manner. Nine times in ten the ball came out of the " scrum " to the Heathside backs, with the re- sult that the opposition was often left guess- ing. Heathside's victory of twenty-two points to nil was well deserved, and it would be un- fair to individualise their players, for all did well.</p>
        <p>For the losing team, Whit&amp;ld worked like a, trojan and saved many difficult situations. He tackled keenly and kicked with great power. On the' right wing, Barlow did many good things, and Pembrton was the pick of the forwards.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-09-23_WidnesExaminer_art0132" n="art0132">
        <head>PLAY.</head>
        <p>THE PLAY.</p>
        <p>Heathside won the toss and elected to play with the sun at their backs. Pemberton started for Beamont, and the firit incident of note was a pass- ing .movement by the Heathside backs, which took them to the Beamont quarters. Here, however, they were penalised, and driven back to midfield. After some even play Forster received for Heath- side, and raced along the touch-line, but in attempt- ing to kick sent ,the ball out of play. From the scrummage the Heathside backs received and worked their way to the Beamont line. Here—some exciting play was witnessed, two or three scrim- mages being fought out, and finally the ball rolled dead. Heathside were having all the play, and the ball becoming loose, Dale dribbled over the Benmont line and scored the first try for Heathside, Harter missing the goal.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-09-23_WidnesExaminer_art0133" n="art0133">
        <head>Heathside in the Ascendant.</head>
        <p>Heathside in the Ascendant.</p>
        <p>On resuming the Beamont forwards went away with a rush, but Griffiths drove them back with-a good touch kick. The ball came out to the Heath- side backs, and after a passing bout Taylor ran in under the sticks, for Dale to •add the extra points. Following the restart, the Beamont threequarter backs started a, passing movement, but Wych missed the final pass in attempting to double in. A minute later a movement by the Heathside backs was checked in fine style by Whitfield. 'Heathside pressed hard, and Forster, receiving, ran well and scored what seemed to be a good trY, but the touch- judge ruled him "in touch." The Heathens, how- ever, would not be denied, and just before the in- terval Dolphin scored, but Dale failed to convert. Half-time: Heathside, one , goal three tries (11 points); Beamont, nil. It was not long before Heathside opened their second half account, for from another passing move- ment Dale forced himself over, but Harter failed to convert. Shortly afterwards Harter was served when in a good position, and, beating the full-back, scored the fifth try of the afternoon. but Gleave missed the goal. Shortly before the finish Harter again scored. Griffiths adding the extra points. Result: Heathside, two goals six tries (22 points); Beamont, nil.</p>
        <p>TO-DAY'S MATCH. Parochial v. Bcamont, on Bylancls's ground Kick-off 3.30. • Referee, Mr. G-. Crozier.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-09-23_WidnesExaminer_art0134" n="art0134">
        <head>ANGLING.</head>
        <p>ANGLING.</p>
        <p>GLADSTONE ARMS CONTEST.</p>
        <p>The anpilal contest from headquarters attracted an entry of 40. The match took place at Newbridge Salt Works end. The officials were on the job early, as they travelled to the scene by taxi, leaving noth- ing to chance, and they had everything in apple- pie order when the members arrived. The secre- tary soon had all at their numbers, and although the morning was not one of the pleasantest it did not damp the ardoiir of the members, for-,no sooner had " time " been called, than " landed " was also called in several places. There never was a lull during the contest, either one or another was calling out di.tring the whole two hours. This match is the record catch from Warrington this year, the lowest weight to receive a prize being 14oz. 13dr. It was generally thought during the match that it would be necessary to have over 11b. to get in the prizes. The weight caught was 351 b. 14oz. 10dr.• 37 mem- bers weighed in; the other two who took part in the match returned their fish to the' water before the weighers reached them, seeing they had no chance. The winner was again found in our old champion, F. Jolley. [Mr. Printer, this is the third pencil I have worn but writing "F. Jolley, Ist."] He received a beautiful oak clock and bronze horses, presented to the society by our patron, S. Donoghue, along with the " Mather " challenge cup, this being the sixth occasion he has held the Cup. Up to the present we cannot think of a member who can hold his own with him in the River Weaver, his weight being 21b. 7oz. Bdr. Other winners: 2, R. Dunbobbin, 21b. 9dr.; 3, E. 11b. 12oz. Bdr.; 4, F. Trigger, 11b. 10oz. 4dr.; 5, J. Green. 11b. Boz. Bdr.; 6 J. Preston, 11b. 7oz. sdr.; 7, T. Perry [first year's novice: Well done!], 11b. 50z.; 8, G. Hamblett. 11b. 3oz. 4dr.; 9, R. Cook, 11b. 30z.; 10, A. Swarbrick, 11b. 2oz. Bdr.; and so on down to 14oz. 13dr. What a splendid contest!</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-09-23_WidnesExaminer_art0135" n="art0135">
        <head>BAY HORSE MATCH.</head>
        <p>THE BAY HORSE MATCH.</p>
        <p>The contest from the above club WdS held at Norton on Saturday last, and drew record entry, 45 competing out of a possible 50. Although you cannot compare this water with the Waver for match' fishing, it always seems to draw a good entry. The officials of the Bay Horse were of the opinion that they would have good sport, as this length has been fishing up to the mark this last week or so. But they were unfortunate in catching it on or.c of its off days. The following day fix members filhed for two hours and took 101 b. of fish (what luck!), and on the Monday following A. Racey weighed in a roach Boz. 12dr. However, the officials can congratulate themselves on receiving such an entry. The winner was found in E. Hill, who seems to have taken the place of his churn (H. Moore, whit) is now on active service), as this is his second first in the canal this year; weight, 6oz. 13dr.; 2, J. Higham, 6 oz. 11dr. (and special fox largest fish); 3, P. Jolley, soz. 4dr. (and special for smallest fish) (Hill and Jolley tie for special for !nest fish); 4, E. Brookes, 40z sdr.; 5 and 6, W. Hewitt and G. Lilly, 3óz. 9dr.; 7, if. Brookes. 3oz. 4dr. • 8, T. Dixon, 30z.; 9, T. Roberts, 2oz. 11dr.; -10, W. Bailey, 2.0 z. 9dr.; and so on down to. loz. sdr.</p>
        <p>At the prize distribution the members, who ha'd invited the treasurer of the Warrington N.A., Mr. E. Brookes, to fish in their match before joining up in His Majesty's forces, took the opportunity of presenting him with a wrist watch, Mr. T. Ttoberts, the treasurer of, the Bay 'Horse, strapping it on his wrist and wishing him " God-speed " and the best luck.</p>
        <p>At this meeting it was decided to hold another contest at Norton in aid of members who are 'serv- ing in the Army, to provide them with comforts and things that are necessary for them, the first meeting to be, held at the Gladstone Arms on Monday next at 7.30 pm. The following are the officials selected to carry out the match: Chairman, Mr. J. Cox; treasurer, Mr. T. Roberts; secretaries, Mr. A. Stout and Mr. J. Potter; committee, Messrs. Fallon, Bate, Jolley, Bryers. Carter, jlolden, Hewitt, Hill, and another. Will all the above put in an appearance punctually, as no postcards will be sent out.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-09-23_WidnesExaminer_art0136" n="art0136">
        <head>ASSOCIATION. (Lancashire Combination.)</head>
        <p>ASSOCIATION. (Lancashire Combination.)</p>
        <p>THE USUAL GOAL.</p>
        <p>Town Go Down To South Liverpool In Stern Struggle.</p>
        <p>That only one goal hould be scored whenever Warrington Town and South Liverpool meet seems to be an edict of the Gods. On two Occa- sions before Saturday the South have managed to pull off a victory with the only goal of the match, and now it has happened again., The teams are to match themselves -against each other at the Dingle to-day, too, and it will be particularly interesting to know the result. There are some superstitious pedple, of course, but there are others, and Warrington goes to Liverpool full of confidence, lacking nothing in motaie and energy.</p>
        <p>Than the game at the Dingle last week I do not recollect a more stern .one. It was a struggle from start to finish and the 2,000 spectators had a sporting treat. Naturally, I was disappointed with the result, but it regis- tered the merits of the play. South Liverpool are a team to conjure with; they go for goal for all they are worth. They are not interested in the dainties of football, although, on occa- sions, they gave us episodes of the picturesque with their expert passing. From the kick-off— the ball was started by AsPinall—,it was obvious that a battle-royal was in the offing and War- rington engaged .with skill and determination.</p>
        <p>An unfortunate circumstance was the un- avoidable and unexpected absence of George Whitfield, causing Fahey to be played at centre-half. With George as the pivot, I think there 'would have been nioie openings. niade ; as it was, however, in the first half the attacks were of equal number, and pretty frequent, too. Glover, at right-half, though, was rather weak —too unsteady and erratic—yet one must not forget that on two or three momentous occa- sions, particularly in the second half, he showed ingenuity in stopping' attacks, and whisked the ball up the field soundly. Owen and Gregson also were generally ineffective. All are players of finosse, but against the South such tactics are hopeless. Opposed to them were-. stolid stalwarts and they want some handling. Owen got badly shaken on two or three occasions, and, I think, a little nervousness was engendered. .</p>
        <p>. That was the dominant weakness of the team. • Never at any .time, perhaps, were the forwards' brilliant, but, on the other hand, no palpable inefficiency brought tears. It was a fairly good line, even with Owen in his wrecked condition. The backs were splendid, and Kite was wonderful—the admired of the crowd, "a real hero he." And it was rather fortunate that Kite was astride his usual form. Very seldom when _South Liverpool shot was their aim far .out; often it was deadly accurate, and only the best of "goalies" could have defended their charges so well. He even saved a penalty shot, after about twenty minutes from the kick-off.' It was after Dawson and Parr had worked nicely Up the left, wing and Fahey had tripped the former in the penalty area. Bennett was entrusted with the free-kick, and, though shooting straight and fast, Kite's hands snatched the ball as in a vice, and, with .a bounce, it was away overhead. A director of South Liverpool told me that in his time Bennett has taken 31 penalty kicks, and this was the first one he has missed. Seven min- utes from time, when the South did score, Kite was in no wise to blame. By excellent com- bination. the . home -forwards had' got almost. within a stride—certainly within a hop, skip and a jump-P--of th 6 goalimmith, and a back failing not ingloricrusly—l think it was. Leak .-,the ball 'literally .ciiinbed' up , his figure,- over. his head, and, striking the. top of the net— the spectators yelled furiously at their victory. Though, I fancy, there was even then a sub-conscious feeling of sorrow that Kite had been beaten. for' they had an idep that Kite was about the best goalkeeper that was ever inade!</p>
        <p>There were yet seven minutes to go. For a considerable time Liverpool had been attacking sternly and skilfully—an offensive en one wing having failed, they would try the other, and then down the centre. It was thrilling to watch. Never were backs more sorely tried, and nearly tbe whole of the Warrington men fen back to prevent their comrades' immersion. But it was the • adoption of this defensive method which culminated in the missing of an equalising goal. Ditchfield had sneaked th ball in the course of its passage to the centrb and forthwith winged it to Siitdall, who, ;void- ing Smith, slung it over to Owen, and he, after a swift dribble forward slipped the ball well into centre and there were only a back and the goalkeeper for the five to beat. Unfortunately. that "five" were only four, however, for Aspinall, intent on defending, and, presumably none too hopeful of the outburst of the left wink, had not yet come up to the line, tind Jenkinson pounced on the ball and relieved the situation for the South in splendid style.</p>
        <p>For vigour South Liverpool cannot• be ex- celled in the League. They are not without subtlety either. In the latter half, when War- rington were facing a strong sun, Liverpool ballooned the ball continually in their attacks, and, our fellows, looking up, had difficulty in locating its flight. Liverpool knew what they were doing..</p>
        <p>As stated already, the game was intensely in- teresting; every one admitted it to be an excel- lent exposition of the game by botlk teams; and that a very large crowd will be at the Dingle to-day may be anticipated with certainty. The possibility of seeing another game like that. on Saturday last is not .to be missed without good reason.</p>
        <p>The teams were</p>
        <p>Warrington: 'Kite; Leah and Millward; Glover, Fahey and Ditehfield; Gregson, SiddalL Aspinall, Giblin and Owen. South Liverpool: Moore; Weldin and Jenkin- son; Smith, Ted Williams and Owen Williams; Hall, Hannaway, Bennett, Dawson and Parr.</p>
        <p>A strong team is going to South Liverpool to-day on the 1.50 from the Central Station. The players to be selected from are: Kite; Leah, Cookson and Milward; Heywood (Manchester United), Whitfield and Bullen; Aspinall, Siddall, Rubotham, Giblin and Owen.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-09-23_WidnesExaminer_art0137" n="art0137">
        <head>WARRINGTON AND DISTRICT JUNIOR LEAGUE.</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON AND DISTRICT JUNIOR LEAGUE.</p>
        <p>An the clubs in the above competition were engaged last week, and it is very evident froin the heavy scoring in some of the games that some of the Juniors have not been long in get- ting into their stride. No fewer 'than 27 goals were sco'red during the afternoon. The winning teams claim 23 send the losers had only four in their favour.</p>
        <p>The St.' Elphin's played their first match on their new ground, and had Higbeebl Juniors as visitors. At the' close of play they were returned easy winners byl seven goals against one.</p>
        <p>The Bewsey Adult School team Visited Victoria Park for the second week in .succession, but on this occasion they were in opposition to the Cadets. As both these teams were defeated the previous week a keen game was afiticipated, but the Bewsoy lads had come with a determination to wipe out the defeat of their previous visit, and were successful in scoring four )times in each half. The Cadets Must have been non. p/ussed by the performanT of their opponnts, as they were. only able to respond on one occasion. On the whole the display of the Cadets was disappointing. For the visitors Bradburn was in fine shooting form, claiming four of the goals, and Roylance (2), Fear and Schofield completed the total.</p>
        <p>A Niery hard game was witnessed between the Recs. and Orford Juniors, the latter showing finer combination and were better target shooters, scored on four occasions, whilst the Recs. only replied once. Lockhart, despite the score against him, saved many fine shots, and</p>
        <p>he received good assistance from Willcocks it back. The Juniors gave a. more evenly balanced nisplay and Witton was at his best at centre- half. Fitzgerald, Hurst and Belshaw were prominent in the front line, and Doughty was good in defence.</p>
        <p>The Penketh Juniors surpassed their own •supporters ip their opening home fixture against Howley -United, and sustained defeat by four goals to one. This reverse was very dis- appointing to the home supporters, as the pre- vious week in their opening fixture on foreign soil they gained a very easy vietory over the Highfield Juniors. The meeting: with Howley was looked forvard to very confidently. There- fore the reverse was a great surprise to all con- nected with the club. Every credit must be given to the Howley lads for their victory, which was well-earned, as they were easily the better team on the day's play, and the honours of victory might be equally shared by all the team. Pearson, Glover, Thomasson and Moss were the yick of the home team.</p>
        <p>During the progress of the match a very re- grettable accident happened to Watson, of the home team. It was during an attack by the visitors that he fell to the ground with his arm 'under him, and it was found that he had sus- tained a broken wrist. Everybody was very sympathetic with the unfortunate player, and wish him a speedy recovery.</p>
        <p>Owing to the. war, -which has caused a great amount of uncertainty of being able to get sufficient players to form a team, the Emmanuel and the Warrington Central Clubs, who have been accustomed to play in the Vi Aoria lark for several seasons, and were only able to . get a team together as the football season com- menced, were; owing to the circumstances, com- pelled to delay making their application to the Parks Committee for permission to play in Victoria Park again. They have received re- plies that their applications are too late, as the grounds have already - been allotted to the Juniors. As the playing mentbers of these teams are engaged on munition work their applications should be reconsidered by the Parks Committee - and, additional.. space be. granted-. to .them, as there is one point which is greatly in the clubs' favour; that accommodation is .made for four Rugby grounds and only tw.3 for the Association . game, and amongst the juniors the Association game is more keenly followed than the Rugby game.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-09-23_WidnesExaminer_art0138" n="art0138">
        <head>VND DISTRICT 31 MOH ‘GUE.</head>
        <p>VND DISTRICT 31 MOH ‘GUE.</p>
        <p>P. W. L. D. F. A. PTS Howley United St. Elphin's Bewsey A.S. Penketh Juniors Orford Juniors Latchford • Recs. Latchford Cadets Highfield</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-09-23_WidnesExaminer_art0139" n="art0139">
        <head>PENKETH JUNIORS v. HOWLEY UNITED.</head>
        <p>PENKETH JUNIORS v. HOWLEY UNITED.</p>
        <p>On the Recreation ground. Penketh Juniors: J. Moss; Newall, Thomasson; White, Watson, Boyle; Magee, Glover, Pearson, Ainsworth, Finch. Howley United: Walters; Rowlinson, Foster; Atherton, Miller, Hind; Boyle, Rylance, Davies, Woodhouse, Humphries.</p>
        <p>Referee, Mr. P. Gosling. The visitors won the toss and played with the wind at their backs, and also the slope in their favour. Pearson set the ball rolling, and at once advanced to the visitors' goal, where the effort was spoiled by Finch being in an off-side position. From the ,free kick the visitors were enabled to make an assault on the home goal, where Newall and Thomasson defended finely. Some keen play was witnessed. and Miller, for the visitors, checked many attempts of the home forwards, and play was transferred to the home goal again, when a corner kick was conceded. This was well placed, and some lively exchanges took place in the home goalmouth, Davies finally hooking the ball between the uprights and opening the score for the`visitors. Encouraged by this success the visitors again attacked the home citadel very hotly and increased their lead. A few minutes later slack play by the home defenders allowed Boyle to get through and put the visitors further ahead. Halftime: Howley United. 3 goals; Penlath Juniors, nil. —The- homesters were quickly in , evidence in the second half, and .after several efforts had been re- pulsed by the visitors' backs, Pearson made a splendid individual effort and scored the best goal of the match for the home team. From the centre kick the visitors became aggressive, and the home backs were severely tested. A penalty kick was given for hands, and Woodhouse failed to add the points. The home team were handicapjPed by losing the services of Watson for the remainder of the game, the latter unfortunately breaking his wrist. The visitors now had the best of the exchanges, and Moss was cheered by the spectators for his fine goal- keeping. The home goal was captured again before the call of time. Result: 'Howley United, 4 goals; Penketh Juniors, 1 goal.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-09-23_WidnesExaminer_art0140" n="art0140">
        <head>LATCHFORD CADETS v. BEWSEY A.S.</head>
        <p>LATCHFORD CADETS v. BEWSEY A.S.</p>
        <p>In the Victoria Park, before a fair crowd of spectators.</p>
        <p>Cackets: Oreenhalgh; Williams, Jones; Porter, Massey, Cook; Cartwright, Ashton, Smith, Mayors, Gibson.</p>
        <p>Bewsey A.S.: 'Perks; 'Main, Stephens; Worral, Kent, Dickenson.; Hassell, Roylance, Bradburn, Fear, Schofield. Refe.ree, Mr. R. Williams.</p>
        <p>The opening exchanges were keenly tontested, and each goal NVas visited without any success. After ten minutes' play the Bewsey forwards combined well together, and Bradburn beat Greenhalgh with a good shot. The visitors were quickly on the aggressive again, but good play by Williams and Jones relieved the situation. Then followed ,a brief 'visit to the visitors' goal, where Main and Stephens proved too good for the home forwards. Bradburn again beat Greenhalgh, and this was followed with a third from Roylance. From the centre kick the Cadets made a strong rally, but Perks defied their efforts, and they had to retreat in defence of their' own goal. where Royla.nce wait successful again. Half-time: Bewsey A.S., 4 goals; Cadets, 1 goal. The pecond half opened in favour of the Cadets, and several determined efforts were repulsed by the visitors' defence, and following a spell of midfield play. Gibson broke away on the right and swung the ball over to Cartwright. The latter centred finely. for Smith to put the ball past Perks. After this the Cadets had to play on the defensive, and in suite of their efforts further goals were stored by Bradburn, Hassell. Fear and Schofield. Result: Bewsey A.S., 8 g,oahr; Latchford Cadets, 1 goal.</p>
        <p>LATCHFORD RECS. V. ORFORD JUNIORS.</p>
        <p>In Victoria, Park</p>
        <p>Latchford Recs.: G. Lockhart; Cooper, Willcocks; Trainor, Guest, Fairbrother; James, Raymond, Green, Hesson, Dutton.</p>
        <p>lOrford Juniors: J. Walters; Griffiths, Doughty;_ Jones, Witton, Austin,;„ Belsha.w, Clare, Hurst, Fitzgerald, Roughley.</p>
        <p>Referee Mr. Burgess _ _</p>
        <p>The Recs. at once visited the visitors' goal area, where Doughty relieved with a big kick, which en- abled the Juniors to transfer play to the home goal. Lockhart had to save shots in quick succession from Belshaw, Hurst and Fitzgerald. Even 'play was followed by another attack by the Juniors, when Austin opened their score with a hard drive. The Juniors were having the best of the game, and from a good centre by Fitzgerald. Witton obtained second goal. The Recs. adopted kick-and-rush tac- tics, and Green tested Walters with a long shot, but the latter proved very safe. A little later the game was being contested in the Recs.' half, and Lockhart, coming out of goal to meet a long shot, missed the ball, and Hurst had not much Oifficultv in scoring. Half-time: Orford, 3 goals; Recs., nil. Resuming, the Recs, played in determined fashion and were soon pegging away at the visitors' goal. Willcocks hitting the upright with a fast shot, but directly, after Green made amends by scoring for the Recs., The Orford lads combined well, and from:a good centre by Belshaw, Hurst was only a few inches wide with a low shot. Fitzgerald was playing well for the Juniors, and making a neat pass to Roughley, he enabled the latter to beat the home- backs and score the visitors' fourth goal. After midfield play Walters had to save a good shot from Jaines, and at t,'‘e opposite end Cooper intercepted Belshaw, when in a dangerous position. A well-placed. corner kick by Roughley was placed over the bar by -Witton. Result: Orford Juniors. 1 goals; Latchford Recs., 1 goal.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-09-23_WidnesExaminer_art0141" n="art0141">
        <head>FRIENDLY MATCH.</head>
        <p>FRIENDLY MATCH.</p>
        <p>SANKCY ATHLETIn v. BRUNSWICK VILLA (EARLESTOWN).</p>
        <p>The above clubs opened their season's fixtures last week with a friendly encounter on the Atherton Quay enclosure.</p>
        <p>Sankey Athletic: Gleave; .Holden. Erinksman; Ashurst: Shaw, Worrall; W. Taylor, Hunt Lister, E._Taylor, Kenyon.</p>
        <p>_ Brunswick Villa: Lawrence; Winders, Rodgers; Jackson, Walker, Vizard; Derricott, Hughes, Robotham, Collins, McDermott.</p>
        <p>The opening exchanges were very even, and then the visitors assumed the aggressive. Robotham opened the score for them from a Penalty kick. The home team made several spirited attempts on the visitors' goal, but failed to break through the de- fence of Winders and Rodgers, and in a few min- utes the home team were defending very stub- bornly, Gleave and Holden clearing smartly on siveral occasions. Good combination from midfield by the visitors resulted in Collins registering their</p>
      </div>
