      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-09-23_WidnesExaminer_art0148" n="art0148">
        <head>EXAMINER, SATURDAY, SEPTEMBER 23, 1916. THANKS FOR THE HARVEST. BLIND MUSICIANS.</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER, SATURDAY, SEPTEMBER 23, 1916. THANKS FOR THE HARVEST. BLIND MUSICIANS.</p>
        <p>Wanted. ROUGH-HAIRED FOX DOG Wanted.— • Fern's Cafe, 75, Chapel-street, Salford.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-09-23_WidnesExaminer_art0149" n="art0149">
        <head>Churches Express Gratitude In Prayer And Song.</head>
        <p>Churches Express Gratitude In Prayer And Song.</p>
        <p>Thanks for the good harvest has been the theme of the services at several Warrington and district churches during the week and at the week-end.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-09-23_WidnesExaminer_art0150" n="art0150">
        <head>BEWSEY ROAD WESLEYAN.</head>
        <p>BEWSEY ROAD WESLEYAN.</p>
        <p>On Sunday the Bewsey-road Wesleyan Church held their harvest festival, and there was an abundance of gifts all tastefully arranged. In the morning the preacher was the Rev. A. J. Morton and in the evening the Pastor the Rev. W. T. Derry). The hymns sung were all appropriate, as also wer% the prayers offered. At the evening service the anthem "Praise the Lord, 0 Jerusalem" (Maunder) was sung by the choir; Mi.. Al E. Baker 'taking the solo part in praiseworthy style. Mr. -George Pendlebury presided at the organ. On Monday the gifts •fruit were sold for the benefit of the poor.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-09-23_WidnesExaminer_art0151" n="art0151">
        <head>ST. ANN'S.</head>
        <p>ST. ANN'S.</p>
        <p>" Harvest feslkival services in connection with St. •Ann's Church were field on Thursday, when the_Vicar, the Rev. James Davies, apd the Rev. J. R. Jones, vicar of Great Sankey, conducted the service, the "latter delivering the sermon. The musical part of the service contained the anthem, "Praise the Lord, 0 my soul" (Maunder), the solo in this being taken by Master H. Desley, who sang with a full and clear voice. Mr. W. B. Randles was at the organ. The services will be continued to- morrow.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-09-23_WidnesExaminer_art0152" n="art0152">
        <head>ST. PETER'S.</head>
        <p>ST. PETER'S.</p>
        <p>Harvest thanksgiving services were com- menced at St. Peter's Church on Thursday evening, when the special preacher was the Rev. Horace Gray, headmaster of the Bateler Grammar School. Special music was rendered in an efficient mariner by the choir, Mr. E. H. Padmore being at the organ. Simper's " Mag- nificat " and " Nunc Dimittis " were sung, as well as J. Barnby's anthem, " 0 Lord how manifold." Collections were taken on behalf of special church expenses. The services will be continued to-morrow (Sunday), when the preacher morning and evening will be the Rev. H. S. Bolton, vicar. of Ravenhead, and, in the afternoon, the Rev. T. M. Armour, curate, of St. Elphin's.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-09-23_WidnesExaminer_art0153" n="art0153">
        <head>LEGH-STREET PRIMITIVE.</head>
        <p>LEGH-STREET PRIMITIVE.</p>
        <p>The harvest festival services in connection with Legh-street Primitive Methodist Church were held on Sunday. The preacher was Rev. T. H. Hunt, of Southport. In the afternoon a sacred cantata, "A. song of, thanksgiving" (Maunder), was rendered by the choir, under the conductorship of Mr. A. Spruce. Mr. F. Gandy presided and gave an interesting address. Solos were rendered by Mrs. C. Worthington, Mrs. A. E.' Hoyle, Miss M. Jones, Mr. W. Tynan and Mr.. J. Dakin. • At the evening service the choir gave a good interpretation of Sullivan's anthem, "Hearken unto Me, My people," and Mr. Dakin sang " Christis Salvator " With ability. Mr. T. ,Tanner presided at the organ with his usual taste and judgment. On Mon- day evening a fruit banquet was held, when songs were given by Mrs. Hoyle :and iMiss Hamer, and recitations by hliss Edwards. Mr. J. F. Rowlands presided and the Rey. A. J. Chessum gave an address.. A sale of fruit and vegetables concluded the meeting.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-09-23_WidnesExaminer_art0154" n="art0154">
        <head>STOCKTON HEATH lAIESLEYAN.</head>
        <p>STOCKTON HEATH lAIESLEYAN.</p>
        <p>Harvest festival services were held in Stock- ton Heath Wesleyan Church on Sunday. Mr. M. H. Llewellyn preached at' the morning ser- vice, and the Rev. John Gunnell at the even- ing. In the afternoon a young people's service was held, when Mr. H. Brereton presided. Solos and recitations were given by the scholars,. and an address was delivered by the Rev. Frederick Hudson, the newly-appointed minister to War- rington Circuit. The church was beautifully decorated. Special anthems were sung by the choir at all the services, under the conductor- ship of Mr, F. Penketh, choirmaster. Mrs. G. Gould was organist. The fruit, flowers and vegetables were afterwards forwarded to the Raddon Court Hospital for the wounded soldiers, and the gifts were greatly appreciated. On Monday night there was a fruit banquet and social gathering in the schoolroom, which was largely attended. Songs were given by Miss Houghton and recitations by Mr. J. Sanson and Mrs. Percy meson. Refreshments were then served, after which the evening was spent in games.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-09-23_WidnesExaminer_art0155" n="art0155">
        <head>PADGATE WESLEYAN CHURCH.</head>
        <p>PADGATE WESLEYAN CHURCH.</p>
        <p>The harvest festival services in connection with the Padgate Wesleyan Church were held on Sunday, the preacher in the morn- ing being Mr. J. Upson, of Warrington. Speak- ing from Hebrews NH., 11, he said that we are not to test or judge God's purposes by the process, but by the complete results. Why have not all the prayers been answered to end this great conflict? God is producing and perfect- tag all that is best in us.. We must have greater trust and confidence in God and learn the great lesson that is taught us, by the har,7 vest.</p>
        <p>In the afternoon a children's service was held, conducted by the Rev. A. J. Morton. His chief thought was thankfulness to all who at any time did anything to help us; thanks to parents and teachers for kindnesses showri and thank- fulness to God for the .many benefits which we enjoy.</p>
        <p>th-e Rev. A. J. Morton also conducted the evening service, and speaking from Genesis viii., 22, he pointed out that gold is all right for its purchasing powers, but if there is no corn to purchase it is useless. He also emphasised the unspeakable worth of thA farmer. The anthems "Angel voices ever singing " (E. Vine Hall) and "Praise the Lord, ye heavens adore Him" (Edmund Turner), were sung by the choir, Mr. W. H. Ward officiating at the organ. The church had been very taste- fully decorated for the occasion with fruits and flowers, which were afterwards distributed amongst the wounded soldiers at the Barracks and Whitecross Hospitals.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-09-23_WidnesExaminer_art0156" n="art0156">
        <head>_ CONSCIENTIOUS OBJECTOR.</head>
        <p>_ CONSCIENTIOUS OBJECTOR.</p>
        <p>Handed Over To The Military At Stockton Heath.</p>
        <p>Yesterday, before .a Special Court at Stockton Heath, composed of Messrs. James Hepherd and George Dakin, a young man, about 25 years of age, named Stanley Hillsborough Bodle, of Oaklands, Hunt's-lane, Latchford Without, was charged in custody with having failed to report. • Defendant Pleaded "Not Guilty."</p>
        <p>Police-Constable Kelford stated that on the 30th May he received a notice from the Army authorities saying that the prisoner had been absent without leave since the 23rd May. He went to his home and saw his Mother, who said that he had left -home: . She showed witness a letter written by the prisoner, in which he stated that he was leaving home in ,order to avoid military Service and that he, would keep away until the Government recognised the conscientious objector. The previous night -witness met defendant in Hunt's-lane and asked him if he had anything to show. Why he was not in the Army. He replied: "No; r suppose you know I am a constientious objector and as such cannot recognise the military rultis." 'He admitted having received two notices to join Uri. Witness arrested him and brought him to the lock-up. Defendant said that what the witness had stated was, quite correct. Captain Luck, Chester Castle, gave, evidence -as to the service of notice calling defendant up. Defendant returned the notice with a letter -in the terms referred to by previous 'Witness. Sergeant Wilding said the prisoner had given the authorities a great deal of trotkble. Defendant said that according to the law he should be handed over to the. civil authorities. The Chairman said he was in the hands of the military authority at the present ,moment. Defendant said he ;could not take up Military duties, as it was against the -principles in which he had been brought up. The Chairman asked where he had been brought up. Defendant mentioned the International Bible Students' Association, who say that the whole teaching of the Bible' is onoc.se(no _war.</p>
        <p>TheChOrnian: You will Jive to do your duty like everyone eNe.</p>
        <p>I)efenl'ant: I am doing my duty, I -- The Chaiiman: You are not. You .should join the Colours. Defendant was then fined 40s. and handed Over to the military authorities.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-09-23_WidnesExaminer_art0157" n="art0157">
        <head>Unique Concert En Parr Hall Last Night.</head>
        <p>Unique Concert En Parr Hall Last Night.</p>
        <p>EXQUISITE PERFORMANCE</p>
        <p>Our greatest orator could not make a more striking appeal on behalf of our blinded heroes than the concert given in the Parr Hall last evening by a number of blind musicians. The occasion was unique, bearing in mind the object of the entertainment, and it is little won- der that there were few vacant seats: The blind pleaded for the blind, through the irresistible medium of Music, and what heart could remain unmoved by such .powerful eloquence? The National Institute for tbe Blind sent forth of ith best to‘ arouse and solidify sympathy on behalf of the stricken hi- mates of St. Dunstan's Hostel for our blinded soldiers and ailors'—men from whom the visual ,delights of this world have been blotted out for ever.</p>
        <p>c- A pathetic note was struck is each per- former was gently led on the platform in 'front of the audience; but the tragedy of our blinded warriors was only half displayed. These musi- cians live in another world than ours. Some- one has said, "The brightest star shines in the darkest hour "—the star of hope. St. Dunstan's Hostel will open up new ambitions to those whose existence has suddenly become ,a blank. It is comparatively easy to-understand that :the Braille system. has developed musicians, but the natural gifts must be present, and this fact was demonstrated by our friends last evening. We had a blind composer, Mr. W. Wolstenholme, Mus.Bac. (Oxon), who delighted the audience with some peculiarly characteristic pianoforte solos. They were instructive as being wholly suggestive of sound. There was no sight colour; Witness the descriptive morceau, "The Tor- rent." His attitude at the instrument, his delicacy, precision and accuracy of touch were extraordinary when one remembered that the performer was blind. Most praiseworthy also were the violin selec- tions by Mr. Ernest Whitfield, who, although he walked on to the stage by himself, is almost blind and only able to distinguish between light and darkness. The violin, however, might naturally be considered the readiest and corn- pletest interpreter of a blind musician's soul. It was amply proved by his rendering of Saint- Saens's pretty composition, "Introduction and Rondo," with double stopping, and Sarasate's lively "Gipsy Melodies:" Of the vocalists there 'is nothing to say but praise. • Whether as soloists or in concert they sang most effectively. Miss Ada Jackson (soprano) .was loudly encored for her exquisite rendering •bf "_The Swallows" (Coen), and Mr. Angus Brown, who contributed "111 'sing thee songs of Araby" in faultless style, gave "Annie 'Laurie" as a r,e-call. Mr. E. E. W. Littlewood (baritone) and Miss Ada Thornewell (contralto) were no less •successful in their selections and were the recipients of well- merited applause. The concluding quartet, "Our Empire in Arms" (arranged by Collard), was a fine-spirited vocalistic.effort. The accompanist was Miss Ethel Bartlett, and Mr. Avalon Collard ably direoted•the\concert. During the interval Mr. Avalon Collard gave a short interesting address on the work of Our Blinded Soldiers and Sailors at St. Dunstan's Hostel, illustrated by lantern slides.</p>
        <p>Tke artistes and audience were thanked by the Mayor (Alderman Peacock) and Sir Arthur Crosfield, ;Bart., the latter remarking, in addition, that he and Lady Crosfield had known Sir Arthur and Lady Pearson for many years, and had taken a deep interest in the work the eminent blind Baronet had carried on at St. Dunstan's, and it was at his suggestion to him (the speaker) that that con- cert was held. They had certainly had a great musical treat, and he hoped they would have similar one again. (Applause.) In coming that evening they had certainly been supporting one of the best institutions in the country. (Ap- plause.)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-09-23_WidnesExaminer_art0158" n="art0158">
        <head>PILGRIMAGE OF THE ENIPIRE.</head>
        <p>A PILGRIMAGE OF THE ENIPIRE.</p>
        <p>By Sir Arthur Crosfield, Bart. London: Eyre and Spottiswode, Ltd. 2s. 6d. net; cloth, is. This is a call to arms to England and to the Empire. "I have endeavoured," says the author in a very unnecessary apologia, "to convey some sense of the magnitude of all we are striving for"; and he has succeeded. Not one of the 139 stanzas but abounds with patriotism of peculiar dignity—even austerity. After the brief emotional passage with which the poem opens, the, appeal is more to the reason than to the impulse; and there is con- sequently disclosed a remarkable breadth of out- look in all branches of history and art. There are no bypath 4 to be wandered; the theme throughout is adhered to with an almost ruth- less concentration. Indeed, brevity would appear to have_been the illoet's one desire: "If, reader, it would seem that _we have tarried," he interjects after a passage to which one is tempted to apply his own phrase: Strains of a colour symphony sublime.</p>
        <p>• .) The Spensermn metre is, of course, one beset with pitfalls: yet in this respect there are few slips in the "Pilgrimage," the scansion being consistently simple except in Stanza 19, where spondee and iambus alternate with e quaint effect. On the other hand there occur passages of considerable worth:</p>
        <p>No palace, temple, amphitheatre, dome On which the toiling multitudes have spent Vast labour In grandeur and in dignity compare With Kar Nak : here 'tis poetry hath lent Repose and pathos; come in the night air. Reader, to view a scene so eloquent and fair. The plea for Greece's Alliance is extremely powerful, for here the poet is in his element: Greece and her destiny were hid by night: Lo! Venizelos spake and all was light.</p>
        <p>In statesmanship and courage his career With loftiest traditions cloth' invite Noble comparison; a duty clear He demonstrtites for those who hold their honour dear.</p>
        <p>" The Pilgrimage of the Empire," which is dedicated to all "who in the great war are, or have been, 'fighting the battle of the Empire." is, beautifully illustrated with a series of rare views in. British dominions and in the Mother Country itself, printed upon special leaves. The binding is admirably executed in dark cloth with gdld lettering. "A Pilgrimage of the Empire" is obtainable at all booksellers and iailway bookstalls. The entire profits arising from the sale of the book will be given to Sir Arthur Pearson's fund for those who have lost their eyesight in the war.'</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-09-23_WidnesExaminer_art0159" n="art0159">
        <head>PENKETH.</head>
        <p>PENKETH.</p>
        <p>Village Soldier on Wondehs of India.—Private Leonard Smith, son of Mr. James and Mrs. Smith, Victoria-road, writing home, tells ,of the wonderful scenery he has lately been marching through in the Mysore district of India. The tropical vegetation, . so utterly opposite to our Lancashire flora, has 'held him spellbound . with its vastness and beauty. As his battalion were the first representatives of the British Army that most of the natives had ever -seen, their curiosity and excitement can be easily imagined. On one or two occasions they. had to dig down through the sand to obtain water for much-needed ablutions, but the liquid when reached proved .to be opaque and exceedingly dirty:- The one great excite- ment which will ever live in ,his memory was when His Highness Maharaja of Mysore sent an invitation to the whole of the battalion to be his -guest S for a day. The usual review was held, followed by the 'march past in various formations. When these military' functions were over the Maharaja's palace,- *ith its un- dreamed of brillianee; was thrown open to offi- cers and men for inspection. The magnificent building suddenly burst into a blaze of glorious light; some 15,0Q0 electric globes shone forth. With feelings of awe the party inspected gal- leries of choice. marble, ceilings beautifully moulded, delicately tinted, and gilded with per- fect workmanship; colours so perfectly blended and so softly shaded (seemed above the work of than); saw doors of sandalwood and silver, in- comparably carved and - chased; and went through the throne room into a state room, which held every eye spellbound with sheer delight; not forgetting a cosy music' room, re- plete with a grand piano . and a magnificent organ. Small wonder they came out of the palace amazed and excited, which ended in one and all giving vent to their feelings in the good old British way.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-09-23_WidnesExaminer_art0160" n="art0160">
        <head>WOMAN corroN SPINNER</head>
        <p>WOMAN corroN SPINNER</p>
        <p>Sues Employers Unsuccessfully At County Court.</p>
        <p>On Thursday, in the County Court, his Honour Judge Scott Fox heard an action in which Mrs. Beatrice Galvin, of 47, Eldon- street, Warrington, cotton spinner, sought for' compensation from • Messrs. Armitage and Rigby, Cockhedgo Mills, in respcct of injuries sustained by her pn the 26th May. The re- spondents denied liability on the ground that the injuries were not received in !Le course of applicant's employment. Mr. Spafford, barrigter (instructeil by Mr. C. H. Unsworth, Warrington), appeared for applicant, and Mr. Wingate Saul, barrister (in- structed by Mr. John Taylor, Manchester), represented the respondents. Mr. Spafford said that applicant, a married woman, had ,been employed by respondents, and was a cotton spinner. She was what was called "three-sider," meaning that she worked two sides of one loom and one side of another loom. Her wages were 12s. 4d. a week. Amongst her duties was that of keeping her machine clean. For that purpose an instrument was providett, which was passed along the frame. Over the cog-wheel at the end of the frame there was an iron cover, which could be raised. This system of cleaning by " wipers " had been only lately introduced , into the mill. Having done the wiping, it was necessary to raise this cover to take out the brush. About 12 o'clock on the 26th May applicant was cleaning the frame, and when she lifted the box with her left hand she saw a bobbin which had got to the cog-wheel. In trying to get at the bobbin her, right hand slipped on the cog-whels and the ends of the second and third fingers were caught. She went to the Infirmary and it was found neces- sary to amputate two of her fingers—the tip of one finger and the top of the other down to the second joint. She must have been a very courageous young woman because; an hour after the operation she came down to the mill awl saw, Mr. Ainswiorth, the manager, and Jim Hindes, the foreman. . - .</p>
        <p>In reply to his Honour, Counsel said that applicant had not gone back to work and the Judge said that in that case the claim would be for half wages, 6s. 2d., until further order. Applicant, who is 23 years of age, stated that she had been a spinner since she was fifteen. It was the minder's business to clean the machine. She had always been told • to keep necessary things out of the cog-wheels which happened to fall in. Mr. Spafford : What do you mean by neces- sary things ?—Anything that might cauN,,, damage. If the bobbin had 'gone into the cog-wheel what would have 'happened P—There would be a breakdown and I would have had to make an explanation.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-09-23_WidnesExaminer_art0161" n="art0161">
        <head>Applicant Cross-examined.</head>
        <p>Applicant Cross-examined.</p>
        <p>Applicant said that on going back to the mill she saw Mr. Ainsworth, the manager, and he said she had no right to lift the cover up. She replied that Jim Hindes was always complain- ing to her about lost things being in the wheels, and Mr. Ainsworth said "Don't mind blaming it on Jim Hindes."</p>
        <p>In cross-examination by Mr. Saul, applicant said the biabbin was under the axle and not under the wheel. While, as a matter of fact, it was not necessary to lift the cover to get the wiper out, because it could be done by break- ing the last thread, they did not break the last thread. They were shown how to lift the covers by Mr. Hindes: the cover was sometimes lifted up if the wheels were squeaking, and the over- looker would be sent for.</p>
        <p>Mrs. Rutter, who had worled at the mill for about 12 years, also gave evidence. They were always told to keep the covers clear of any articles. If a bobbin chanced to get under the cover she would have taken it out; otherwise it would have cauSed damage to the frame. There was no notice posted up about the covers. Mr. Saul: Was it your ,business to fetch Hindes if you heard squeaking F—We should find out where the squeaking was first.</p>
        <p>What the Overlooker Said.</p>
        <p>The only witness called for the respondents was Mr. James Hinde, who had been in the position of overlooker in the mills for about 15 years. These wipers were not in use when applicant came into the employment. Those who started in the mill were told with regard to this particular thing that on no occasion were they to lift these covers to any extent any higher than it was necessary to get the wiper out. If the machine squeaked it was not their place to lift the cover, their duty was to stop the machine, because they had no idea what it was that was wrong. It would be a breach of duties, even if there was squeaking,. to lift up the cover. In all his experience he never knew of a bobbin getting under the cover. The only thing be knew of getting under the cover was a cleaning cloth, and then they should stop the frame to take it Out. By Mr. Spafford : It was left to the discretion of the girls whether they stopped the frame or reported it to him. He had never actually seen any girl open a gear box. It Was their duty to keep the boxes free, by senceng down the cleaning cloths. Mr. Saul (for the respondents) said that the box guard in question was put there as required by the Factory and Workshops' Act, by which employers are obliged to fence off securely any dangerous part of machinery. Counsel sub- mitted that the accident did not arise out of 4.r in the course of the applicant's employment. He asked his Honour to say that Mrs. Galvin knew that she had no right to lift this cover. and that it was her duty to stop the machine and report the matter._ .</p>
        <p>his Honour said that he should come to the conclusion that his award must be for the respondents. There was a ,concensus of evidence that speaking generally,. these women were required not to touch this box at all. In; his opinion applicant had no obligation or excuse in regard to her duty to her employer upon such an occasion, or for such a reason to do what was against the whole rule and practice of the establishment and the whole purpose of the machinery, to insert her fingers into the jaws of this dangerous place. In his opinion the applicant's case failed. His award was. therefore, for the respondents. Mr. Saul said his clients did not ask for costs.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-09-23_WidnesExaminer_art0162" n="art0162">
        <head>STAGE AND KINEMA.</head>
        <p>STAGE AND KINEMA.</p>
        <p>Royal Court Theatre. In these latter months, since Mr. Harry War- burton has been the manager, we have had a succession of interesting productions, well varied. .As a domestic drama, "The Little Grey- Home in the Wt," which is being staged this week by Mr. and Mrs. Ernest Vatighan's com- pany, has had few more successful rivals.</p>
        <p>I saw it on the night it was first produced</p>
        <p>at the theatre of the authoress, Mrs. F. G. Kimberley, and had the pleasure of writing a column notice of it for "The Stage." It was, therefore, as a renewal of old acquaintanceship that I met Mr. Vaughan the evening. For four years Mr. Vaughan and his wife, Miss Madge Douglas, have been touring Mrs.- Kim- berley's plays. They were here someotime ago in the "Prairie Outhyw," and the good 'impres- sion they gave then is maintained in "The Little Grey .Home in the West." Miss '.Douglas is a player of personality, and it was to suit her individuality, particularly, that Mrs. ,Kim- berley wrote the one now in possession. at the Royal Court. Dainty, demure and spirited, she makes ,a charming and fascinating Peggy,. •</p>
        <p>•, The company itself,-as well as the scenery, is of excellent quality. Mr. Vaughan himself knows how to act and run a play. For a long time he was with thg Irish players. Seven or eight years ago he wits manager of the famous Abbey Theatre, Dublin. As for his speech, if he wanted to convince anyone that he wasn't Irish, he would have to do it in writing. has the brogue beautifully.</p>
        <p>I have pleasure in announcing that Mr. John Foster Fraser, the brilliant journalist and lecturer, is coming to give. two war talks on a Sunday afternoon and evening in October. Most people know the raciness and knowledge with which he invests his writings, and, having heard him lecture on several occasions, I may say that they will find .him as interesting to listen to as he is to read.</p>
        <p>Next week and hereafter twmatifiees a week will be given at the Royal Court—on Mondays and Thursdays. A better start off in the quality of plays and players need not be desired than Mr. Raynor and his repertoire. The plays he intends submitting are: Monday ,matinee and Saturday night, "A Bunch of Violets "; Mon- day and Wednesday nights,' 'mile Dancing Girl "; Tuesday and Thursday afternoon and evening, "Moths"; and on Friday , night, "Lady Huntworth's Experiment."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-09-23_WidnesExaminer_art0163" n="art0163">
        <head>Palace and Hippodrome.</head>
        <p>Palace and Hippodrome.</p>
        <p>The Hippodrome programmes have one special feature. They do not come within the strictures which we have recently read in the Pres. The turns are bright and inoffensive. Prominent this week is the turn of 'oden Williams, who .entertains with song and story. His songs and patriotic pieces are all original, while his problem 'play skit is a neat bit of work. He is clever and refined as well as original.</p>
        <p>Mr. Williams has many happy memories of Warrington, where he has quite a number of friends'. lie was intimately connected with the, town ,not many years ago, when he acted as professional coach for the Operatic Society., Under his able directorship the society per- formed a number of operas with signal success, notably "The Pirates of Penzance" and "The Yeomen of the Guard."</p>
        <p>In common with some of the best of Our platform entertainers he has delighted numer- ous audiences and followed their lead some years ago by appearing on the music-hall stage. ,He has been fortunate in so doing, as he has found his houses most appreciative and his experience up to the present has been one of continued success. He has been engaged for the whole, of the Macnaghten tour. Mr. Wil- liams has happy recollections of being person- ally congratulated by the late Sir Henry Irving, before whom he recited at the Manchester Arts Club.</p>
        <p>Fred Mottle and Co. present a comedy sketch, "Poor Old George," which shows how a run- away proposition is frustrated. Gene Morelle, the comedy tramp musician, with his stage full of odds and ends, is very laughable; and Victor and Louis, golfer and Caddie, create much fun. Other good turns are :—L. Martinek, in orginal Lady Rag Doll act; the Seven Dandy Lassies, a troupe of clever children; Loie Conn, surprise cornettist; and Phyllis Bray, vocalist.</p>
        <p>The bill for next week includes Harry Russell, the Irishman from York Mare; Allan MacDonald, in' a Scottish sketch; the "Two B'Hoys," Allan Martin and Walter O'Brien; and Flora and Alberta, in a hovel speciality. "Twists and Twirls." Then we are promised Rosa Hamel, who, as a caricaturist, draws. with both hands; Richard Rawson, in dramatic and humorous studies, and Les Van DeUrens, musi- cal acrobats.</p>
        <p>Music hall goers will be pleased to learn that commencing on October 2nd, there will be a full variety show each Monday afternoon at 2.45 o'clock, when the performances will be exactly the same as at the evening entertain- ments.</p>
        <p>The Khtemas. • There is now showing at the Star Kinema House, Church-street, a thrilling drama, "The Flash, of an Emerald," featuring Robert War- wick. For the first half of next week we are to have a Special triangle photo play "Peggy," 4n which Billy Burke is the chief character; and for the latter half a fine drama entitled "Hazel Kirke" and part one of a great serial story of circus life, "Peg o' the Ring."</p>
        <p>At the Grand Electric 'Theatre, Marguerite Snow. is now appearing as Peg in "Peggy of the Cotton ' Mills." During the first half of next</p>
        <p>week "The Blindness of Love," starring Grace Valentine, and the seventeenth episode of "The Diamond from the Sky" will be screened; while for the latter half there will be shown a fine classical and humorous four-act picture entitled "Niobe," featuring Hazel Dawn. ,</p>
        <p>At the Pavilion there is now being screened "Sir," a picture-ised version of the novel by Sir H. Rider Haggard. For the first half of next week a splendid Transatlantic drama, in which Violet Mersereau and William Garwood. take leading parts, will be shown, the title Sf the film being "A Fight for Fortune," and also the seventeenth episode of "The Diamond from the Sky." During the lattnr half of the week tth, " star " picture will be f' I'our Feathers," a soul- stirring photo play, and the eighth adventure of Terence O'Rourke.</p>
        <p>FOYER.</p>
      </div>
