        <p>,</p>
        <p>RAILWAY FARE ALLOWED.</p>
        <p>PYRAMIDS OF FLOWERS.</p>
        <p>Gorgeous Exhibition By Blind And Crippled Girls. i</p>
        <p>•A wonderfully charming scene meets the elO as one . enters the Co-operative Hall, Sanker street, where a floral exhibition and sale of beautiful artificial flowers, made by blind and crippled girls, was started yesterday afternoon, and will continue until the 30th inst. The hall is a blaze of colour. Thousands of myriad coloured flowers grace lattice-work stalls all round the room and in the centre. 111111 platform is a picture, and from it strains o music, vocal and instrumental, are continik ously coming. Tea gardens give it a social 0:e well as horticultural complexion. Seated in various parts of the hall, making more flowers, are poor crippled girls. The opening ceremony took place yesterdaY afternoon, in the presence of a large gatherinf of friends. The affair has the support cm' churches of all denominations. Ladies fool -nearly every church and chapel in the town are giving their services as stall-holders. . Among those with the Mayor (Alderman Peter Peacock) on the platform yesterday I%.ere Mrs. Rylands, Sir Arthur Croeld, Bit, the A. Pc-vs. Canons Willis and Stevnnson, and the Revs. B. Percival, W. T. Derry, T. T. Jan° and H. Ogden. In a short speech, the Mayor gave the exhibi tion a happy send-off, mentioning that 017 crippled girls had made all that wonderfn' array of flowers, and by purchasing their wares they were helping them to be self-,supportink The best help was self-help. Practical Self-Help. Mr. Ernest Trott, who represents the Jelin:, Groom's Crippleage and Flower Girls' Missin' (London), said the institution ranked as one of national importance. Besides accothmodat- ing crippled girls during the fifty years of it° - existence, the institution had been instrurann tal in rescuing from the dangers of street life three thousand girls and finding for them suit: able employment. There was a great deal force in the argument that life was not worth living for those that were afflicted, and the/ were doing all they could to make them hapPY. and useful by making them wage-earners int!: pleasant way. In a year, throughout 0%7" country, as much as two million pounds spent 'in the purchasing .of artificialflovver6i; and they had their "share in that amount. there were any crippled girls in Warrlllgtl who did not know what to do for a living, an they came ':within the rules of the institutioill they would be pleased. to give them a trial alle help them on the way to becoming wage- earners. Mrs. Rylands, who declared the exhibition open, thought they ought to do everything rs support that organisation. Many of the gird were from poor homes and Workhouses, en without that work they had no happy future' Sir Arthur Crosficld And The Sure path T° Happiness. Walking by the aid of a crutch, a criPPle4, girl then presented Mrs. Rylands with</p>
        <p>beautiful bouquet. The pleasure of proposing a vote. of 011n3? to the Mayor and Mrs. RYlands was in the hanns of Sir Arthur Crosfield, Bart. He also took dr opportunity of expressing the hope that the el': hibition would be a great and practical sueees.',: lie thought that one of the greatest factors 119 the work was that it helped to make the gi happier and more self-respecting, because, their occupation, they were able to accoi3ll9r what was both ust-ful and pleasant. And, alwo all, there wkis no surer path to happiness thd• the feeling that one was doing something 11,r,s ful and pleasant. (Hear, hear.) John BrigT'a definition of happiness was that it was pleasurable occupation with a sense of Pr4.l; gress," and let them hope that those poor might, to no small extent, share that feeloll'. (Applause.) The Rev. W. T. Derry seconded the Pr 0173-1.1, tion, remarking that the fact that they Cripples' Guild in Warrington showed tilL/37', they took an interest in afflicted people, an" to was sure it would be an inceir!:ve to them. t help in every way they could. The conaPl,BlT:o they sometimes heard that they ought linldid support charities unless for war purposes jcl not apply in this .case, for besides being all -cbr society, they had a war flavour, because'alrea several children of dead soldiers had been tillft,eo into the orphanage in connection with -0". society. (Applause.) The motion ,motion having been carried en astically, there was a general inspection ca.; flowers and the money began to jingle rnerrill.</p>
      </div>
