        <p>second goal, and a little later Robotham missed scoring from a penalty kick. The visitors continued to have the best of the game, and Brinkswater• con- ceded a corner when he ought to have cleared easily. Robotham, by a piece of good individual Play, again lowered the home goal, and the interval arrived with the score: Brunswick Villa, 3 goals; Sankey Athletic, nil. The home team, with the aid of the wind, were quickly attacking the visitors' citadel, and a smartly hooked shot by Hunt was well got away by Law- rence.• Some even exchanges were seen in midfield, and then the home team led another attack on their opponents' goal. After Lawrence had saved several good shots, E. Taylor scored from a centre by Ashurst. • After this reverse the visitors showed some smart foot work, and caused trouble to Holdetn and Brinkswater to clear their lines. A free kick to the home team just outside the penalty area was well cleared by Lawrence, and then the visitors' for- wards transferred to the opposite end, and Gleave had to save a high shot from Derricott. The visitors were now playing the best game, and good shots by Derricott and Hughes were well saved by Gleave, and then Hughes hit the cross-bar with a fast drive. Taylor, with a fair opening, was a shade too wide, and then Robotham scored the visitors' fourth goal, and just before the close of play Collins added the fifth goal. Result: Brunswick Villa, 5 goals; San- key Athletic, 1 goal. ST. ELPHIN'S v. HICHFIELD JUNIORS. St. Elphin's opened their hyne fixtures on their new ground. Steel-street, with the Highfield Juniors as opponents. The following players turned out under the control of Mr. J. Wright::— St. Elphin's: Dorrian; Tunstall, Furness; Buck- ley, Miller, Watson; Chisholm, England,' A. Jones, Gleave and Holden. Highfield Juniors: Thornhill; Dunn, Jepson; Wishlade, Hankey, Wilding; Highfield, Moulsdale, Bell, Ford and Tomlinson. Both ends were quickly visited, and a very fast pace was set. After five minutes' play Ford gained possession and dribbled finely, beating 'several opponents and finally beat Dorrian with a high shot. The Saints followed with a strong attack, but England's final effort was erratic. Jones, the Saints' centre-forward, supplied Holden with some neat passes, and the latter trade the scores equal after fifteen minutes' play. After some midfield play the same player gained the lead for the Saints. Ford, the visitors' pivot, made many fine attempts to break through, but received poor support from his colleagues. Jones improved a grand centre by Chisholm, Thornhill being welt beaten for a third time. Ford was again prominent for the visitors, and it was only by sterling play that Furness pre- vented him scoring. During a scrimmage in front of the visitors' goal Holden scored again. Half- time: St. Elphin's, 4 goals; Highfield, 1 goal. The second portion opened very spiritedly, and the visitors attacked keenly, but failed to improve their position. England broke away and added a fifth for the Saints. From the centre kick Bell, when getting near the danger zone, was dispos- sessed by Furness, and England transferred with a grand run. and Jones. with a fine shot, added the sixth point. The inside forwards of the Saints were showing a good understanding, and Jones hit the cross-bar with a fast shot. England scoring from the rebound. The visitors played up very keenly in spite of their arrears, and Dorrian made a good save from a hard shot by Ford. Owing to their keenness the,visitors lost several good openings by infringing the off-side rule. Chisholm added to the Saints' total before the final whistle. Result: St. Elphin's; 7 goals; Highfield, 1 goal. For the winners, Furness played a very smart game at full-back, and Miller was the best of the halves. Jones was very smart at Centre-forward, Ad played unselfishly throughout, and Holden and the "hat trick" for the Saints. Thornhill, the 'visitors' goalkeeper, was not very reliable, and ought to have saved some of the shots that beat him. Ford and Bell were the outstanding for- wards. The tit-bit of to-day's fixtures is the meeting of the St. Elphin's and Howley United in Victoria Park. Both teams will be at full strength, and a very keen game is sure to be played- 'LEA IS FOR 'TO-DAY. EMMANUEL V. SANKEY ATELETIC. At Sankey. Emmanuel Team: H. Wright; S. Hughes and J. Hallam; W. Ayres, E., Hardy (capt.), and P. Parr; W. Eaton, H. Stubbs, J. Ford, W. Knight and T,- Hughes.. Players and Members to meet at Rylands-strect at 2.15 p.m. CROSFIELD'S JUNIORS v. GRAPPENHALL JUNIORS. At Crosfield's Recreation Ground. Kick-off 3 o'clock prompt. Crosfield's Juniors: J. Tabernacle; 'H. Beswicir. S. Hardy ;.J. Sudlow, A. Mason, D. Morrison ; Rien., W. Sandy, S. Ravenscroft. D. O'Brien a.nct H. Biz sell. Reserves: W. Lawton and Mills. HOWLEY v. ST. ELPHIN'S. .St. Elphin's: J. Dorrian; W. Tunstall (capt.), V. Furness; A. Buckley, H. Miller, D. Cooper; J. Chisholm, G. England, A. Jones, H. Gleave and J. Holden. Reserves: W. Watson and J. Barton. MATCHES FOR TO-DAY. Howley United v. St. Elphin's; S. C. Essenhigh. Latchford Cadets v. Latchford Recs.; J. Wright. Highfield v. Bewsey A.S.; P. Gosling. Penketh Juniors v. Orford Juniors; R. Williams.</p>
